</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-808a-bac0-c9541a588f07" class=""><mark class="highlight-teal"><strong>Module 2 : Secret and Public Key Cryptography</strong></mark></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80ea-a3fd-e670eb77d629" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Modern Block Ciphers: Block Ciphers Principles</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8056-9d0c-e25b0f29e264" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Shannon’s Theory of Confusion and Diffusion</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80a1-8289-f4bf2f861828" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Festal Structure</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8071-a4c4-cfd79c8f87d0" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Data Encryption Standard (DES)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80aa-b739-cb712601ab41" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Strength of DES</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-801c-9eee-c328eed67ba0" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Idea of Differential Cryptanalysis</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8011-9580-dd194f081d85" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Block Cipher Modes of Operations</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8049-8c05-f05513ecbd96" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Triple DES. </mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-807f-9ae0-c723dd62379e" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) encryption and decryption</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80b4-a5bd-edaa709b08c4" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Fermat’s and Euler’s theorem</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8003-8943-cbeaa8de5b9d" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="11"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Chinese Remainder theorem</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80f6-9e62-e14477d37d4a" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="12"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Principals of Public Key Crypto Systems</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-808f-9622-d79b1be5e7f4" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="13"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">RSA algorithm</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-805f-b082-e8cc2e63e899" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="14"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Security of RSA algorithm.</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-8071-9ea5-d6123866c68b" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="30cf2fef-a309-80cf-966c-fa04a11fe10c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-802d-8b66-cc6d88b975fa" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-8016-9cfa-fc559c211a80" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-80f2-881b-f435e341c2fe" class=""><mark class="highlight-teal"><strong>Module 3 : Authentication Standards &amp; Key Management</strong></mark></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80f5-9329-efffbbc9312f" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Message Authentication Codes:</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80c0-9a39-c93284722011" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Authentication requirements</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8091-86bb-d31e7e88f8ec" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">authentication functions</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80ea-8e2c-d9ab9ebf03ed" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> message authentication code</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8010-8f08-c0ef62cd6fd3" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Hash Functions</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-801d-be85-ff95765bb385" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80bf-ac76-c0cdc48da8a7" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Message Digest</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80ec-bbac-d7b995ab81f7" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Digital Signatures: Digital Signatures</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80a7-a151-d5120252f082" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Digital Signature Standards (DSS)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8064-8e62-c5217dd8e756" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> proof of Digital Signature Algorithm</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-80d1-ba06-cd6908af34ec" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="30cf2fef-a309-8097-8e9f-d7364e30d01d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-8062-87fe-d8abb621bca9" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-80bc-ab45-e9b76d8b3252" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-801f-b141-f92f5e77a98a" class=""><mark class="highlight-teal"><strong>Module 4: Web Security</strong></mark></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80ea-8f2c-dd5d6fea7409" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Authentication Applications: Kerberos</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-807b-a5ff-f7fb04f06b23" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Overview of Access Control List (ACL)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80ee-830d-da9ec848576d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Electronic Mail Security: Pretty Good Privacy (PGP)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8064-b899-ddffd24a233c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> S/MIME.</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-804f-a791-e04ea1672e06" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">IP Security: Architecture</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8000-ac29-f20fb1604753" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Authentication Header</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-806b-ac0a-f3e5df8be786" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Encapsulating Security Payloads</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8005-befd-c270290a510d" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Key Management</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80a7-9982-ffc5fe6ac1e1" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Introduction to Secure Socket Layer</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8017-be18-f3e53616979f" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Secure electronic, transaction (SET)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-801a-9a64-f3642a0186a0" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="30cf2fef-a309-802a-8b0f-fbc247891d9a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-80ca-80b6-d0903348d41d" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-8028-9ca8-e66a9c3a7f41" class=""><strong>Module 5 : Computational Learning Theory</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="30cf2fef-a309-8076-83c2-f14f2587decf" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-808e-a271-fa4ac6621787" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>System Security: Introductory Idea of Intrusion</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-809f-99a5-f594707f41f6" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li> Intrusion Detection Techniques</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8030-919c-d85454b35020" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li> Viruses and Worms</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80d1-b44e-db4e48f388b1" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Firewalls</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80e5-b42b-cd5481bc7aff" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li> Design Principles of Firewalls</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-809d-81e9-db1150a2657a" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li> IT Acts and Cyber Laws (Global Standards)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80d4-a83a-eca3c4cc4ae4" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li>Virtual Private Network</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80f6-b937-c180e5d31d6d" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li> Recent attacks on networks (Packet Analyzer, Buffer Overflow and Port Scanning)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80fc-8b64-de7ebd3a5b80" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li>Blockchain Technology</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-807c-aaa0-ddcfae9247cc" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li> Preventing Privacy and Piracy on Internet</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8052-ac9c-ffe3e0518621" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="11"><li>Brief Overview of Cyber Forensic</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80d1-b734-d98fc8c36f94" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="12"><li>Cyber Forensic Processes</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8084-a471-e9ac28e42ab2" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="13"><li> Cyber Forensic Applications and Cyber Forensic Tools</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-8074-a5ba-f8cfa2ab487e" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="14"><li>Digital Evidence</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-80d1-9db7-c4f1166d8936" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="15"><li>Investigating Windows and Unix Systems</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="30cf2fef-a309-804b-ab29-d79f96dd450f" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="16"><li>Email Tracing.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80cf-b25f-fcfd790fe19e" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800a-9914-d1aabc1c24ad" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8080-af8f-d4c4229b6dc4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b4-bcf8-cbff2852e4c7" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ae-acc9-f3e36e9bfaa2" class=""><strong>Module 1: Introduction to Network Security</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80be-940d-cedfb9819c68" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Introduction to Security Attacks</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f2-827c-c72c97e086cf" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Services and Mechanism</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f5-b9b5-ff6116e9e51e" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Classical encryption techniques</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8092-9fa6-ca3fb6f1d938" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li> Substitution Ciphers and Transposition ciphers</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80b0-b603-fce3d7cf996e" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Cryptanalysis</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-b67e-d137ac8c80a4" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Steganography</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8059-8f42-d7315a053128" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li>Stream and Block ciphers</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80db-81a6-e0782644e404" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li> A Brief Overview of Hardware Security</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-937f-da9faeed2dd2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-b5d9-cba1754a90fc" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Introduction to Security Attacks : </mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80c2-a016-d2f0d78ce500"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8013-953c-e400e90a48a6" class="">Security attacks pose a serious threat to computer systems and networks. They can compromise the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information. Understanding different types of attacks such as passive and active attacks helps organizations implement appropriate security measures. By using encryption, authentication, and monitoring tools, systems can be protected from potential threats and ensure secure communication.<br/><br/></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808c-8d65-c2b0768d9da8" class="">In today’s digital world, computer systems and networks store and transmit large amounts of sensitive information. Protecting this information from unauthorized access, misuse, or damage is known as <strong>information security</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8060-93f9-d076925d8745" class="">A <strong>security attack</strong> refers to any action that attempts to compromise the <strong>confidentiality, integrity, or availability</strong> of information or system resources. Attackers may try to steal data, modify it, or disrupt system operations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8040-a734-f079e3b5230a" class="">Security attacks are a major concern for organizations, governments, and individuals because they can lead to financial loss, privacy violations, and damage to reputation.<br/><br/></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80ae-997a-d4251c4fee78" class="">Objectives of Security Attacks</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806e-9837-cffbefa3e80d" class="">The main goal of attackers is to violate the <strong>CIA triad</strong> of information security:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-802a-b903-c76a0a164bba" class="">1. Confidentiality</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ed-a089-eda84f25912f" class="">Confidentiality ensures that information is accessible only to authorized users.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805d-becb-ef531e6166da" class="">Attackers try to access sensitive data such as passwords, financial records, or personal information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806b-8158-cee91ba67f2c" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-beba-d938c68ac4b5" class="">A hacker intercepting login credentials during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8024-9f08-f04df82bd90a" class="">2. Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8073-8da6-d9d181d691dc" class="">Integrity ensures that data remains accurate and unchanged during storage or transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-804b-8983-fa05fa46c784" class="">Attackers may alter or manipulate data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801a-a3fd-c34a4d50efe5" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8006-9a79-ef266d06c1f5" class="">Changing the amount of money in a bank transaction.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8069-852c-dc5f3485042d" class="">3. Availability</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8036-9b3c-d77b7a3613ac" class="">Availability ensures that systems and information are accessible to authorized users when needed.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800a-9709-dab474a35c18" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fb-8de2-e7a2a2a9a8c3" class="">A Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack that crashes a website.<br/><br/></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8030-ae15-cbb70d6e0682" class="">Classification of Security Attacks</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e8-8dfd-f75165db55e4" class="">Security attacks are broadly classified into two main categories:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-806c-be50-fa29f901862c" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Passive Attacks</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80d7-a0ca-f4769dc150c5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Active Attacks<br/><br/></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809b-8a90-f6b10ab25723" class="">4. Passive Attacks</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8096-9417-d32c33e5824a" class="">Passive attacks involve <strong>monitoring or listening to communication without altering the data</strong>. The attacker only gathers information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8053-9c3d-c4369f758f85" class="">These attacks are difficult to detect because no data is modified.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-800d-a553-e0261dbfdbfd" class="">Types of Passive Attacks</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80d1-a8a7-ecde6938ced7" class="">1. Release of Message Contents</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8067-98ec-eae56407aaa1" class="">In this attack, an unauthorized person reads confidential data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8031-bfb3-e41202be5bb9" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809a-80d3-f2defa0d3457" class="">Intercepting email messages or confidential files during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ad-ba3e-d2bf325282bb" class="">2. Traffic Analysis</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a7-b499-f98a2f572a16" class="">Even if messages are encrypted, attackers analyze communication patterns to gather information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8051-aa7d-e0b2c4ba06ea" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d9-ad5b-f71ccdd4829b" class="">Observing which employees communicate frequently in a company to understand organizational structure.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8061-9062-e67a1d38b4ae" class=""><strong>Characteristics of Passive Attacks</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8005-aec4-e749811a7b8d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Difficult to detect</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80dd-845d-f946df284fb5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Do not affect system resources directly</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-801a-892c-f8ca6db40b2e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Mainly target confidentiality</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a0-aeb6-c82b3d7fa34d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80ba-b399-d403bd2eed88" class="">5. Active Attacks</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8007-a2df-f9b4dc612149" class="">Active attacks involve <strong>modifying, deleting, or disrupting data or system operations</strong>. These attacks directly affect system performance.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-802f-8b94-ccdffce0e8d4" class="">Types of Active Attacks</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-805a-af12-d38f34dba0d0" class="">1. Masquerade Attack</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807b-a102-c989197b094b" class="">In this attack, an attacker pretends to be a legitimate user to gain unauthorized access.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a1-9fa6-ce2336a8d176" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8011-95ba-c9e3bc0a1884" class="">Using stolen login credentials to access a company’s database.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80d1-87a3-c4a4c7ae3292" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>security service</strong></mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8090-bcb1-dd77a031643d"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-805e-8947-eafc519b9d67" class="">Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8023-a0b4-f5799837b3d5" class="">A <strong>security service</strong> is a service provided by a system or network that ensures adequate security for data transmission and protects information against attacks.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8047-89a1-e24bc770d002" class="">Security services ensure that communication between users remains <strong>secure, reliable, and trustworthy</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8019-a6e8-d81af2ee64f3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8011-a915-e0858a67d052" class="">Types of Security Services</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8082-b381-d19840fd8ece" class="">1. Authentication</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805d-bba7-c391d6072f47" class="">Authentication ensures that the identity of the communicating parties is verified.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8036-af08-e43898690203" class="">It confirms that the sender and receiver are genuine and not imposters.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-807d-a576-f0ee7d86cb64" class="">Types</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-803b-8bc2-c0e074ce1318" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Peer Entity Authentication</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8063-bd10-f6c4e2ab0075" class="">Confirms the identity of a communicating entity during connection establishment.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8007-9c67-c6432835de44" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Data Origin Authentication</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ad-a4b2-e1fc1f414513" class="">Ensures that the message comes from the claimed source.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8025-ba15-fc443e458573" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8033-91a7-dba269721574" class="">Login authentication using username and password.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8042-8972-f6cd0f65d58e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8030-b2ea-d985f658bee6" class="">2. Access Control</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-b5f6-ecf42c627816" class="">Access control prevents <strong>unauthorized users from accessing resources</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ff-af17-c7bafbcea7fc" class="">It ensures that only authorized individuals can use specific systems, files, or services.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807f-9e44-e7171bdbe20d" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8090-b877-e795138a716f" class="">File permissions in an operating system.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8075-9cd3-d981e0ec39c4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8017-ba22-e3df07c17fe5" class="">3. Data Confidentiality</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80da-943f-e553e4373b2c" class="">Confidentiality ensures that information is <strong>accessible only to authorized users</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8090-931c-fb84cbb227f8" class="">Sensitive data is protected from unauthorized disclosure.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8075-9f1e-c99a788b0295" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-98a7-e75ea382eb60" class="">Encryption used in secure websites.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8068-af98-eb3b30a20f42"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8005-a2cc-f53a507d076e" class="">4. Data Integrity</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a5-a043-cf6b14967671" class="">Integrity ensures that data <strong>is not altered during transmission or storage</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e3-870d-edf4919a3a07" class="">It guarantees that information remains accurate and unchanged.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e5-96b2-f328c41baddf" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80dd-b2b2-f8260973bd23" class="">Digital signatures verifying that a document has not been modified.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-803e-a686-e6fe6eea9d8b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-a14c-c77812f6b832" class="">5. Non-Repudiation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8035-b47f-f1821b45622d" class="">Non-repudiation prevents a sender or receiver from <strong>denying their participation in communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f0-b587-c90b3d08c619" class="">It provides proof of the origin and delivery of data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-98f1-d087fa3f93c9" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803e-a604-d41436a2ff07" class="">Digital signatures in online transactions.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-ba3a-d16e3abd59af"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809b-b1f4-db6d0f9c41dd" class="">3. Security Mechanisms</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-9374-e037d29a70ac" class="">Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8059-a904-ca19323a29c6" class="">Security mechanisms are <strong>techniques or methods used to implement security services</strong> and protect systems from attacks.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d8-8008-f8b6922cde8e" class="">They help detect, prevent, and recover from security threats.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-89e7-f9dc2d5f2116"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a3-a513-fb5f25ab478e" class="">Types of Security Mechanisms</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807d-bfe7-c0b83e552484" class="">Security mechanisms are classified into two categories:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80ae-9af3-e11875d86e98" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Specific Security Mechanisms</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-806f-93b0-c8cdd8bc7be4" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Pervasive Security Mechanisms</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8012-b45f-cbd020601619"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-803d-9dcb-e633eebc0477" class="">4. Specific Security Mechanisms</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807c-8b66-cc817d1c0141" class="">These mechanisms are designed to provide particular security services.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8013-9e48-f16be71d1bac" class="">1. Encipherment</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-9a9c-c509bcfe92d0" class="">Encipherment refers to <strong>encryption of data</strong> to protect confidentiality.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8037-8a1c-f903b7fe41c0" class="">Plain text is converted into cipher text using encryption algorithms.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803a-a01a-fe62371d56ae" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8090-83aa-d50a3ba679e0" class="">Secure communication in online banking.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-8ac9-df19b57cb818"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80ad-a3e8-e2364345d334" class="">2. Digital Signature</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807e-907a-e65a38d35ba2" class="">A digital signature is used to verify the <strong>authenticity and integrity of messages</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8005-b613-f8e85d440346" class="">It ensures that the message has not been modified.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8038-96d1-da73675b0746" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8001-b9c1-df6f253336d6" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Classical Encryption Techniques</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8002-8273-f28d5934b24f"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8032-864d-cedcad7862ac" class="">Classical Encryption Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80dd-8124-ff42896a948d" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fa-8292-d9bfc258f0d1" class="">Cryptography is the science of protecting information by converting it into a secure form so that unauthorized users cannot understand it. Encryption techniques transform readable data (<strong>plaintext</strong>) into an unreadable form (<strong>ciphertext</strong>) using an algorithm and a key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-9fb2-f23cc772362e" class=""><strong>Classical encryption techniques</strong> are the traditional methods used in early cryptography before the development of modern encryption algorithms. These techniques mainly rely on <strong>substitution and transposition</strong> methods to secure information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-983d-d33ebe0cd22f" class="">Although classical techniques are not secure against modern attacks, they form the <strong>foundation of modern cryptography</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-804f-b451-ca29cb35d485"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809c-a093-d5661ea2cbb5" class="">2. Types of Classical Encryption Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802c-b28a-f8f2ef291162" class="">Classical encryption techniques are broadly classified into two main categories:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8049-af79-fbf508614087" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Classical Encryption Techniques
            |
     -------------------
     |                 |
Substitution      Transposition
Techniques        Techniques</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a9-85c2-d0ec1d83dd3d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8044-b233-e48a246ee562" class="">3. Substitution Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8001-861e-f61d04e7989d" class="">In substitution techniques, each element in the plaintext (such as letters or groups of letters) is <strong>replaced by another letter or symbol</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-a30b-f34606b3aff2" class="">Characteristics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-809b-87dd-d363313d010c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Characters are substituted with other characters.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-808d-ae64-e941f3ab83e7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">The position of characters remains unchanged.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80a5-8de7-ea211a0f581b" class="">Types of Substitution Techniques</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8095-8823-ecbe528bebae"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-803f-81ed-ca8a63c8fc62" class="">1. Caesar Cipher</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8024-9676-eb7e7c237c43" class="">The <strong>Julius Caesar</strong> cipher is one of the earliest and simplest encryption techniques.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8025-9824-df554c7a5843" class="">In this method, each letter in the plaintext is <strong>shifted by a fixed number of positions</strong> in the alphabet.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8082-acb5-ca274e98a45f" class="">Example (Shift = 3):</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-80be-ad62-c0b774c7e5eb" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80c7-b8cf-c7816eae714d"><th id="BJW?" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Plaintext</th><th id="KikA" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Ciphertext</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8086-b0d6-d3ba4a8cd6a5"><td id="BJW?" class="">A</td><td id="KikA" class="">D</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-8055-c651e31e6a54"><td id="BJW?" class="">B</td><td id="KikA" class="">E</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8048-924b-fa574cb5705d"><td id="BJW?" class="">C</td><td id="KikA" class="">F</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a5-bc69-f2d19e92201f" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-81b3-d54fd97b0d5a" class="">Plaintext:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8071-8ea6-ea937f1f2356" class="">HELLO</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8088-acad-f8dd0baeac08" class="">Ciphertext:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8059-9454-cfba2dbd0afb" class="">KHOOR</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8023-8da0-d80405982fdd" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-809e-a8c4-f80beec4a8a9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simple to implement.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-8bac-f0bef7ed7a1a" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8015-ba5d-f6f36bc68e69" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Easy to break using brute force.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8078-ba0f-dc1c260e129d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-808b-9ced-fbb8b1db6941" class="">2. Monoalphabetic Cipher</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-9788-f026485d2b5f" class="">In this technique, <strong>each letter of the plaintext is mapped to a unique letter in the ciphertext</strong> using a substitution table.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8038-8caf-d871c03dad67" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-802e-ad63-fb97f33cf3a9" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-805c-930a-db3246cfcc7d"><th id="EPV:" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Plaintext</th><th id="@gJj" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Ciphertext</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-809d-9e73-f6a7c9aece3d"><td id="EPV:" class="">A</td><td id="@gJj" class="">Q</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80c4-8a49-f2384179fd8c"><td id="EPV:" class="">B</td><td id="@gJj" class="">W</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80df-afc5-edc3f09c91d3"><td id="EPV:" class="">C</td><td id="@gJj" class="">E</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8029-97e5-c27f83d68c9a" class="">Characteristics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8027-9748-c0d326b8636d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Uses a fixed substitution for the entire message.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805c-8bea-cf3593f30c0e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">More secure than Caesar cipher but still vulnerable to <strong>frequency analysis</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8040-820a-dedfe228c73d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-805d-a420-efaad4cf9198" class="">3. Playfair Cipher</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f8-bea8-de6c9f085590" class="">The <strong>Charles Wheatstone</strong> Playfair cipher encrypts <strong>pairs of letters (digraphs)</strong> instead of single letters.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80c2-98c9-e6f0e8f05276" class="">Steps</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-8022-efc6fdc491a4" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Create a <strong>5×5 matrix of letters</strong> using a keyword.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-807c-8449-fde7fb21cbdb" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Split plaintext into pairs.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80de-894b-c0c1298a1efe" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Apply encryption rules based on row and column positions.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-8658-c3aef7b8ddfa" class="">Example keyword: MONARCHY</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8095-b269-f33827ea481d" class="">Matrix:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-8066-a8c4-e4f9624faa8e" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8066-80e5-dc0d0607024c"><th id="&gt;yN[" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">M</th><th id="`TYe" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">O</th><th id="S~ye" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">N</th><th id="[VhM" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">A</th><th id="TkKH" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">R</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80d3-b0f4-c1fc845de50f"><td id="&gt;yN[" class="">C</td><td id="`TYe" class="">H</td><td id="S~ye" class="">Y</td><td id="[VhM" class="">B</td><td id="TkKH" class="">D</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-806e-89a5-f8673c8ded84"><td id="&gt;yN[" class="">E</td><td id="`TYe" class="">F</td><td id="S~ye" class="">G</td><td id="[VhM" class="">I</td><td id="TkKH" class="">K</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8083-982a-c6251485c3ac"><td id="&gt;yN[" class="">L</td><td id="`TYe" class="">P</td><td id="S~ye" class="">Q</td><td id="[VhM" class="">S</td><td id="TkKH" class="">T</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8099-b64a-ed97563d1595"><td id="&gt;yN[" class="">U</td><td id="`TYe" class="">V</td><td id="S~ye" class="">W</td><td id="[VhM" class="">X</td><td id="TkKH" class="">Z</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8017-b439-dde3bceaeb31" class="">Advantage</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-804b-86e7-f6201d239d99" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">More secure than simple substitution.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8077-900f-f069e1500900" class="">Disadvantage</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8035-8c32-f9d1ff148724" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Still vulnerable to cryptanalysis.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d0-a3eb-c834714ae455"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8048-8a6f-f349758cb7aa" class="">4. Hill Cipher</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b7-8f27-c8c1bcca7bbe" class="">The Hill cipher uses <strong>matrix operations and linear algebra</strong> to encrypt blocks of letters.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-8728-d35ff5540612" class="">It was introduced by <strong>Lester S. Hill</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-808b-a844-f0cb33f59b9d" class="">Features</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80db-ad6d-f917cd147204" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Uses mathematical matrices.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8037-9d5c-dc414587a606" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encrypts multiple letters at a time.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807e-bd46-f15cc0e90991" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Stronger than earlier substitution ciphers.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-a57b-e539339be9e9" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809d-9a32-e8afd012dae1" class="">Plaintext letters are converted to numbers and multiplied by a <strong>key matrix</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8031-a39b-f588368a6e6e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8064-b037-d9d7ddb51275" class="">4. Transposition Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803d-9ff2-ddda604f347b" class="">In transposition techniques, the <strong>positions of characters are changed</strong>, but the characters themselves remain the same.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8067-89c0-ec8c459a3fa5" class="">Characteristics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ee-82da-daea963cb24a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">No letters are replaced.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-8346-dfea6c7ffb96" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Only the order of characters is rearranged.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-802b-98f4-d041c70f2cae"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8052-b735-fad8d0b84c0c" class="">1. Rail Fence Cipher</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8076-af99-f785ba1d1bc9" class="">In this method, plaintext is written in a <strong>zigzag pattern</strong> across multiple rows and then read row by row.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-9800-c453beba9cdb" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8017-99a3-d77c53ef1de3" class="">Plaintext:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803d-86dc-f5d17ed9f4b6" class="">HELLO WORLD</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8045-b6aa-e6519da4e6af" class="">Writing in zigzag (2 rails):</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-ad5b-e875ac0101cb" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">H   L   O   O   L
 E   L   W   R   D</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807a-b440-c0a19631cfde" class="">Ciphertext:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8073-960b-c3c704260228" class="">HLOOLELWRD</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80a7-b534-f96bd53c57f5" class="">Advantage</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8001-8367-e5ec484ccb69" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simple and easy to implement.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-857f-fce6990bae18" class="">Disadvantage</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8029-b869-eb47c01ab46c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Easy to break.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8022-85a9-cadd42efe3d0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80ac-8495-de09c9f2ff2d" class="">2. Columnar Transposition Cipher</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8080-a559-cb74bd7d55d6" class="">In this technique, plaintext is written in rows under a keyword and then read column by column according to the key order.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f5-9b68-d1c26371ce3d" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809d-9db7-cc2c7d8c83e5" class="">Keyword: ZEBRA</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8015-9397-c577d4e82ef0" class="">Plaintext is arranged in columns and then rearranged based on the alphabetical order of the keyword.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-804a-9ad6-ec9da85cbb37" class="">Advantage</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-803b-8509-f871cce3f3d6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">More secure than simple transposition.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8010-8cd9-dcf0f299a343"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-806a-8ed8-ea1462bce5ec" class="">5. Difference Between Substitution and Transposition</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-9691-ff0a9d6377fe" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-8c71-c0f2464c8147"><th id="}_a~" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Feature</th><th id="d&lt;xl" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Substitution</th><th id="MUEJ" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Transposition</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80fc-9b89-ea6938c9b9da"><td id="}_a~" class="">Method</td><td id="d&lt;xl" class="">Replaces characters</td><td id="MUEJ" class="">Rearranges characters</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8040-96b2-d034220f8878"><td id="}_a~" class="">Letter positions</td><td id="d&lt;xl" class="">Remain same</td><td id="MUEJ" class="">Change</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-809f-a5d5-f2d861c58bc7"><td id="}_a~" class="">Example</td><td id="d&lt;xl" class="">Caesar cipher</td><td id="MUEJ" class="">Rail fence cipher</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-803c-9123-e19dfb242a0f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8011-bde5-fce310196eb0" class="">6. Advantages of Classical Encryption</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8005-998b-f143ea677c9c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simple to understand and implement</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8020-bf6d-c46acf068185" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Foundation for modern cryptography</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8048-b07c-d7afbf1f38d2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Useful for educational purposes</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8015-82f8-d49d5ba61056"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a5-bf61-f3f719addab3" class="">7. Limitations</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-aa5f-def7ba5eded9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Easily broken using modern cryptanalysis techniques</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805f-8e98-ccd8818c0984" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide limited security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8087-a090-f8a28f8156b8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Not suitable for modern communication systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8004-9845-f235919c58f4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8070-b4bd-d0193a85f5d8" class="">8. Conclusion</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80da-abc8-f64af6e0c29e" class="">Classical encryption techniques played a significant role in the development of cryptography. Techniques such as substitution and transposition were widely used in the past to secure messages. Although these methods are no longer secure for modern applications, they provide the basic concepts that led to the development of advanced cryptographic algorithms used today.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8050-83c2-f1f7b849219f" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Cryptanalysis</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-802c-a88d-d2daf37538ff"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-808b-b776-ca7bf2fd530d" class="">Cryptanalysis</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-800a-972e-ede88395a8fd" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8097-b709-d2454b79ff00" class="">Cryptography is used to protect information by converting readable data (<strong>plaintext</strong>) into an unreadable form (<strong>ciphertext</strong>). However, attackers may attempt to break this protection to reveal the original information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8056-9f23-c33cf4e1f858" class=""><strong>Cryptanalysis</strong> is the process of analyzing and breaking encryption systems in order to recover the plaintext or secret key without knowing the key used for encryption.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808b-8ab4-e9acfffc9d10" class="">The person who performs cryptanalysis is called a <strong>cryptanalyst</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806a-8407-f94462323b25" class="">Cryptanalysis plays an important role in <strong>testing the strength of cryptographic algorithms</strong> and improving security systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8036-8033-d08d8f444bae"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80de-9fc6-ebf2e5707352" class="">2. Objectives of Cryptanalysis</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a2-abea-f3d397cd6c5e" class="">The main objectives of cryptanalysis are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8079-be9b-fa6eb9f7dd1b" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Recover the plaintext</strong> from the ciphertext without knowing the key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8048-aba2-ccb4418dc9ae" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Discover the secret key</strong> used in the encryption process.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-801c-9cab-f2d61fb4d372" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Identify weaknesses</strong> in encryption algorithms.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-aecb-e67f35386092" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Evaluate the security strength</strong> of cryptographic systems.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8047-9866-f786cd115f0c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8029-b2ce-d6454cc02331" class="">3. Types of Cryptanalytic Attacks</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e5-a98f-debd7cb1ce93" class="">Cryptanalytic attacks are classified based on the <strong>amount of information available to the attacker</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8001-87b1-f8c449127d4e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80f3-abd2-ef2607cd910e" class="">1. Ciphertext-Only Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8015-a9f6-f1b82c927e85" class="">In this attack, the attacker only has access to the <strong>ciphertext</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808b-a2ec-f86e347812b0" class="">The attacker tries to deduce the plaintext or encryption key by analyzing patterns in the ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8062-9273-f216e572a574" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8045-b8f8-c8c43af06b2b" class="">Analyzing letter frequency in encrypted messages.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ed-a006-c4e37723eca0" class=""><strong>Difficulty Level:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d3-845e-df4d41888cad" class="">Very difficult because limited information is available.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-803a-ab09-efdb60ca74bd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8071-88bd-f462f41e0cd9" class="">2. Known-Plaintext Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802d-8990-d4d60f5ea80a" class="">In this attack, the attacker knows <strong>some pairs of plaintext and corresponding ciphertext</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80de-9615-dc23727cefef" class="">Using this information, the attacker attempts to determine the encryption key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803f-ae10-f484aa9eb191" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800d-bfc6-ff43f84956ff" class="">If an attacker knows that the encrypted message corresponds to a specific known document.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8006-8707-c98e97226d37"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8096-b15f-fbf4d2a00c0a" class="">3. Chosen-Plaintext Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8055-9445-f69ca57ba5b7" class="">In this attack, the attacker can <strong>choose arbitrary plaintext messages and obtain their ciphertexts</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e0-a49a-e6f417417507" class="">By analyzing how the encryption system transforms plaintext into ciphertext, the attacker tries to determine the key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8064-8279-eafc76af019b" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-9919-cb9dbeb98f96" class="">Testing how different inputs affect the encrypted output.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-b31d-d911ad953950"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8026-bf35-e0d6a031213a" class="">4. Chosen-Ciphertext Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8054-b8a3-d9e17eed2d54" class="">In this attack, the attacker chooses a ciphertext and obtains the <strong>corresponding decrypted plaintext</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8089-b69e-f8f0287385a6" class="">This helps the attacker analyze the decryption process and determine the secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-8ecf-e2dbeb2b1ec5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-806e-a846-df31320b4e56" class="">4. Common Cryptanalysis Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8093-9b80-eb108dc2cb23" class="">1. Frequency Analysis</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805b-b229-c299727b5dd0" class="">This technique studies the <strong>frequency of letters or symbols</strong> in the ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8034-81cf-c3601ec20db9" class="">In many languages, some letters occur more frequently than others.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807a-b829-ea66c26d31ac" class="">Example in English:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-a5bd-dce43c93cae2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">E is the most common letter</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807d-878d-f1e588e27777" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">T, A, O are also frequent</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8027-a95f-ce8ac5f88141" class="">By analyzing these frequencies, attackers can guess the plaintext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80c4-a42e-db9348db3073"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80a5-a3d7-dcd6bee9a7d9" class="">2. Brute Force Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b4-b968-f9a3701330f4" class="">In a brute force attack, the attacker <strong>tries every possible key</strong> until the correct one is found.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8061-8a58-c8be5db4a9b8" class="">Although simple, it can be time-consuming depending on the key size.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-808d-98ab-cacb205c1b39"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80b2-b621-e1d1486e9905" class="">3. Pattern Analysis</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8000-b8da-c0ec99263dc5" class="">Cryptanalysts look for <strong>patterns, repeated sequences, and structures</strong> in ciphertext to infer possible plaintext messages.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-801c-8bd6-fe39b5d8e72a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8017-ad23-cc0d9b30c00a" class="">5. Importance of Cryptanalysis</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807e-a6fe-eb55017e7b04" class="">Cryptanalysis is important because:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8040-8858-fc4a736fd11f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">It helps <strong>identify weaknesses in cryptographic algorithms</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-806f-a632-c501c739ab99" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">It improves the design of <strong>more secure encryption systems</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8006-aebb-e4dbd74f233d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">It ensures the <strong>reliability of secure communication systems</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-a6f5-f0337ee5e57e" class="">Many modern encryption algorithms are tested using cryptanalysis before being adopted.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8097-af42-ced220cbfa62" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Steganography</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80d0-8bfa-d45db4ec2e48"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-805c-be30-ff44daefd0c1" class="">Steganography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-9127-fb53cdf46723" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-83dd-dfb9ff7d949b" class="">Steganography is the technique of <strong>hiding secret information inside another ordinary medium</strong> so that the presence of the hidden message is not noticeable.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8095-bb7d-ff04ba749911" class="">The word <em>Steganography</em> comes from Greek words:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-800c-b7e0-ea8a7dd934b3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Stegano</strong> – meaning <em>covered or hidden</em></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-9dc3-ddaa2529cce0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Graphy</strong> – meaning <em>writing</em></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8079-8e34-cc2191804811" class="">Unlike encryption, which only converts a message into an unreadable format, steganography <strong>conceals the existence of the message itself</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8084-8cbd-f1375d49d75c" class="">For example, a secret message may be hidden inside an <strong>image, audio file, video file, or text document</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8043-a273-d7ce95efa00c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-805a-b611-cd9e3319e9b5" class="">2. Basic Concept of Steganography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8034-9224-cb45b7ba85a7" class="">In steganography, a secret message is hidden inside a <strong>cover medium</strong> using a special algorithm and sometimes a secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ea-b086-f9ef58fe9304" class="">Components of Steganography</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-801e-bf23-c598ffe831a1" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Cover Object</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8060-bd32-cb225f73e096" class="">The original file used to hide the message.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8011-b9c9-f56665812d46" class="">Example: image, audio, or video.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-803a-a779-d87c8a97056c" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Secret Message</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8030-9ff4-f0a6b63c3ab0" class="">The confidential information that needs to be hidden.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-807c-b077-f12488407f32" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Stego Object</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-8777-c00897b0374c" class="">The final output that contains the hidden message.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-807d-8423-dfc9d6edc11a" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Stego Key (Optional)</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d5-a989-c466c7a1ea5d" class="">A secret key used to hide and retrieve the message.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8009-b2ae-f9efabae2339"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8068-b559-c7ad07b29e80" class="">Basic Steganography Process</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8049-b4e4-c6f537e042ad" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Secret Message + Cover Object + Key
              |
        Steganography Algorithm
              |
         Stego Object (Hidden Message)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8046-b406-d087d30416b0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8009-8234-ec7a5467e118" class="">3. Types of Steganography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d9-937c-dfef536460fa" class="">Steganography techniques can be classified based on the type of medium used.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-9fce-fb909c35e25d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80db-b6db-f306f57afe6a" class="">1. Image Steganography</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8038-83c6-ea0e133c32eb" class="">In this method, secret information is hidden inside an <strong>image file</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800c-b84f-edc61c3478c4" class="">Small changes are made to pixel values that are usually not visible to the human eye.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808c-9677-e29f282ac442" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8062-910a-daf6e48bb224" class="">Hiding text inside a PNG or JPEG image.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8028-a9d4-e2d5a3d4bab9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8095-8495-fae55dacbe56" class="">2. Audio Steganography</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809e-87a7-d42d8e8b3164" class="">In this technique, secret data is embedded into <strong>audio signals</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b4-bc10-d3d0698fc4b7" class="">The hidden data is inserted into the sound file without significantly affecting the audio quality.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8012-80b0-d92eb6371ed6" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a7-bdfa-fbeeed35c6ce" class="">Embedding messages in MP3 files.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80de-94d0-c553e1086f3d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80f8-8d6a-c3c2e99d9416" class="">3. Video Steganography</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8074-b34c-d9ef92188bec" class="">Video steganography hides information inside <strong>video files</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-9e1c-d0c371826855" class="">Since videos consist of many frames, they provide a large capacity for hiding data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8048-b53c-d5772b97e7fc"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8043-a964-c0903861c293" class="">4. Text Steganography</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806e-bb43-c7cb056d6ab3" class="">In this technique, secret messages are hidden within <strong>text documents</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c2-9fc8-e016af69d26f" class="">Methods include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-9674-ddda6b6ad225" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Changing spacing</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-9202-d3f2fa4f25b4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Using special characters</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8004-b261-d654d59bb331" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Modifying text formatting</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8071-a25d-f664e544f722"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8073-9b5e-ec1126a26101" class="">4. Steganography Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-800e-bf9b-ed5f2846f7d4" class="">1. Least Significant Bit (LSB) Technique</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-a2ba-fd0c63402f0d" class="">This is the most common method used in image steganography.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ac-a94b-c87fd8d47a30" class="">The <strong>least significant bit of pixel values</strong> is modified to store hidden data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806d-b676-c9903a47a01f" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8062-941a-c67eb62dfa0f" class="">Original Pixel Value:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d8-8041-c3f5c3457832" class="">10110010</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-8b43-e134fc4f3b71" class="">Modified Pixel Value:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-b444-ff272cbc7968" class="">10110011</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c1-8c5b-fa3c05a6c630" class="">The change is very small and usually not visible.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-809a-9013-e881df912314"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-b728-faeeb7522ee4" class="">2. Masking and Filtering</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807c-a584-e1553ade3686" class="">This technique hides information by <strong>modifying the brightness or color intensity</strong> of images.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80cb-9316-c59f90f66917" class="">It is often used in image watermarking.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8007-a50f-e21222b8d250"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-808a-a729-d304cbf7170d" class="">3. Spread Spectrum Technique</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8069-bc2a-c864da7fedbe" class="">In this method, secret information is spread across the <strong>entire frequency spectrum</strong> of the cover medium.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806d-8457-d08b5faffb0c" class="">This technique is more secure and difficult to detect.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-961f-c4e0cb1393ba"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8071-8d16-c400b59bd831" class="">5. Applications of Steganography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806f-9854-f18cece998b0" class="">Steganography is used in many fields, such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8067-8df0-ca81297eb408" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Secure communication</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-9e35-f84bfb7515b4" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Digital watermarking</strong> for copyright protection</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80fc-a0f9-f41e09bb5175" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Military communication</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8007-a546-ce93eba68624" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Data authentication</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f8-889e-cdfab0a4ab18" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Covert communication systems</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8071-aa90-d8edf6ae3bbd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8060-a445-ca12470c3673" class="">6. Advantages of Steganography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-802b-b786-d01164757099" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hides the existence of secret information</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8055-8bd9-cae28d435d0c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Difficult to detect by attackers</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8096-801e-d90ad4b44031" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides additional security when combined with encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-802a-8b6d-d31a1e9ac90b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-802a-9495-ec3beba49ff8" class="">7. Limitations</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8039-a1dd-cbaca5bc9114" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hidden data capacity is limited</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8083-9966-e2a9e9d5a3e1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If detected, the hidden information may be extracted</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8067-8979-fdb769684c1f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Some techniques can be vulnerable to steganaly</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8083-833d-e547f7e613b6" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-9738-eccd2fa3a903" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">A Brief Overview of Hardware Security</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80cb-867d-e36365a54d01"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8004-bf1a-c6f967371ffd" class="">A Brief Overview of Hardware Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-800c-bb3c-d4c1d8cb4394" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b0-917c-cf7d7aed00be" class="">Hardware security refers to the protection of <strong>physical devices, hardware components, and integrated circuits</strong> from unauthorized access, tampering, or damage. It focuses on securing the <strong>physical layer of computer systems</strong> to prevent attackers from stealing data, modifying hardware, or inserting malicious components.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8023-ba27-d3144b59c9a6" class="">With the increasing use of computers, servers, mobile devices, and Internet-connected systems, hardware security has become an essential part of overall <strong>information security</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a9-bad3-cd675d0bc656" class="">Hardware security ensures that devices operate reliably and that sensitive data stored in hardware components remains protected.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-a992-dea7b0604bc5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-801f-88ae-f8353a94635f" class="">2. Objectives of Hardware Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8090-bfbf-ed87145ba6e6" class="">The main objectives of hardware security are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80d6-97fc-c05c110ff1d7" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Protect hardware components</strong> from unauthorized physical access.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80b2-a8f8-ee9c02a532af" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Ensure integrity of devices</strong> so that hardware cannot be altered or tampered with.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8031-9f8a-c043300f5b4d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Prevent data theft</strong> from storage devices.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8009-9be4-fa4a1e5933fa" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Maintain system availability</strong> by protecting hardware from damage or attacks.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8064-9d96-d9c7fd68ae94" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Secure embedded systems and IoT devices</strong> used in modern networks.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e8-8a07-fc622b172e64"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8063-917b-dd7f10c90316" class="">3. Importance of Hardware Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8016-9c59-dc3815509403" class="">Hardware security is important because:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8017-898f-fbd7eff884f5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Physical access to a device can allow attackers to bypass software security.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-809b-b8c5-ca4ac2e403dc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hardware components store sensitive data such as encryption keys.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8048-a08f-e65382e7166e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Attacks on hardware can compromise the entire system.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8056-a569-c9e1e36982b9" class="">For example, attackers may attempt to steal data from <strong>hard drives, memory chips, or network devices</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8091-bf7f-e907716de556"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80c7-ba2c-e5ffe6852b33" class="">4. Common Hardware Security Threats</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8072-bdb0-e32c123d97d2" class="">1. Physical Tampering</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80eb-8333-e1aec61b8f03" class="">Physical tampering occurs when attackers gain direct access to a device and attempt to modify or damage its components.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802f-9f64-ca837c2bd284" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e3-bad2-e304561ab8f0" class="">Opening a device and altering internal circuits.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-800f-87e6-d3d8ba2c9f40"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-803b-9e27-ef8e1b5b7cb0" class="">2. Hardware Trojans</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8085-a044-d05ba9cfc51c" class="">Hardware Trojans are <strong>malicious modifications inserted into hardware circuits</strong> during manufacturing or design.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8098-9deb-c446d90d0280" class="">These hidden circuits can leak sensitive information or disrupt system operations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8008-841e-c0a5866721d2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8094-ba6a-cba4221dd683" class="">3. Side-Channel Attacks</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a2-b1fd-c0756437ebcc" class="">Side-channel attacks exploit <strong>physical characteristics of hardware</strong>, such as power consumption, electromagnetic signals, or timing information, to extract secret data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8067-a70f-cbce58ab873a" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-81f1-cc5e5b5eff6b" class="">Analyzing power usage to discover encryption keys.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-805c-8337-f16967bc035e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-805f-b3be-d2b789e64821" class="">4. Theft of Devices</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80be-a0f3-d9eabe16ba80" class="">If devices like laptops or servers are stolen, attackers may gain access to stored data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8050-b276-c792a9c63f1c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-9e73-e69ab6956848" class="">5. Supply Chain Attacks</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806a-8e26-ee23dab08e6b" class="">Attackers may introduce malicious hardware components during the <strong>manufacturing or distribution process</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b2-b389-ec6a3008b933"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8054-9094-fc91da024fee" class="">5. Hardware Security Mechanisms</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-a431-e9c5324b7468" class="">Several mechanisms are used to protect hardware systems:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-bc0a-f0ed3d6126a9" class="">1. Trusted Platform Module (TPM)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8039-9d9b-c8d318687cd3" class="">A TPM is a specialized chip that securely stores <strong>cryptographic keys and authentication data</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8057-a260-e9f946001016"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8079-b10f-e0a69e3571df" class="">2. Hardware Security Modules (HSM)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fd-a439-c5a8b8f392cb" class="">HSMs are dedicated devices used to <strong>manage encryption keys and perform cryptographic operations securely</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-805a-9a8d-c6ea7cac6083"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-ab71-c333344dcd46" class="">3. Secure Boot</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80bd-a6d1-fcf992e3a02e" class="">Secure boot ensures that a device starts only with <strong>trusted and verified software</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8046-a4e7-f18f591edb35"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-806e-a342-fa6a94881206" class="">4. Physical Security Controls</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805e-aaaf-eb6648ba60a0" class="">Examples include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8094-a307-ef11e11eabe4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Locks and access control systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-804b-b46d-d7aaff2b9e56" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Surveillance cameras</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8062-ab25-dabceaa63fa7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Biometric authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807f-a5ff-e09723fd9661" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure server rooms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e3-8ba4-e27fade93dd1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8036-8106-d1ca4469e2a4" class="">5. Tamper-Resistant Hardware</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8051-b3f9-eb6507689ef6" class="">Some devices include mechanisms that <strong>detect physical tampering</strong> and automatically erase sensitive data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8041-a079-c5691921fb2f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8086-8c19-d052790f2f74" class="">6. Applications of Hardware Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8059-8fd1-d8a29989079e" class="">Hardware security is widely used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8044-aa43-c1e471bf4ec7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Banking systems and ATMs</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-acb3-ecf3f7ec3f38" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Smart cards and payment systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8008-97f2-ebad6f92917c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Government and military systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-9099-ca061e71401b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data centers and servers</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ad-8fbe-c4d1076a7412" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Internet of Things (IoT) devices</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-ac47-d2e6a895de9b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8015-933a-d2197b396233" class="">7. Advantages of Hardware Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8005-874f-cd90eb41dfe0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides strong protection at the <strong>physical level</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8017-911b-caa04867b2cc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Difficult for attackers to bypass</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-8598-dcdaa31c77e0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects sensitive cryptographic keys</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e5-9bc9-ef10c9e21665" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Enhances overall system security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-808f-9665-d9640da9b1ce"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8053-bfa8-f6e607246af0" class="">8. Limitations</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8090-9196-c22882000b0c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Implementation cost can be high</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80f4-a969-e19389a671ef" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hardware upgrades may be required</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ec-aa28-c5cc391803d7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Physical protection alone cannot prevent all cyber attacks</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ea-906c-c63deb8187c3" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80ac-a504-dde188f2715a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80db-be79-d6df1eeac1b1" class=""><strong>Module 2 : Secret and Public Key Cryptography</strong></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f1-a840-ccf86d5805b6" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Modern Block Ciphers: Block Ciphers Principles</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-800b-8041-e3c95b73d8e8" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Shannon’s Theory of Confusion and Diffusion</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-86f4-de55acd91f46" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Festal Structure</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8065-85cc-dc9adc9abeb4" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Data Encryption Standard (DES)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8086-856b-d25187333ec7" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Strength of DES</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8012-9c1a-e7baa5f49dfb" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Idea of Differential Cryptanalysis</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8054-8f28-d1b081c82d44" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Block Cipher Modes of Operations</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8073-a93c-ce6f98717299" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Triple DES. </mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-afbb-d45c451c193d" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) encryption and decryption</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8019-a3c2-ea45f3c6e9e0" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Fermat’s and Euler’s theorem</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80cb-8b48-f687aa4cd328" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="11"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Chinese Remainder theorem</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8081-981f-d76c6d453cd1" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="12"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Principals of Public Key Crypto Systems</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-805f-9753-c39d307dde59" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="13"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">RSA algorithm</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8015-a7be-dd50585747d1" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="14"><li>Security of RSA algorithm.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8065-8266-c105871fbee5" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8075-be67-e582a5a3d011" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Modern Block Ciphers: Block Cipher Principles</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80de-9112-eae3e889947b"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-801a-9a45-c1f6c10f15ef" class="">Modern Block Ciphers: Block Cipher Principles</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-9980-d1e965057f96" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ac-afd1-c66c31ad8258" class="">A <strong>block cipher</strong> is a type of symmetric key cryptographic algorithm that encrypts data in <strong>fixed-size blocks</strong> rather than encrypting one bit or character at a time.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807a-9bf3-d76ecfc01059" class="">In block ciphers, a block of plaintext is taken as input and transformed into a block of ciphertext using an encryption algorithm and a secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8083-a3dd-d02027e68cee" class="">Modern block ciphers are widely used in <strong>secure communication, data protection, and digital security systems</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803a-8eca-eb35ca97d23b" class="">Examples of modern block ciphers include <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong>, <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong>, and <strong>Blowfish</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8070-bb20-d8736fb3d8e6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80b0-8f86-d9b130836626" class="">2. Basic Structure of a Block Cipher</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-b6c2-e2cf06e8147c" class="">A block cipher performs encryption by applying a sequence of <strong>mathematical transformations</strong> on plaintext blocks.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8062-af67-f6e017c86ef3" class="">Basic Encryption Process</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-9838-c7e2e9292152" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext Block + Secret Key
           |
     Encryption Algorithm
           |
       Ciphertext Block</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8094-8d86-cca65798a692" class="">The same key (or a related key) is used in the <strong>decryption process</strong> to recover the original plaintext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80c1-9d5a-d7aad00ed7a9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-803f-9748-d37ee2aa7ff0" class="">3. Characteristics of Modern Block Ciphers</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803c-85b0-ec5ee0a9f6fb" class="">Modern block ciphers have several important characteristics:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-809b-b1ef-fc5461f36e26" class="">1. Fixed Block Size</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8071-ac52-d1f38f1aa273" class="">Data is processed in <strong>fixed-size blocks</strong>, such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8084-b58e-e040f8e3156c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">64 bits (DES)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ac-b67a-d73320fd513a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">128 bits (AES)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-b44a-c4524e6c5cfd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80dd-b2a8-f40157d34056" class="">2. Symmetric Key Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8010-a7d5-cad7f14e4265" class="">The <strong>same secret key</strong> is used for both encryption and decryption.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-806d-afb8-fd0c0d593ccb"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-807e-baf0-e58c462fa034" class="">3. Multiple Rounds of Processing</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8048-b05c-f144bd7a2ae1" class="">Encryption is performed through <strong>multiple rounds of transformations</strong> to increase security.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800d-9415-e4ca99c7793a" class="">Each round performs operations such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8059-bf5e-eab0fc34ba33" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">substitution</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8060-a10b-dca30993e43d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">permutation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-809f-933d-ce81cf9d421c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">mixing of data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80cc-b01d-f20eb458041f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-805c-80b0-ed2dae15f0a5" class="">4. Principles of Block Cipher Design</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8061-b84e-e232c6def994" class="">Modern block cipher design is based on two fundamental principles introduced by <strong>Claude Shannon</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80bc-86b0-e781a95d3ea0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-919d-ec1605631913" class="">1. Confusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-9b8a-ee4bb75b2bfd" class="">Confusion refers to making the relationship between the <strong>encryption key and ciphertext as complex as possible</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8085-b1e3-ea0481008081" class="">This ensures that attackers cannot easily determine the key even if they analyze the ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-801d-9c10-cd980960d595" class="">Example</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8045-b00a-f8da2ae00f8c" class="">Substitution operations that replace bits or characters with others.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8005-83b2-f3c8282b5f53"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8016-93d3-f9141009c359" class="">2. Diffusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ff-a866-defce4b8ec6a" class="">Diffusion spreads the influence of <strong>each plaintext bit over many ciphertext bits</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8013-9497-f043cf2e4e55" class="">This ensures that a small change in plaintext produces a <strong>large change in ciphertext</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-808e-8444-fe910089d1aa" class="">Example</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-beb8-f46f7c0279aa" class="">Permutation operations that rearrange bits.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-802c-b0e2-f2bfd86abe97"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-804b-8021-de3df1c40c2b" class="">5. Feistel Structure</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fa-9d99-d6e7433c4f57" class="">Many modern block ciphers use the <strong>Feistel network structure</strong>, which divides the plaintext block into two halves and processes them in multiple rounds.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8077-b936-fcadacf5b386" class="">The Feistel structure was used in <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8024-9e70-e468ab91f5bd" class="">Basic Feistel Structure</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-809d-ba43-d4f90a8b383b" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext Block
      |
Split into Left (L) and Right (R)
      |
Round Function + Key
      |
Swap and Repeat for Several Rounds
      |
Ciphertext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80cc-8e0c-d373511e6a52" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805e-bac6-c95aa105c8f9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Same structure can be used for encryption and decryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8008-a25f-fb816f9e2697" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides strong security through multiple rounds</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-808a-a51a-eab8245c0048"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8049-a0ea-f6176314318b" class="">6. Components of a Block Cipher</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801e-bbe1-cee8aec56b37" class="">Modern block ciphers typically include the following components:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8052-9486-db1628dfbf3b" class="">1. Substitution (S-box)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805e-9c5a-da8f7684c3cd" class="">Substitution boxes replace input bits with different output bits to create confusion.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-809f-81c7-f36b51a8bd4f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8051-ae22-e7f209079fc8" class="">2. Permutation (P-box)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-91ee-e410573de5ed" class="">Permutation boxes rearrange bits to achieve diffusion.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80de-bb02-cc30cc1bf77f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8052-9e28-e1cf65151683" class="">3. Round Function</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80aa-be4e-f898f24e26c4" class="">Each round applies a specific transformation using a <strong>round key</strong> derived from the main key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8057-8bc6-cd16331986a8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-804e-9ae5-d29eefcb5f18" class="">4. Key Schedule</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-a960-e2985005d0f4" class="">The key schedule algorithm generates <strong>different round keys</strong> from the original secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80f2-9fea-c8b8d7abcf74"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80fc-8f8f-cb8089fd7f83" class="">7. Advantages of Block Ciphers</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-803b-8569-f38eb95eadeb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide strong security for data encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8037-ad37-f01336dfefe9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Suitable for large data encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80f3-867c-eeca801211d4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Widely used in secure communication protocols</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8084-8483-d7aa24cfb467"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809f-82ba-fdfa369c6d6a" class="">8. Applications of Block Ciphers</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-9383-f874ae6ee86e" class="">Block ciphers are used in many security systems:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8045-b678-f52f9834eb10" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure internet communication (HTTPS)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80fc-94c3-e52027b2433a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Banking and financial transactions</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-802f-9055-c7699888970e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data encryption in storage systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8006-96f3-f2ff49b6d27f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Virtual Private Networks (VPN)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-88c4-e3d57a0d9c53"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-801e-a270-c527d88db825" class="">9. Limitations</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-808c-8c50-d55bbacb49d0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires proper <strong>mode of operation</strong> for large data encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80a2-adfe-f1f758e9fbee" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Computational overhead may be higher than stream ciphers</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806f-8b6b-f0e32250668d" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801c-8502-dc5ea7bd1e27" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-86e9-c38b747399e3" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Data Encryption Standard (DES)</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8075-96e5-f9d979a1b423"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8036-b4ff-d14f1457eb59" class="">4. Data Encryption Standard (DES)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8053-8f2e-e23d99a256c7" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8016-8fb9-efc2cbdc53e1" class="">The <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong> is one of the most widely known symmetric key encryption algorithms used for securing data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b7-8252-ef0a19b6e6cc" class="">It was developed by <strong>IBM</strong> in the early 1970s and later adopted as a federal standard by the <strong>National Institute of Standards and Technology</strong> in 1977.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809d-8671-da09caa63b0c" class="">DES is a <strong>block cipher</strong> that encrypts data in <strong>64-bit blocks</strong> using a <strong>56-bit secret key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806d-b31b-fa4a0510c5ed" class="">Although DES was widely used in banking and communication systems, it is now considered insecure due to its short key length.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8006-bbd0-c8a7fb1b9f78"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8076-8ffd-e8c5b045e4cc" class="">2. Basic Characteristics of DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-8058-9872-f03be8915d1c" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-806d-9aaf-f8e3b0404707"><th id="qtM`" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Feature</th><th id="ZyfM" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Description</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80cb-8c07-df753355224f"><td id="qtM`" class="">Block Size</td><td id="ZyfM" class="">64 bits</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-bce9-f10954265029"><td id="qtM`" class="">Key Size</td><td id="ZyfM" class="">56 bits</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80d3-97df-d60d0dd8e745"><td id="qtM`" class="">Number of Rounds</td><td id="ZyfM" class="">16 rounds</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80d1-8f81-c5ebe504f43f"><td id="qtM`" class="">Cipher Type</td><td id="ZyfM" class="">Symmetric Block Cipher</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-806e-a592-cefe4bff7805"><td id="qtM`" class="">Structure</td><td id="ZyfM" class="">Feistel Network</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-807c-9d06-c71c2ebf7681"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-bd9c-efbe07024fbc" class="">3. Working of DES Algorithm</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8023-83e8-f05651a0e8b2" class="">DES works by applying <strong>16 rounds of complex operations</strong> on the plaintext block.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8054-ae2f-f0e329dba12b" class="">Step 1: Initial Permutation (IP)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805b-bd8d-d84a56f9e04d" class="">The plaintext (64 bits) undergoes an <strong>initial permutation</strong>, which rearranges the bits according to a predefined pattern.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8023-b03c-fad427efde9d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8066-bc19-e52a3c44c25e" class="">Step 2: Splitting the Block</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809a-9b88-cbd8224013fe" class="">The permuted block is divided into two halves:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805d-9dda-d336ce470ee0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Left half (L)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8099-be09-d9d78f1f267c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Right half (R)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ea-b6e2-e742cce42c75" class="">Each half contains <strong>32 bits</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8081-a00f-ccf030c9b36a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-806f-92af-f239a0abe286" class="">Step 3: 16 Feistel Rounds</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809a-ae3c-eac8baca7a78" class="">DES performs <strong>16 rounds of encryption</strong>, and in each round the following operations occur:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-9f9c-e2b6ef79295e" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Expansion of the right half</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8075-851e-d88c8d787b3b" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>XOR operation with the round key</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8091-980c-e165ee9e5dbc" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Substitution using <strong>S-boxes</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8003-871d-e40eec177734" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Permutation using <strong>P-box</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803e-ac55-c9bf950d144d" class="">Then the halves are swapped.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8007-bc72-f89634326363"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-807c-b6f7-ca438b71dd22" class="">Step 4: Final Permutation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d9-99c3-f31635694245" class="">After the 16 rounds, a <strong>final permutation (inverse of initial permutation)</strong> is applied to produce the ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a3-8fee-ca79341f1402"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-a2f7-c71b2372f53e" class="">Basic Structure of DES</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-805b-9f72-cd89326fc1ab" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext (64 bits)
        |
Initial Permutation
        |
Split into L and R
        |
16 Feistel Rounds
        |
Final Permutation
        |
Ciphertext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80be-9096-d77fe8823bae"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-a4de-d6abbd7ea9ad" class="">4. Key Generation in DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8080-a1fb-ede2b9b01560" class="">The 56-bit key is used to generate <strong>16 different round keys</strong>, one for each round of encryption.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c4-ab10-c2e891038e9b" class="">This process is called the <strong>key schedule</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8087-bf04-cb4cd37021f3" class="">Each round uses a <strong>48-bit subkey</strong> derived from the original key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8097-be63-d4b26ad07053"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809a-a4dc-d2dfb7857f30" class="">5. Applications of DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-b200-ff00ffca1d17" class="">DES was widely used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b9-a6e2-d4291f5343c4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Banking systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8026-b2d3-f645ac0d87ca" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">ATM networks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8048-9f8e-e02da583ae31" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure communication systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-801c-9ae5-c103883c718b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data encryption in government organizations</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b0-bf4f-c36b77649002"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8037-b819-c427a1fd07a7" class="">5. Strength of DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-809a-a3f1-f3fac33162b4" class="">1. Strong Encryption Structure</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802d-9c7a-ee6b9e7737a3" class="">DES uses the <strong>Feistel structure</strong>, which provides strong security through multiple rounds of transformations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8098-bc7e-e6cb860781aa" class="">The 16 rounds make it difficult to directly analyze the relationship between plaintext and ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-a972-d5f1e676c8b2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8012-a4bf-f5a630563a46" class="">2. Confusion and Diffusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-804e-bd2e-dff2cbd96f29" class="">DES implements the principles of <strong>confusion and diffusion</strong>, introduced by <strong>Claude Shannon</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8065-86ab-f22cc341926a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Confusion</strong> – achieved using substitution boxes (S-boxes)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-803e-b310-c2abc75e5da1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Diffusion</strong> – achieved through permutation operations</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-9935-ee08c7096ce5" class="">This increases resistance to cryptanalysis.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8028-9726-df798ca26bb1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8071-93cf-cffa9898d808" class="">3. Resistance to Some Cryptanalytic Attacks</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-8a67-db48298aabe1" class="">DES was designed to resist certain types of attacks such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-86dc-e1635c3e7ba3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Differential cryptanalysis</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8042-8b13-c16dad5fe817" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Linear cryptanalysis (to some extent)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8068-b84d-c40b18fc08d9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-bdcb-cda408972e51" class="">4. Efficient Hardware Implementation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-a777-f932682f231a" class="">DES is efficient when implemented in <strong>hardware systems</strong>, which made it practical for early secure communication systems.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80be-8cd6-d36b2d02557c" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-91f1-cd0cd3ffe336" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Idea of Differential Cryptanalysis</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803d-bd83-fea2d50b7efb" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8060-8ccb-c0f1691317f3"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8072-82a0-de1d282f85aa" class="">Idea of Differential Cryptanalysis</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-af2c-e4bff54ada60" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8074-9ff5-c33802a68845" class="">Differential cryptanalysis is a powerful method used to analyze and break symmetric key cryptographic algorithms, especially <strong>block ciphers</strong>. It studies how <strong>differences in plaintext inputs affect the differences in ciphertext outputs</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8020-a065-cd5931268f98" class="">This technique was developed by <strong>Eli Biham</strong> and <strong>Adi Shamir</strong> in the late 1980s. It became one of the most important methods for evaluating the security of encryption algorithms such as <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801b-94c3-e13dfc90c5b3" class="">The main idea behind differential cryptanalysis is that certain <strong>patterns of input differences may produce predictable output differences</strong>, which can help attackers discover the secret encryption key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d1-b8b0-d7bbbf5409ca"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8025-9383-f5ca35197810" class="">2. Basic Concept</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8074-95ea-ca2bd6b2847f" class="">Differential cryptanalysis focuses on analyzing <strong>pairs of plaintext messages</strong> that have a specific difference.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803b-b352-c7047546b626" class="">The attacker observes how this difference propagates through the encryption process and affects the resulting ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-99e0-c85a8a102022" class="">Basic Steps</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-809a-b029-f69babbbb79a" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Choose two plaintext inputs with a specific difference.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-8ebb-f0911783eb7f" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Encrypt both plaintexts.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-82cf-ea6ed87bf404" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Observe the difference between the resulting ciphertexts.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8065-8534-ede3f26fbe24" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Analyze patterns to guess parts of the encryption key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-801f-90d1-d58ddcfcee18"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-8ed7-c18e0fa7c102" class="">Example Concept</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fd-956e-ea9f1fb3d236" class="">Suppose two plaintext messages differ by only one bit.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8034-86da-d0f88c3fb30a" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext 1 → Encryption → Ciphertext 1
Plaintext 2 → Encryption → Ciphertext 2</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ff-bea1-cb8272be34b6" class="">By comparing:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8007-91c1-dd823f15b8d2" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Difference in plaintext → Difference in ciphertext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8068-85ba-f274693567bc" class="">An attacker can analyze how the cipher processes data and eventually recover the key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8084-a101-d5699ae24728"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8079-b336-c49cfab290b2" class="">3. Differential Cryptanalysis Process</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8020-b5fd-d884d8aea304" class="">The process generally involves the following steps:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80de-a085-ed1834283e86" class="">1. Selection of Plaintext Pairs</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8068-9360-ef99ea6d2cee" class="">The attacker selects many plaintext pairs with a specific difference.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-9c16-e6154bcf54c7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8029-b781-c7fd06d9fbb2" class="">2. Encryption of Plaintext Pairs</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80aa-9b29-c720430f9919" class="">Each pair is encrypted using the unknown secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80ed-b514-ffc1fb8c134d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-b035-c8ca90b5826e" class="">3. Observation of Ciphertext Differences</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e0-b7ac-eed93e1fb950" class="">The attacker calculates the difference between ciphertext outputs.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-804f-9b5a-d27ee6a433a0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ee-907f-f5d488e17456" class="">4. Key Guessing</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e8-baf0-dd7cd34c759a" class="">Based on the observed patterns, the attacker guesses possible values of the encryption key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80aa-910e-fb97398a5465"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-8b4b-d8bf9af7fde8" class="">4. Differential Characteristics</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8062-a5d6-dbcd38ee4f3b" class="">A <strong>differential characteristic</strong> describes how differences in plaintext propagate through the rounds of a cipher.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8069-a7f3-dd8434cc2dfa" class="">It is used to estimate the <strong>probability that a specific input difference produces a specific output difference</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808a-bd5a-ee1a83d14c94" class="">High-probability characteristics help attackers predict how differences move through the encryption process.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-800a-bff2-ce6aab25320c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809f-8a60-c1421bb85e85" class="">5. Application to Block Ciphers</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8018-80ef-fe12048c791c" class="">Differential cryptanalysis is mainly used against <strong>iterative block ciphers</strong> that perform multiple rounds of encryption.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-80fa-c945ef7b0538" class="">Examples include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8083-a547-fbf91bcdc098" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807d-be19-d2b8f28718ce" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80de-be59-f3155dd78b84" class="">Modern algorithms are designed specifically to <strong>resist differential cryptanalysis</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8090-9ae2-c9b2a6c3617b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8067-9369-e2a2271bfe8f" class="">6. Advantages of Differential Cryptanalysis</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-800a-9f92-efd8e016dcaf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Powerful method for analyzing encryption algorithms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8057-b6f1-e29f4ae3557e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Helps evaluate the strength of block ciphers</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80cc-8e10-e380271882a5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Useful in designing more secure cryptographic systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-805d-8adb-fd656c38365a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80dd-99d1-f0c33986e350" class="">7. Limitations</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8059-a458-f9d0f50b0286" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires a <strong>large number of plaintext pairs</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80cb-91e4-d679779b9645" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Not practical for well-designed modern encryption algorithms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8062-99b3-f3af18823103" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Computationally complex</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806f-82e3-d6e79d317a87" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8076-a883-c1ed8c2c03f0" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Block Cipher Modes of Operation</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8059-8bfa-c6e677c7be88"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8021-93c4-f6be12fb6937" class="">Block Cipher Modes of Operation</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8003-9b59-e7d9729097a8" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8075-985a-c1e85ab7f8e4" class="">A <strong>block cipher</strong> encrypts data in <strong>fixed-size blocks</strong> (for example 64 bits or 128 bits). However, in real-world applications, messages are usually much longer than a single block. Therefore, <strong>block cipher modes of operation</strong> are used to securely encrypt long messages by processing multiple blocks of data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8012-8027-d891778c9ffa" class="">These modes define <strong>how plaintext blocks are encrypted and how ciphertext blocks are produced</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8023-9eb3-f09f6bd327d1" class="">Block cipher modes are widely used with algorithms such as <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong> and <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80c1-89b2-defd2673461e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80ec-acc0-c5ac8e0ef015" class="">2. Need for Modes of Operation</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8011-b489-f54ceabf8648" class="">If the same encryption algorithm is applied directly to each block, identical plaintext blocks will produce identical ciphertext blocks. This can reveal patterns and weaken security.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8052-a8f1-cc09bb5a1f2e" class="">Modes of operation solve this problem by introducing <strong>chaining, feedback, or counters</strong> so that each block is encrypted differently.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-b5c7-cd217f5af02a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80de-bd00-cdac64972fd1" class="">3. Types of Block Cipher Modes</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-b350-f42866190206" class="">The commonly used block cipher modes are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8072-82e5-d90d6efc3a64" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Electronic Codebook (ECB)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8020-b73b-c0a9664f2bc1" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Cipher Block Chaining (CBC)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8003-b7b2-f355bf9f7aa1" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Cipher Feedback (CFB)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8008-b36b-eddd8eb2c9ee" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Output Feedback (OFB)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8057-8534-e1e804de69c1" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Counter Mode (CTR)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8089-977b-cf5934956932"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80e8-850d-e315e66ed0fa" class="">4. Electronic Codebook (ECB) Mode</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8084-a166-c89cd1a65c7e" class="">In <strong>ECB mode</strong>, each block of plaintext is encrypted independently using the same key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8016-9de7-d0cbcd7c320d" class="">Working</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-803d-8134-f672817b0a9c" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext Block → Encryption → Ciphertext Block</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8097-bde8-fc137bdf6a7a" class="">Characteristics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e3-a0c3-d647eb0484cf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simple and fast</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e0-a18a-f7733764f910" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">No dependency between blocks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8075-b399-e4735fb04862" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8056-be5e-c55fe608ec2e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-806e-b11c-cb1e29b89dd3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Patterns may be visible</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8016-85dc-f4baaaf83f43"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-806e-a4f9-eb4fad28afaf" class="">5. Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) Mode</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8099-b446-e3d00c8c7a79" class="">In <strong>CBC mode</strong>, each plaintext block is <strong>XORed with the previous ciphertext block</strong> before encryption.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807a-9958-f69c6c31a3bf" class="">An <strong>Initialization Vector (IV)</strong> is used for the first block.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8087-a776-ec05ae0017df" class="">Working</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8083-8c4e-d77ec15ba657" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext Block
       |
XOR with Previous Ciphertext
       |
Encryption
       |
Ciphertext Block</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-801d-b72b-f96729bb31a3" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8009-9159-d2e2b61629d2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides better security than ECB</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805c-ab39-e8c9907d47af" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hides patterns in data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-808f-ac10-f4ae58f2b785" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80d7-a6c1-d7138c7134ac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption is slower because blocks depend on previous blocks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8012-9566-ca060cf06731"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80b2-bb98-dd48003cbf25" class="">6. Cipher Feedback (CFB) Mode</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d8-905f-d047d0082d3a" class="">CFB mode converts a block cipher into a <strong>stream cipher</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8007-af63-cb31ec7a9263" class="">Instead of encrypting plaintext directly, the previous ciphertext is encrypted and then combined with plaintext using XOR.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8094-9405-fb24afc87fe6" class="">Features</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8018-8bfb-e4c0dcf40c0a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Suitable for real-time data transmission</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8076-b078-d4676027de7f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Errors propagate to some following blocks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e6-ba9c-d58eae114ba7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-807e-85b3-f524f9548c71" class="">7. Output Feedback (OFB) Mode</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80eb-b80f-c15e7ecfa430" class="">In OFB mode, the encryption algorithm generates a <strong>keystream</strong> that is XORed with plaintext to produce ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80cf-b127-efc0de7dd5b5" class="">Characteristics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8017-9ba6-eee76c1944a9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Prevents error propagation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ba-8aa4-f6c5c4192514" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Works like a stream cipher</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e8-bd84-eca3cf129117"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-804e-a960-f0df2e63aad7" class="">8. Counter (CTR) Mode</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809a-ac66-e0f299550283" class="">In CTR mode, a <strong>counter value</strong> is encrypted and then XORed with plaintext to generate ciphertext.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-802d-a86f-d5033e0795f0" class="">Working</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-809f-9f5b-d7603a18c3fa" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Counter Value
     |
Encryption
     |
Keystream
     |
XOR with Plaintext
     |
Ciphertext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80c8-a8a3-d49f9cd34941" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80fb-9c57-fe7a0138f7e5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">High performance</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8035-ad86-db64af12632c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Allows parallel encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8007-84bf-de86a91cdeb5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Widely used in modern systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-807a-a034-ec23f3f91b39"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-800d-8561-fde39d2297f5" class="">9. Comparison of Block Cipher Modes</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-8022-97dd-fcbedf6eee26" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-99c3-f84f811771d8"><th id="?{?E" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Mode</th><th id="qbvJ" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Security</th><th id="q|gX" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Speed</th><th id="fljC" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Error Propagation</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8084-a94a-fdeb5c798b0c"><td id="?{?E" class="">ECB</td><td id="qbvJ" class="">Low</td><td id="q|gX" class="">Fast</td><td id="fljC" class="">No</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-abdb-f4d742f1c88f"><td id="?{?E" class="">CBC</td><td id="qbvJ" class="">High</td><td id="q|gX" class="">Medium</td><td id="fljC" class="">Yes</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-b22d-fd13dce7a37e"><td id="?{?E" class="">CFB</td><td id="qbvJ" class="">Medium</td><td id="q|gX" class="">Medium</td><td id="fljC" class="">Partial</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8005-a601-fa245d890461"><td id="?{?E" class="">OFB</td><td id="qbvJ" class="">Medium</td><td id="q|gX" class="">Medium</td><td id="fljC" class="">No</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8079-a492-fea7572e635d"><td id="?{?E" class="">CTR</td><td id="qbvJ" class="">High</td><td id="q|gX" class="">Very Fast</td><td id="fljC" class="">No</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80f6-84e6-ec455184cb0d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80e8-bc60-f6c743edfc5a" class="">10. Applications of Block Cipher Modes</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8034-af48-d4612d251928" class="">Block cipher modes are used in many real-world systems:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8001-bd1d-eb7ed951b8a2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure internet communication (HTTPS)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8078-8bd0-dd91586b8478" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Disk encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-9ed9-fe24890f3957" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure messaging systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8005-b4db-e50941665da8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Virtual Private Networks (VPN)</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8061-8657-c19010426dbc" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8059-8bc6-fca7a8fd93ea" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-baf9-c5aae8e2be50" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Triple DES (3DES)</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-83c3-e3f14c718ab8"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8037-8e6b-f9c40ea9a28e" class="">Triple DES (3DES)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8004-b3a2-d04d71aee3f3" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-b67f-d359acdae0c0" class=""><strong>Triple DES</strong> is an improved version of the <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong> designed to increase the security of DES.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c7-abff-e838823d21df" class="">DES uses a <strong>56-bit key</strong>, which became vulnerable to brute-force attacks with modern computing power. To overcome this weakness, Triple DES was introduced. It increases security by <strong>applying the DES algorithm three times</strong> to each block of data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-b8b6-e31d9b8f9e04" class="">Triple DES is also known as <strong>TDEA (Triple Data Encryption Algorithm)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-804b-826a-eb22964b8d45"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80b4-ae28-f039ce2ebcf7" class="">2. Basic Idea of Triple DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-8a21-f2353a54e01f" class="">In Triple DES, the plaintext block is encrypted <strong>three times using DES operations</strong> with either two or three different keys.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807b-b750-ed9152754c8d" class="">This multiple encryption greatly increases the <strong>effective key length and security</strong> compared to single DES.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-b3cf-ce836c76e464"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8057-95f1-e864cd3f35af" class="">3. Working of Triple DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80eb-b9b3-ea0248899b1e" class="">The encryption process follows the <strong>Encrypt–Decrypt–Encrypt (EDE)</strong> sequence.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ab-8fd4-d95bcf33aaf1" class="">Encryption Steps</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-9b33-ec14c2c73adc" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Encrypt the plaintext using <strong>Key 1</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-803f-9190-e9f54455e5e1" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Decrypt the result using <strong>Key 2</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-802d-85e5-dc28d95a11b8" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Encrypt again using <strong>Key 3</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-801f-8cc1-f880d6e1f629" class="">Basic Process</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8056-bfdd-ddb71730b608" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext
    |
DES Encryption (Key1)
    |
DES Decryption (Key2)
    |
DES Encryption (Key3)
    |
Ciphertext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80da-8f41-db22d5c86085" class="">During decryption, the process is reversed.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-8d6c-cbf2fc88797e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8045-95bc-fee5c5492312" class="">4. Keying Options in Triple DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8061-951e-eb229159a7d4" class="">Triple DES can use different key combinations:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-804f-81e7-cd7f20580dc5" class="">1. Three-Key Triple DES</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-b15d-faae7fcdba14" class="">Uses <strong>three different keys</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8047-a2c4-f397b5ef4d7c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K1</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-9725-f9be1417eaf4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K2</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-803a-8f6e-ec4c0bd0886c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K3</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c9-b59f-fe0985215361" class="">Effective key length = <strong>168 bits</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-a7f6-d710ff5c8b7f" class="">This provides the highest level of security.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-801b-97b4-fb3f8ca3b4ab"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-800c-bd31-d609d08741d2" class="">2. Two-Key Triple DES</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-be3b-fda7123efff3" class="">Uses <strong>two keys</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-9bc4-ec3065e3d4a9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K1</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80df-8308-e24a96a45bdc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K2</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-a0f2-d0b90f0f2353" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K3 = K1</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8040-9d62-e7413262421f" class="">Effective key length = <strong>112 bits</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8032-833a-febeb9be6f90" class="">This version is more commonly used.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-803b-923d-f48124ff82de"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-805f-804a-d1aff4076a78" class="">3. Single-Key Triple DES</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8070-b7cf-dd777e0313f8" class="">Uses the same key for all three operations:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-b03b-ebe27cfab4a7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">K1 = K2 = K3</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803c-aa61-ffb54359fcb5" class="">This is equivalent to normal DES and therefore not secure.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8014-937e-debbc91033de"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8033-b6c9-e9d38e2dc243" class="">5. Advantages of Triple DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8020-9e07-ea0943633855" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Much stronger security</strong> than DES</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8029-bb7b-c7a2d7f41fe9" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Resistant to <strong>brute-force attacks</strong> compared to single DES</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8067-bbf0-c977f2ebd0fe" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Compatible with existing <strong>DES systems</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8002-9f77-ee70280f63e3" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Widely used in financial and banking applications</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-809b-a032-f12110f92edc"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a7-bf84-c22f8dcf07c4" class="">6. Disadvantages of Triple DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8017-a7f2-d878ea1f51bc" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Slower performance</strong> because encryption occurs three times</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8003-beb3-eec5bb3e8cc5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Requires more computational resources</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8092-aab2-e11b77be00fb" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Still less efficient compared to modern algorithms</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807b-9da4-fd9257cf194c" class="">Because of these limitations, many systems now use <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong> instead.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-802c-ba89-d3ab540b6b9f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8032-80f7-d9c25648d41a" class="">7. Applications of Triple DES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b4-b5ee-cd4963ad0cf2" class="">Triple DES has been used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80bc-b489-e57b7471bd35" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Banking systems and ATM networks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8018-8032-dc4c80c199c2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Payment card security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8053-972a-dbc76d50f82d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure financial transactions</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8049-abd7-c556e9744401" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Legacy encryption systems</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8078-bcee-f1539ba06935" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-a6db-ccaee1b5419f" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-802f-aba1-f2963ae72bcc"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-806b-9bdb-d8c36ffb8bf8" class="">Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-a726-db008e92ab72" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-804e-b571-ee1443ca90d8" class="">The <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong> is a modern symmetric key encryption algorithm used to secure digital data. It was selected by the <strong>National Institute of Standards and Technology</strong> in 2001 to replace the <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8089-a7f6-f257fdd7f80c" class="">AES was originally developed by <strong>Joan Daemen</strong> and <strong>Vincent Rijmen</strong> and is also known as the <strong>Rijndael algorithm</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ba-a8fa-fd47b40b5d27" class="">AES is widely used for secure communication, data protection, and encryption in modern systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80cf-980f-c6d7d89bcaaa"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8044-aa28-e633ec69d098" class="">2. Key Features of AES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-8050-a093-ee80aa238c64" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-b72d-cce21c54b2c7"><th id="`hbJ" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Feature</th><th id="o}MA" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Description</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-a8ab-f9ea0fd172ce"><td id="`hbJ" class="">Block Size</td><td id="o}MA" class="">128 bits</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80da-9d76-f88885f931b2"><td id="`hbJ" class="">Key Size</td><td id="o}MA" class="">128, 192, or 256 bits</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-803c-90cd-e2aabcc86f6d"><td id="`hbJ" class="">Structure</td><td id="o}MA" class="">Substitution–Permutation Network</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-800e-adff-f3ee4fe7b823"><td id="`hbJ" class="">Number of Rounds</td><td id="o}MA" class="">10, 12, or 14 rounds</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-808f-9942-c27c798d82c2"><td id="`hbJ" class="">Cipher Type</td><td id="o}MA" class="">Symmetric Block Cipher</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-806c-ac46-da35a329fc0d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-806c-9b89-dd6f195975a3" class="">3. AES Encryption Process</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8087-8d5e-cc058e124a02" class="">AES encryption transforms plaintext into ciphertext through <strong>multiple rounds of transformation</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-802b-9a2d-c025457042b7" class="">Basic Encryption Structure</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-80c8-9ad5-e56c169c1a07" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Plaintext
    |
Add Round Key
    |
SubBytes
    |
ShiftRows
    |
MixColumns
    |
Add Round Key
    |
(Repeat for multiple rounds)
    |
Ciphertext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-bcb1-ef6f184d4f7a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8047-a4c9-fef2f0c85922" class="">AES Encryption Steps</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8058-bc73-f1301a424883" class="">1. AddRoundKey</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-9186-f291e92fcda8" class="">In this step, the plaintext block is <strong>combined with the encryption key using XOR operation</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8036-ad68-d077be2357a0" class="">This is the first transformation applied before the main rounds begin.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-800b-b70b-edb2e14cb31d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8022-a19c-e5457bd3875e" class="">2. SubBytes</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b1-a0ed-ed5097d30c91" class="">Each byte in the data block is substituted using a <strong>substitution box (S-box)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8064-8969-f5758f22dc93" class="">This step introduces <strong>confusion</strong> in the encryption process.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-a76f-de505b2bb66e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-8608-f9c76ad49d8b" class="">3. ShiftRows</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8058-be32-d4d6106d3f70" class="">Rows of the data matrix are <strong>shifted left by different offsets</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8072-bca3-c6ea4975e667" class="">This spreads the data across the block and increases diffusion.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803b-b1a7-c4c7c2da4164" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-b801-d688d91ff728" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80de-bff7-d87e46e930a7"><th id="{Y`U" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Row</th><th id="g;[Y" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Shift</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-803d-bb64-f15678ba29c3"><td id="{Y`U" class="">Row 0</td><td id="g;[Y" class="">No shift</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8089-bc87-df377bcd3384"><td id="{Y`U" class="">Row 1</td><td id="g;[Y" class="">Shift by 1</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-805b-ba9c-c4722f8c30ec"><td id="{Y`U" class="">Row 2</td><td id="g;[Y" class="">Shift by 2</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80df-b40a-f76b5aa3d671"><td id="{Y`U" class="">Row 3</td><td id="g;[Y" class="">Shift by 3</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d0-b1b2-de1165ce7b43"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ab-b91d-f9de9dd613d3" class="">4. MixColumns</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8050-a10b-db579ec2fc91" class="">In this step, columns of the matrix are <strong>mixed using mathematical operations in finite fields</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8089-bfe7-f1382344e2a5" class="">This step ensures that changes in one byte affect the entire column.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-803a-8e88-c894de762a6d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8060-9478-f7a3a8139161" class="">5. AddRoundKey</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8057-a66c-e144d2b46091" class="">The result is XORed again with a <strong>round key</strong> derived from the main key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-801e-b989-e51835d0fe1e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-801f-9b7c-e25bccadd399" class="">Final Round</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8085-b6cc-f8dde3537257" class="">The final round performs:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807b-b6a1-f6a621b3678c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">SubBytes</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-804b-a049-f6240e86ab1e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">ShiftRows</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80be-97f7-cbae2ac7d842" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">AddRoundKey</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-b9b9-fcc4caf1180a" class="">The <strong>MixColumns step is not applied in the final round</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80f6-a953-c6cb520b9569"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809b-89bf-e1cea2011ba6" class="">4. AES Decryption Process</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807a-a2b1-ded7a0675321" class="">AES decryption reverses the encryption process using <strong>inverse transformations</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-8d5e-ecbcd36b568a" class="">Basic Decryption Structure</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-802d-a463-e03c2d716830" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Ciphertext
    |
Add Round Key
    |
Inverse ShiftRows
    |
Inverse SubBytes
    |
Inverse MixColumns
    |
Add Round Key
    |
(Repeat rounds)
    |
Plaintext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80be-b48a-fde89eedab32"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-802e-8cc8-d97fa9e852f9" class="">AES Decryption Steps</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8020-bd09-f9b30990f366" class="">1. Inverse ShiftRows</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a1-9ca2-e84e4568797f" class="">Rows are shifted <strong>to the right</strong> to undo the ShiftRows step.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80aa-88e1-f240b0683b50"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80ec-8f9c-f97befaf89c3" class="">2. Inverse SubBytes</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-9432-dd5661613e84" class="">The inverse S-box is used to reverse the substitution.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-806c-b550-fe7ad8ac157a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8089-a87a-e1195e5031f5" class="">3. Inverse MixColumns</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809c-a00c-dddfa5a9d58a" class="">Columns are mathematically transformed back to their original values.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8046-84af-d0c1638b819a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-803c-8b1b-da667a43f758" class="">4. AddRoundKey</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803f-abcd-d35f9fff34ab" class="">Round keys are XORed with the state matrix to recover the original data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-b4eb-eab1b0833ab7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8099-9a66-ce9420312182" class="">5. AES Encryption Round Structure</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-8074-ac81-fb72b556666f" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80af-b496-c21bbc2e8d5f"><th id="g`F|" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">AES Version</th><th id="fFop" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Key Size</th><th id="idPD" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Number of Rounds</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-806b-ba6c-c4b84b79c0b6"><td id="g`F|" class="">AES-128</td><td id="fFop" class="">128 bits</td><td id="idPD" class="">10</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-804a-a41e-eae6f26eb6e6"><td id="g`F|" class="">AES-192</td><td id="fFop" class="">192 bits</td><td id="idPD" class="">12</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80d2-817b-ecdf64d69852"><td id="g`F|" class="">AES-256</td><td id="fFop" class="">256 bits</td><td id="idPD" class="">14</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-806b-8950-fef1f210af0e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8043-98e5-f6b4f421fa69" class="">6. Advantages of AES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-803e-8f29-e1168c4be6b7" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Very <strong>strong security</strong> compared to older algorithms</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80fa-b1ee-fdc3a6f3166f" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Resistant to most known cryptographic attacks</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-805a-b962-d42d1466c227" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Fast and efficient in both <strong>hardware and software</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-95e1-dddbad69afc3" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Widely adopted global encryption standard</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8026-a29e-d5093694a57a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a1-89fd-d1fdaf6ffa6c" class="">7. Applications of AES</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80cf-b837-cb1ad07d6b33" class="">AES is used in many security systems, including:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-af91-ec1dec5714fb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure internet communication (HTTPS)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8088-b485-d4c62c26c363" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Wireless security (Wi-Fi encryption)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80db-8cc6-de0d341dff9b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">File and disk encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8072-bad6-e6eef8b03769" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Banking and financial systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8009-97da-e0dd01ff58fd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Government and military communications</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8089-a246-fa32a9284fe5" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80de-80a8-ecf1be8c5f1e" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80f8-88be-fcf8bcc8c31b" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Fermat’s Theorem and Euler’s Theorem</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8021-b2c0-d5d2d24ef029"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80c0-96e2-de4211d18e92" class="">Fermat’s Theorem and Euler’s Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-9eec-e25811e55fd2" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8005-82a3-cba575e96ebc" class="">In number theory and cryptography, Fermat’s and Euler’s theorems are important mathematical results used to simplify calculations involving <strong>modular arithmetic</strong>. These theorems help in designing encryption algorithms and are widely applied in public-key cryptography systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-b259-dfe1560aace9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8037-ac34-d3c96226b3ec" class="">2. Fermat’s Theorem (Fermat’s Little Theorem)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807e-b2e9-de9df4c3cbab" class="">The theorem was proposed by the mathematician <strong>Pierre de Fermat</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8013-99bf-f43c3b922afd" class="">Statement of Fermat’s Little Theorem</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800b-8e95-f1613b0dd828" class="">If <strong>p</strong> is a prime number and <strong>a</strong> is an integer such that <strong>a is not divisible by p</strong>, then:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8040-a3de-e273c5a36980" class="">ap−1≡1 (mod p)a^{p-1} \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod } p)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805a-a5a1-fa2029560683" class="">ap−1≡1 (mod p)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-809b-a0f4-ffdbb34ade40" class="">Explanation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8007-926f-edca2f3f1d0a" class="">This means that when <strong>a raised to the power (p − 1)</strong> is divided by <strong>p</strong>, the remainder will always be <strong>1</strong>, provided that <strong>p is prime</strong> and <strong>a is not a multiple of p</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-a4f2-c7b9e8954e4f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80fc-98d5-da000d958bba" class="">Example</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8046-84cf-d602d4d7c16b" class="">Let:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8010-86bd-f690625e18eb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">a=2a = 2a=2</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8017-8a93-e13ce51ff368" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">p=7p = 7p=7 (prime number)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803c-b260-eb8767868aae" class="">According to Fermat’s theorem:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-89c8-d9a43b4735c2" class="">26≡1 (mod 7)2^{6} \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod } 7)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d7-b436-f0ce50366461" class="">26≡1 (mod 7)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805b-ba1e-e27930827701" class="">26=642^{6} = 64</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800b-9dcf-c6bb390eab93" class="">26=64</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-9b49-d64c97f421d4" class="">64÷7=9 remainder 164 \div 7 = 9 \text{ remainder } 1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8068-8038-d7b8adc1359a" class="">64÷7=9 remainder 1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8069-9189-ecc951f08a6f" class="">So the theorem is verified.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8081-b1da-fe81531ca595"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-804d-beb2-c7380a67bf31" class="">3. Importance of Fermat’s Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-baae-f62d57cb5ff7" class="">Fermat’s theorem is useful for:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8034-8023-e23d8f254dd4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simplifying large modular exponentiation calculations</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8075-8b97-c7d77492bda8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Checking prime numbers</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-8f28-eb1f0072448d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Designing public key cryptography algorithms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-801a-ad6c-e4ed8f631f74" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Understanding number theory concepts used in cryptography</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8036-a581-d57b8caccadc"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80f8-86c8-e5af3b9dc51c" class="">4. Euler’s Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-aefd-ddbfd7d6481e" class="">Euler’s theorem is a generalization of Fermat’s theorem and was proposed by <strong>Leonhard Euler</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8046-92f6-e477d608bc14"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80eb-8b28-f5676fa49b5e" class="">Statement of Euler’s Theorem</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fd-8b26-d69ba3eb71b7" class="">If <strong>a</strong> and <strong>n</strong> are coprime (their greatest common divisor is 1), then:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-b239-dd6878b680d9" class="">aϕ(n)≡1 (mod n)a^{\phi(n)} \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod } n)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8001-96f0-d84e7da73706" class="">aϕ(n)≡1 (mod n)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f3-92a7-e0b3588b85ed" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807b-baf8-fe24a2e1b4d8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>φ(n)</strong> is the <strong>Euler Totient Function</strong>, which counts the number of integers less than <strong>n</strong> that are relatively prime to <strong>n</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-b3d8-eb66fb149d5e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8023-8fff-f1c9c2575b7c" class="">Example</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8048-9859-e87c1b54b06c" class="">Let:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-824c-c5a60d0a2ed3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">a=3a = 3a=3</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8085-9c31-eb376ad122a1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">n=10n = 10n=10</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801e-ba6d-e0292174e743" class="">First find:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8032-a0a6-c4b7dcb7c85e" class="">ϕ(10)=4\phi(10) = 4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80de-8c49-c888b4e7c48c" class="">ϕ(10)=4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8054-b336-f9c8297da712" class="">Because numbers relatively prime to 10 are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8064-b2bb-f585c3a170fe" class="">1, 3, 7, 9 → total = 4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807f-8bd3-c11510649381" class="">Now apply Euler’s theorem:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8014-8639-cf26df430551" class="">34≡1 (mod 10)3^{4} \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod } 10)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ed-840f-e5bd633c36ff" class="">34≡1 (mod 10)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f4-af84-c18ef743be06" class="">34=813^{4} = 81</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8056-9d93-c104799edb10" class="">34=81</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8008-89b7-d3a5595e3892" class="">81÷10=8 remainder 181 \div 10 = 8 \text{ remainder } 1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c2-822f-c126f6ed2a2a" class="">81÷10=8 remainder 1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8046-9d36-c374b69d000a" class="">So the theorem is verified.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-806d-88ef-e9ac5f4591e7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8042-a519-f50bd79dda23" class="">5. Relationship Between Fermat’s and Euler’s Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8089-9ddb-c064ba486ae4" class="">Fermat’s theorem is actually a <strong>special case of Euler’s theorem</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ae-a6eb-e298e03d2c82" class="">If <strong>n is a prime number p</strong>, then:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b0-90ce-f3e5b9cedd5a" class="">ϕ(p)=p−1\phi(p) = p - 1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-ae8b-d4bf1594b720" class="">ϕ(p)=p−1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ac-952c-f55d6f8f3b06" class="">Therefore Euler’s theorem becomes:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8094-8411-dc7f1d89be6d" class="">ap−1≡1 (mod p)a^{p-1} \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod } p)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8075-a58a-f637635d5991" class="">ap−1≡1 (mod p)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806f-a730-da35cfb53261" class="">Which is exactly Fermat’s Little Theorem.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8078-97ab-ec5a3bc309f2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8087-b76c-fad93c38a803" class="">6. Applications in Cryptography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-9259-fa296fb3f5ca" class="">Both theorems are used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805c-8a79-cf80231edc29" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>RSA cryptosystem</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807d-9b2a-d10936e75498" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Modular exponentiation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8073-b2cc-d667794ce53f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Key generation in public key cryptography</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ae-b745-d36db097d41a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure communication protocols</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803b-b571-c53e1d06c2d2" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8061-bfbc-e804d74755a1" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT)</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80ad-bcac-c92d727dbf4e"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a9-8a78-eaf685846f66" class="">Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-802b-9033-cfa53fe0ad43" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-b48b-efd183b918ec" class="">The <strong>Chinese Remainder Theorem</strong> is an important concept in number theory that helps solve a system of <strong>simultaneous congruences with different moduli</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8007-83db-ecb30b7fc7d0" class="">It was first described by the ancient Chinese mathematician <strong>Sun Tzu</strong> around the 3rd century.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8062-9de2-cb672072dd28" class="">The theorem states that if several integers are divided by different pairwise coprime numbers, then there exists a <strong>unique solution modulo the product of those numbers</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8008-9f08-d656b65684e9" class="">CRT is widely used in <strong>cryptography, computer science, and modular arithmetic calculations</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80aa-941b-ec58cec0d130"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8078-aac5-d4470ea4b5da" class="">2. Statement of Chinese Remainder Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8022-a637-deef5aa48bb3" class="">Suppose we have a system of congruences:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805a-a02a-f4b10e412abb" class="">x≡a1 (mod n1)x ≡ a_1 \ (\text{mod } n_1)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ba-a6aa-e272771c62b0" class="">x≡a1 (mod n1)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8018-9a20-d569c3555396" class="">x≡a2 (mod n2)x ≡ a_2 \ (\text{mod } n_2)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d8-9fbb-ce42c2cb56d1" class="">x≡a2 (mod n2)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808c-8c1b-f8751dea1e75" class="">x≡a3 (mod n3)x ≡ a_3 \ (\text{mod } n_3)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800a-a4f1-fd0fcac773fd" class="">x≡a3 (mod n3)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806a-a428-ca9815acc980" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b1-b878-c88dbb7cff92" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">n1,n2,n3n_1, n_2, n_3n1,n2,n3 are <strong>pairwise coprime numbers</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-808a-a027-febba6d6cf7d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">a1,a2,a3a_1, a_2, a_3a1,a2,a3 are the remainders</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800e-afd7-c5c8d135c83f" class="">Then there exists a <strong>unique solution for x modulo</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801d-853f-d5d654e2d943" class="">N=n1×n2×n3N = n_1 \times n_2 \times n_3</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c3-855e-e49e0e5743fa" class="">N=n1×n2×n3</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8078-98ac-fea4cc805822"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80c7-90fb-c0844270d9d7" class="">3. Conditions for CRT</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8053-83f4-ca03f0339523" class="">For the Chinese Remainder Theorem to work:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8090-bf21-fd334057cf82" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>All moduli must be <strong>pairwise coprime</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-b8ab-d51bd5a6afc1" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Each congruence must be independent</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8067-8a59-ce78cb8b42ee" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The solution will be <strong>unique modulo the product of moduli</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-807b-b84a-dc25baea0abe"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80ba-af38-fb7dc5730c42" class="">4. Example of Chinese Remainder Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8071-9124-c00e1aeb10c4" class="">Solve the system:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c5-9e0e-d646c2cc4140" class="">x≡2 (mod 3)x ≡ 2 \ (\text{mod } 3)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803b-a762-f0e46b2c7216" class="">x≡2 (mod 3)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fb-8f42-db3b241e529c" class="">x≡3 (mod 5)x ≡ 3 \ (\text{mod } 5)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d8-9ef7-f465be25f35a" class="">x≡3 (mod 5)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8013-bdcd-fd733d6a244c" class="">x≡2 (mod 7)x ≡ 2 \ (\text{mod } 7)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8040-abb8-c8576e61e206" class="">x≡2 (mod 7)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-809b-9162-e4eb697c8816" class="">Step 1: Calculate product of moduli</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8031-a91f-f752344fecbd" class="">N=3×5×7=105N = 3 \times 5 \times 7 = 105</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8075-8abc-dbc078bfb31f" class="">N=3×5×7=105</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-85f4-e9c95b77bb97"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-bfff-e14d92cfa94e" class="">Step 2: Calculate partial products</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c1-a189-d7b2a6072f08" class="">N1=105/3=35N_1 = 105/3 = 35</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802b-a414-c57052d855d9" class="">N1=105/3=35</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800e-a4db-e4edc6293a72" class="">N2=105/5=21N_2 = 105/5 = 21</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f3-aabf-e2a60b40acbb" class="">N2=105/5=21</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8017-aad9-d24a61130688" class="">N3=105/7=15N_3 = 105/7 = 15</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-a80e-c4ee99fe5981" class="">N3=105/7=15</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-bc6e-f4e3aa24b73d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8000-a051-fe98ebf7358d" class="">Step 3: Find multiplicative inverses</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8005-8d45-f5036dbc52bc" class="">Find numbers M1,M2,M3M_1, M_2, M_3M1,M2,M3 such that:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-a984-d282dd5fc5e0" class="">35M1≡1 (mod 3)35M_1 ≡ 1 \ (\text{mod } 3)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-a880-fba6fa580db5" class="">35M1≡1 (mod 3)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805b-806b-ce80c1d9ee3c" class="">21M2≡1 (mod 5)21M_2 ≡ 1 \ (\text{mod } 5)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8076-abf5-c22cef2f86d0" class="">21M2≡1 (mod 5)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80df-89b3-e1c12fde7eb3" class="">15M3≡1 (mod 7)15M_3 ≡ 1 \ (\text{mod } 7)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d9-b927-f90820500586" class="">15M3≡1 (mod 7)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8051-a28a-f3efeb9a07f0" class="">Solutions:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80a3-83ff-daf3d6702633" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">M1=2M_1 = 2M1=2</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8022-a8fa-c4857df58b1b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">M2=1M_2 = 1M2=1</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80a3-b1aa-c5a1ec3053ea" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">M3=1M_3 = 1M3=1</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8070-94bb-f2f41f8dc618"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8002-a672-f2bf1d5b2058" class="">Step 4: Compute the result</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8012-81b2-d628576b843d" class="">x=(a1N1M1+a2N2M2+a3N3M3) mod Nx = (a_1N_1M_1 + a_2N_2M_2 + a_3N_3M_3) \ mod\ N</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ed-9d71-d9b8692d3101" class="">x=(a1N1M1+a2N2M2+a3N3M3) mod N</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8078-8b2c-e545dc7362be" class="">x=(2×35×2+3×21×1+2×15×1)x = (2×35×2 + 3×21×1 + 2×15×1)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d1-ac37-e29bed9b2eb4" class="">x=(2×35×2+3×21×1+2×15×1)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8068-9c8a-c1db5ce4e6ff" class="">x=(140+63+30)x = (140 + 63 + 30)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80aa-8748-de2cac7c4ef6" class="">x=(140+63+30)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e0-a666-f56712fbb044" class="">x=233x = 233</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b1-ade5-e6c20df4913a" class="">x=233</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d4-af9f-cd7c696970e4" class="">Now:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8076-a25a-d714ac90d950" class="">x=233 mod 105x = 233 \ mod\ 105</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a0-b54b-ffc9a1ca05f1" class="">x=233 mod 105</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8015-ba41-c55616667155" class="">x=23x = 23</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80dd-a9e0-e25cd9063ddb" class="">x=23</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8014-9a35-cd6eeb2ed779" class="">Final Answer</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807b-87c3-dc0779214699" class="">x=23x = 23</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801b-a8a6-e6a224e3ca09" class="">x=23</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-ba64-f705b2ca592d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-b133-d1f540fbc323" class="">5. Applications of Chinese Remainder Theorem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-b002-d6b4ecf091f4" class="">CRT is used in many areas such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8068-8bdd-fa1ff7cfa072" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Cryptography algorithms like <strong>RSA cryptosystem</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8085-8ced-d3d3fc1b64b5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Fast modular arithmetic computations</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-956e-f14b7cb53fea" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Computer algorithms and distributed systems</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80be-9551-ecda2a0ae49c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Error detection and correction</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8036-96ff-d54654fb9b40" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Cryptographic key generation</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-808d-89d4-d948ed46d319"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8080-90b8-e8b5937d795b" class="">6. Advantages of CRT</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8056-8ced-e5eb39c5b91f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simplifies complex modular arithmetic problems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-808b-b32f-d160db1a0c4d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>efficient computation of large numbers</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-802d-9c7e-d8f5d56afa74" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Useful in cryptography and algorithm design</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8038-a295-f376a1d0a1fa"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8058-9f3a-e2e3e691d8f9" class="">7. Conclusion</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-a3ae-c2ca5caf7e5e" class="">The Chinese Remainder Theorem is a powerful mathematical tool used to solve systems of congruences with different moduli. It guarantees a unique solution when the moduli are pairwise coprime. Due to its efficiency and simplicity, CRT plays an important role in cryptography, computer science, and number theory.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8093-9cae-e16b054561cb" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80f0-be92-cc15a4eb6abb" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Principles of Public Key Cryptosystems</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8093-af5e-e4747eddfee5"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80f5-bfcf-eb1dd9637952" class="">Principles of Public Key Cryptosystems</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8047-bf1f-c95fa12e227b" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e3-8f00-f9a3e43d2839" class="">A <strong>Public Key Cryptography</strong> system is a type of cryptographic technique that uses <strong>two different keys</strong> for encryption and decryption.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d9-954e-d78c9f326f97" class="">Unlike symmetric encryption, where the same key is used for both operations, public key cryptography uses:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8073-b81f-f441245b169b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Public Key</strong> – available to everyone</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8017-b476-f4c8ff7d014d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Private Key</strong> – kept secret by the owner</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8047-bd06-c848f7a1d6f8" class="">This concept was introduced by <strong>Whitfield Diffie</strong> and <strong>Martin Hellman</strong> in 1976.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80df-a599-e179ad7a4283" class="">Public key cryptography forms the foundation of many modern security systems such as secure web communication and digital signatures.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80fc-83f0-f4e760422e0c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-802b-a9eb-cbec251f74fa" class="">2. Basic Concept of Public Key Cryptosystem</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8035-bfb7-d2a8cc745a4e" class="">In a public key system:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8080-a867-d8d773138e05" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Each user generates <strong>two keys</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-802d-a9af-e821949232c1" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The <strong>public key</strong> is shared with others.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-807b-86ac-fe0892d75afc" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The <strong>private key</strong> is kept secret.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8004-90a9-dfb224480674" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Data encrypted with the public key can only be decrypted using the private key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8053-8fd8-e5c4a8da3f05"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8048-b89f-e786896151a6" class="">Basic Working Structure</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="321f2fef-a309-8052-b610-c66f8f1f329f" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Sender
   |
Encrypt with Receiver Public Key
   |
Ciphertext
   |
Receiver
   |
Decrypt with Private Key
   |
Plaintext</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a9-938e-ecfe3bb07283"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-808d-93b1-d922f4b8ce22" class="">3. Principles (Requirements) of Public Key Cryptosystems</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8068-adb9-f086082e5fc8" class="">For a public key cryptosystem to be secure and practical, the following principles must be satisfied:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-86a4-e644824f9b52"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-ade5-f1a04e761414" class="">1. Key Pair Generation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a9-a8d9-c6a0f91a8855" class="">Each user must be able to <strong>generate a pair of keys</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-84ee-f36a66c92463" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Public key</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-804f-91d3-deb32a6daf5e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Private key</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80db-8f49-c5fcae51b369" class="">The two keys are mathematically related but difficult to derive from each other.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-826c-dafc92be668b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-803e-936f-f81a75eda49e" class="">2. Easy Encryption and Decryption</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8049-afe4-f0491d7aa747" class="">The encryption and decryption processes must be <strong>computationally efficient</strong> so that they can be performed quickly by computers.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-bdba-d99b8a104a7b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80b7-bc5d-e1e14b297ae3" class="">3. Security of Private Key</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8004-8734-ee603e415689" class="">It must be <strong>computationally infeasible</strong> to determine the private key from the public key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c3-bf8b-d72e1b4ea340" class="">This ensures the system remains secure even if the public key is known.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-800f-83ea-e2ac895b5987"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8076-b947-fc42c316f793" class="">4. Difficulty of Plaintext Recovery</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8061-ac0f-db19442ae504" class="">An attacker should not be able to determine the plaintext message even if they know:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8051-8416-f23d59f94ce2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">the public key</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80cb-a905-ef7c93748a26" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">the ciphertext</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-802d-911f-d0c45c362ebe"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8002-81a0-fc88145b5712" class="">5. Mathematical Relationship Between Keys</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f7-867f-e69a4d322280" class="">The public and private keys must be <strong>mathematically related</strong>, allowing one key to reverse the operation performed by the other.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8030-937e-c8e9ba3c89ad" class="">For example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807f-b623-d0744dc2d6c4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Public key → encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8032-b68d-d6fe1b25f1d9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Private key → decryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80d6-9b5c-fbb049ddda1f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8024-b81b-e74a42463ba1" class="">6. Either Key Can Be Used First</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ca-864b-de018296bbfd" class="">A secure public key system should allow either key to be used first.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80bb-bdca-f04e7dcb16f6" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-b1b2-f9dfd35d4a19" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encrypt with public key → decrypt with private key (confidentiality)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8063-93b6-e1d75a7b4b06" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encrypt with private key → decrypt with public key (digital signature)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-804b-a8f9-c3e29ce6eded"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80a0-a52f-fac833b37f32" class="">4. Characteristics of Public Key Cryptography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8045-866a-eff459bacfda" class="">Important characteristics include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-86d5-f3d9928ab8b7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Uses <strong>two different keys</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8023-9929-de57010502f5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Supports <strong>secure key distribution</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80ff-9b23-d3019b277208" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Enables <strong>digital signatures</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-809e-a62f-e74acfbc1443" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>authentication and confidentiality</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-9aa9-d8930b1d8dcb"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-809e-b92d-db40ea8bced0" class="">5. Applications of Public Key Cryptosystems</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8012-9b5e-de0d026d5fad" class="">Public key cryptography is widely used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8094-a135-f97934fa52b4" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Secure web communication (HTTPS)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-b443-cf4f9454c084" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Email encryption</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8096-8b18-cfe731092f36" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Digital signatures</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8015-b5f7-ce881b2709a7" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Secure key exchange</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8010-9a67-c999db0c2c03" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Blockchain and digital currency systems</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e0-87b0-fa2dc51d424f" class="">One widely used algorithm based on these principles is the <strong>RSA cryptosystem</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8040-b93b-f8d6f25d17b1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80da-a81d-f2b0dcfbbbbd" class="">6. Advantages of Public Key Cryptography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-b778-c9fb6a57c6b3" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Solves the <strong>key distribution problem</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80d0-abd3-ebe09a3576ee" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Provides <strong>strong security</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f1-8679-f4392233fa3b" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Enables <strong>digital signatures</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80c7-aaab-c296dc65de0a" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Allows secure communication over public networks</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8098-843b-cd3b12a0ef55"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80fb-841f-d5ef236e89b1" class="">7. Limitations of Public Key Cryptography</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f2-ae5f-cb2ee77e5f49" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Slower than symmetric encryption</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f0-a312-e109cd95d47b" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Requires complex mathematical operations</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8093-ab57-c52031ee34bd" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Needs secure management of private keys</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8093-b6ec-fbf572962518"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8018-b40f-c91391ebb394" class="">8. Conclusion</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-a4a2-c52d0393dd36" class="">Public key cryptosystems are essential for modern digital security. By using a pair of mathematically related keys, they provide confidentiality, authentication, and secure communication over open networks. These principles form the foundation for many modern cryptographic protocols and secure internet technologies.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ae-a735-d86ea28e76f9" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-806a-99a8-e2b94e7df03e" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">RSA Algorithm</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-8015-b3b8-e7cf5ac74209"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-83f5-c86e51c11217" class="">RSA Algorithm</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80e6-b12d-c5c23aa551ab" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-91c3-faa53ca827c7" class="">The RSA algorithm is one of the most widely used <strong>public-key cryptographic algorithms</strong> used for secure data transmission. It was developed in 1977 by three researchers:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-b7e2-cf5933c8df84" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Ron Rivest</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8094-9894-f69a35bd0c1a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Adi Shamir</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8043-973d-ffb94ef349c8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Leonard Adleman</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c8-841f-d9fcf319e2be" class="">The name <strong>RSA</strong> comes from the <strong>first letters of their surnames</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80cd-9dbd-f2ded9104c18" class="">RSA is based on the <strong>mathematical difficulty of factoring large prime numbers</strong>, which makes it secure.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8002-8807-c884c61ee508"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8015-be54-ea99269e5a83" class="">2. Basic Idea of RSA</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8093-8ce0-e6e9931c6506" class="">RSA uses <strong>two different keys</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8092-9942-c3e59fd2927c" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Public Key</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8089-a3d7-c8f7ee8967d0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Used for encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8028-8cb9-cc09b809a80b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Shared with everyone</li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-805c-8574-d6da46d16fef" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Private Key</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-80a1-fcc21d3100ef" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Used for decryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8035-b742-ceea439b7201" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Kept secret by the owner</li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-ae5d-e62034a58cec"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8065-97ca-fa1794cc8cfb" class="">3. Steps in RSA Algorithm</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8027-b490-d7bc18bbd62d" class="">The RSA algorithm consists of three main stages:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8009-afa6-fd36f86e05b3" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Key Generation</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-8919-d8afb9fc834b" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Encryption</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80fe-8203-fc6a7da3ad2a" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Decryption</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8018-9eaa-d5a87a6358bb"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8016-a32a-d2a8ced46b4e" class="">4. RSA Key Generation</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8098-acbb-d330777505f2" class="">Step 1: Choose two large prime numbers</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d9-a613-cb01458e241d" class="">pandqp \quad and \quad q</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b4-9f49-db52ff889f2c" class="">pandq</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8079-b5e1-cd8559289b67" class="">Step 2: Compute</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803e-957e-e1973ebd6281" class="">n=p×qn = p \times q</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8042-8132-c822d40ef1ea" class="">n=p×q</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801a-b6f5-ca606511b2f9" class="">Step 3: Compute Euler’s totient function</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8085-9304-e2b820a07584" class="">ϕ(n)=(p−1)(q−1)\phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-a13d-cc3218471514" class="">ϕ(n)=(p−1)(q−1)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-804a-b5a9-e06ebc4856be" class="">Step 4: Choose a number <strong>e</strong> such that</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8079-9d71-d4969530a601" class="">1&lt;e&lt;ϕ(n)1 &lt; e &lt; \phi(n)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d5-af62-cdd58c8e893b" class="">1&lt;e&lt;ϕ(n)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803e-a68c-fbb4ae15b29a" class="">and</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-807d-a4a8-e3fc7999f77d" class="">gcd(e,ϕ(n))=1gcd(e,\phi(n)) = 1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809a-b9a8-f13e3b1dcbf8" class="">gcd(e,ϕ(n))=1</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808a-9f2d-fc76aecd1e35" class="">Step 5: Compute <strong>d</strong> such that</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-8e1b-cb14bc9f6bb9" class="">d×e≡1 (mod ϕ(n))d \times e \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod } \phi(n))</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8008-bdc9-cc66bef3b0e1" class="">d×e≡1 (mod ϕ(n))</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-804a-b55f-df199d901b7b" class="">Now the keys are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80d0-aa33-d5402c2f9e1d" class="">Public Key = <strong>(e , n)</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8006-8ea7-d844ac3f348e" class="">Private Key = <strong>(d , n)</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-809e-bc61-fcefc612e535"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-a860-cd5bdc0e8b22" class="">5. RSA Encryption</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b6-8be6-d2601a88e11a" class="">To encrypt a message <strong>M</strong>, the sender uses the <strong>receiver’s public key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-a565-c449f86b396a" class="">Encryption formula:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8006-8ceb-c88aebd3a597" class="">C=Me mod nC = M^{e} \ mod\ n</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f2-80cf-d5b3aa268a90" class="">C=Me mod n</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800c-9867-c1fc08193c21" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8071-b3e0-f9d1bc9baa1c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = plaintext</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80b5-9816-d869eeb37d8b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>e</strong> = public key exponent</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8020-bfc7-c4dc358ba25f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>n</strong> = modulus</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8062-b33b-cc28e25ee65b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>C</strong> = ciphertext</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8056-8c23-db3fcef1ac1a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80af-a561-e0b03952c47e" class="">6. RSA Decryption</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800f-b276-e9312512e5f9" class="">To recover the original message, the receiver uses the <strong>private key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802d-aea9-d43cefbbadfb" class="">Decryption formula:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806b-ae0e-dbc8fd76fd24" class="">M=Cd mod nM = C^{d} \ mod\ n</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8028-8f6f-f94da01a2d4c" class="">M=Cd mod n</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8013-b052-c938974ab8e5" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8061-8ae2-ebba84b86ba9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>C</strong> = ciphertext</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8076-8d69-c046b4b87df9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>d</strong> = private key</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8094-944c-d53b787674d5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = original message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8044-a93f-fc8c242e72ab"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8042-b703-da110c1b7ece" class="">7. Example of RSA Algorithm</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-a049-edbec19273b3" class="">Step 1: Choose primes</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8008-8e68-cb998680725c" class="">p=3,q=11p = 3, \quad q = 11</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8082-97e2-e37e5721b32b" class="">p=3,q=11</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801e-a8d5-d3c1f9102565" class="">Step 2: Compute</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-9c6c-d6a3803062b5" class="">n=3×11=33n = 3 \times 11 = 33</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8090-977c-cee4296fdae9" class="">n=3×11=33</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8002-becd-c639182ac0c7" class="">Step 3: Compute</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802c-831c-cf052f151264" class="">ϕ(n)=(3−1)(11−1)=20\phi(n) = (3-1)(11-1) = 20</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80da-81b7-c2ae3c17864c" class="">ϕ(n)=(3−1)(11−1)=20</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800d-bfa2-c80132d6db0d" class="">Step 4: Choose</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8007-8140-ca152c2a28b9" class="">e=3e = 3</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c6-b6a6-e106e09e1726" class="">e=3</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802b-9d6d-faf9bbee2079" class="">Step 5: Compute <strong>d</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e6-a034-c7c5380b0dcb" class="">d=7d = 7</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-98a5-d081a95ce49c" class="">d=7</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8045-81f6-ee87a7254413" class="">Keys:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-808f-888b-e0ddcacf3949" class="">Public key = <strong>(3, 33)</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8072-996a-d4d56e7d44cd" class="">Private key = <strong>(7, 33)</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8095-8f02-f520309bebd4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80c0-a307-dfc6d5d88a3f" class="">Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e3-9fb1-de0d8bfa1fa9" class="">Plaintext:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8013-b349-fdd0ae355eee" class="">M=4M = 4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8014-9405-e614af7cce88" class="">M=4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f8-8b3d-fea59cb7c0be" class="">C=43 mod 33C = 4^{3} \ mod\ 33</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8076-a6aa-c550e8bafe66" class="">C=43 mod 33</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809e-8b92-cd8703976dfe" class="">C=64 mod 33=31C = 64 \ mod\ 33 = 31</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ed-99c4-c066f352b88c" class="">C=64 mod 33=31</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8079-abe0-edf4cc5ef6e0" class="">Ciphertext = <strong>31</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-805e-bc50-fdd13d8e4af5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-8cf7-dd32eaf93e03" class="">Decryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c2-be57-d7f26593b3da" class="">M=317 mod 33M = 31^{7} \ mod\ 33</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8083-85c3-e51b57ee8c3c" class="">M=317 mod 33</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805f-bd48-d3fb8825b207" class="">Result:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801e-85f6-faef9d823c08" class="">M=4M = 4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8021-9228-d9c865e17766" class="">M=4</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a1-b4f6-c30f0639bef6" class="">Original message is recovered.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8060-9a20-f3795c00377a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8080-8fed-e0007ca284be" class="">8. Applications of RSA</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80ce-853e-c1fa71fcc78d" class="">RSA is used in many security systems:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80de-a998-d24841ea61b7" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Secure web communication (HTTPS)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8051-8ba6-f58da5355c7b" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Digital signatures</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8030-b987-c98b578fb90f" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Email encryption</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8044-a5f3-de8b7a97f244" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Secure key exchange</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-809b-9dc6-e891ccc62334" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Online banking systems</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-805e-8e80-e8f6eb2f082d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8040-9275-edcc8ff92e04" class="">9. Advantages of RSA</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8062-b9b4-ff1c27e30ecc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>strong security</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-801f-8687-f1566927bce8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Supports <strong>digital signatures</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-807c-8bf7-e43b1ac860ca" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Solves <strong>key distribution problem</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-9f93-c487d9b158bf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Widely accepted and standardized</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a4-baa8-c3697955ab58"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80f9-9cce-f4d7e34a6e2b" class="">10. Limitations of RSA</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8030-a985-ea4871101f19" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Slower compared to symmetric encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-b820-ff091ed1d70f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires large computational power</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8029-8114-e041be1942d9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Vulnerable if small keys are used</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8080-a218-e45f1d9cdb4d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-803b-8900-eeb108928543" class="">11. Conclusion</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e1-a95c-f0c2a7dde5c9" class="">RSA is one of the most important public-key cryptographic algorithms used for secure communication. By using two mathematically related keys, it ensures confidentiality and authentication. Due to its strong security and reliability, RSA is widely used in modern digital communication systems.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8023-867b-fc98a850aabc" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-80e6-be5e-c7578a8ebf44" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Security of RSA Algorithm</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="321f2fef-a309-80ea-90ed-eba07968401d"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8036-ae60-d93d95fc7218" class="">Security of RSA Algorithm</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80ad-8ac3-fd29d39e92e2" class="">1. Introduction</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80eb-9c44-fd9ee3147340" class="">The security of the <strong>RSA cryptosystem</strong> is based on the <strong>computational difficulty of factoring very large numbers</strong>, specifically the product of two large prime numbers.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-805d-ab76-e13bf9ae1452" class="">Although the <strong>public key (e, n)</strong> is known to everyone, the <strong>private key (d)</strong> remains secure because it is extremely difficult to derive it from the public key without knowing the original prime numbers used to generate <strong>n</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80bf-b1bc-c825956ed8e0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8090-a74f-e7f4e7c33c49" class="">2. Basis of RSA Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80c3-8853-d830658ac14d" class="">The security of RSA mainly depends on the following mathematical problem:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="321f2fef-a309-8092-8cbe-e9baecf18244" class="">Integer Factorization Problem</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-802c-80d2-f4be5cb112e0" class="">If:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-803c-b8f8-c783bf1e86d1" class="">n=p×qn = p \times q</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a8-b23d-f6bc5a65a28f" class="">n=p×q</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-809e-9eb5-c0c670fdab5c" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-804f-9cf2-d452f379b0a5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>p</strong> and <strong>q</strong> are very large prime numbers.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8028-b493-f1a8b3aa34a1" class="">If an attacker wants to break RSA, they must <strong>factor n into p and q</strong>. For very large numbers (such as 2048-bit keys), this task becomes <strong>computationally infeasible</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80c1-9539-efca9ce38556"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8095-a801-d5f5719c698a" class="">3. Factors Affecting RSA Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8045-9738-d47ddd50f344" class="">Several factors determine how secure RSA is.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8098-bf20-e4ee7530d843"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-bdb2-ff748696e02f" class="">1. Size of the Key</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80a6-bc6a-ce74ae20295b" class="">The <strong>larger the key size</strong>, the more secure the system.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b1-b3d1-ef693f5af56b" class="">Typical RSA key sizes:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="321f2fef-a309-80f4-8e01-d3b431a5ee41" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-809f-b5cc-ca9e7be1907a"><th id="s]pc" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Key Size</th><th id="RfCo" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Security Level</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8084-8981-e0b5c0ada869"><td id="s]pc" class="">1024 bits</td><td id="RfCo" class="">Weak today</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-80a3-ae92-dde8655fd39f"><td id="s]pc" class="">2048 bits</td><td id="RfCo" class="">Standard secure</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="321f2fef-a309-8017-b3a8-d8e82df4b4ad"><td id="s]pc" class="">4096 bits</td><td id="RfCo" class="">Very strong</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b2-baf2-f242d195eaee" class="">Large keys make <strong>factorization extremely difficult</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80b8-9694-d211262f9543"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8072-8811-f2ba9f725ec0" class="">2. Prime Number Selection</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8023-8df1-c2d7b9f85a41" class="">RSA security depends on choosing <strong>large and random prime numbers</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e2-9450-fb25b39a2fa3" class="">If the primes are small or poorly chosen, attackers may factor <strong>n</strong> easily.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8018-8854-d323f47b9306"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80c1-b9e9-c218c3e562d5" class="">3. Protection of Private Key</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80da-9b04-cfb827f340ef" class="">The private key must be kept secret.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-801b-901f-dd8807922b46" class="">If the private key is exposed, attackers can immediately decrypt messages.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-806b-bf6e-e68a8ce279d4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8025-b56c-f3ca644ffbb8" class="">4. Use of Secure Padding Schemes</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80dc-bd05-eb8ebe9762fd" class="">Modern implementations use <strong>padding techniques</strong> such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80e6-a48f-f0e15037fb2f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">OAEP (Optimal Asymmetric Encryption Padding)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b9-ac6c-d0cf633c8442" class="">Padding prevents attacks like <strong>chosen-plaintext attacks</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80a7-a920-ee603c99d523"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-808c-88c8-ea7334f3f3ca" class="">4. Possible Attacks on RSA</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8066-b200-dd6a5e107e86" class="">Even though RSA is secure, some attacks can occur if the system is poorly implemented.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-80e9-8e60-d0f06460b414"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8051-80a0-f299470b9826" class="">1. Brute Force Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f5-88ff-e6c2ec52e0f1" class="">Trying every possible key combination.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b7-9ae7-dc596b69c260" class="">This attack is impractical for large key sizes.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-801c-a367-cdcf6dee7d63"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-8029-b7ca-fff213f1ae39" class="">2. Mathematical Attacks</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8017-8922-e33f9a1b8403" class="">Attackers attempt to factor the modulus <strong>n</strong> using advanced algorithms.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80f2-844d-ebbd0613c84b" class="">Example methods:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-804e-b86f-ed9f65bc542a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Quadratic sieve</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80bf-865d-f9969dbe4553" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">General number field sieve</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8038-abf7-fd090ab91fe9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-80fd-a6a2-c075d218a9c2" class="">3. Timing Attacks</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8037-9d81-fe186656d6db" class="">Attackers analyze the <strong>time taken during decryption operations</strong> to guess the private key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8096-b960-f26e578c9313"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="321f2fef-a309-803a-8d9c-c8b65e95d388" class="">4. Chosen Ciphertext Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-800c-a86a-cb1e37fc58a8" class="">If padding is not implemented properly, attackers may manipulate ciphertext to reveal information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-801b-9c94-ee85e92d2c9b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8003-97f2-d9ded722ecd2" class="">5. Methods to Improve RSA Security</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-806f-b614-cf7b32818abc" class="">To make RSA more secure, the following precautions are used:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8079-ae5a-d053048ed473" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Use <strong>large key sizes (2048 bits or more)</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-805c-a12c-ff875bcb178c" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Choose <strong>strong random prime numbers</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8012-ba08-d65ef27e1f0d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Implement <strong>secure padding techniques</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8019-b8d2-dc87fae38bc3" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Protect private keys using secure storage</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-938c-e1d253b6d3c8" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Use <strong>hybrid encryption systems</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-803b-bbe9-c17ee2c2a2d8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8087-bffa-ef5537c30bce" class="">6. Applications of Secure RSA</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-8017-9b20-e1204f45214b" class="">Because of its strong security, RSA is used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8062-8e86-d1edb545d1b0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure web communication (HTTPS)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-805e-a1a0-f9c9efd43365" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Digital signatures</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80a7-a254-c92a9f41e321" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Email encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-8038-b9f3-e3d92aaf48bb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Online banking security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="321f2fef-a309-80f1-bd80-cc0cf98b6c85" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure key exchange protocols</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80b3-a305-fc4f075bd9b7" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="321f2fef-a309-8057-aa22-c277a1d945a2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80e4-8e07-cdc0ed42202d" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="321f2fef-a309-8026-87b8-f8db96f627e1" class=""><strong>Module 3 : Authentication Standards &amp; Key Management</strong></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="321f2fef-a309-80fd-ad6a-eddbc7dbd75f" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80ef-8596-f343706271d9" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Message Authentication Codes:</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80f4-bad4-d2b17be83aed" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Authentication requirements</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-805f-8a74-e8697f3b97b2" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">authentication functions</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-8059-8f55-d3092a54072b" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Hash Functions</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-802f-87ec-cf0080c60083" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80b1-b9b8-e6a815d2b42b" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Message Digest</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80d1-b495-e4a43bcf1ec7" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Digital Signatures: Digital Signatures</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-807d-977e-d86f447d8fc0" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Digital Signature Standards (DSS)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="321f2fef-a309-80fb-9485-d60979306494" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li> proof of Digital Signature Algorithm</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808a-9242-e037dad54d9c" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-802e-bd8d-e5717f5ccb8a" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Message Authentication Codes (MAC)</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-b734-ed328c48bb8b"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-be9d-c7a019289167" class="">Message Authentication Codes (MAC)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8026-baf5-d0279cc06c86" class=""><strong>Message Authentication Code (MAC)</strong> is a <strong>cryptographic technique</strong> used to ensure <strong>data integrity and authentication</strong> of a message. It allows the receiver to verify that the message has <strong>not been modified</strong> and that it was sent by someone who <strong>knows the secret key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8009-b110-e2d9f7578338"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8002-8007-c02d82f7b279" class="">1. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80be-b7c2-ebe284890d3f" class="">A <strong>Message Authentication Code (MAC)</strong> is a <strong>short piece of information (tag)</strong> generated from a <strong>message and a secret key</strong> using a cryptographic algorithm.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803f-b525-efad54818638" class="">The MAC is sent along with the message so that the receiver can verify the <strong>authenticity and integrity</strong> of the message.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8032-aef5-e376c34cbb3b" class=""><strong>Mathematical Representation</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805b-a5f1-ee964e1fddbc" class="">MAC = f(K, M)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8068-bed5-f7b5f5651c96" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8053-8f84-da588f53a75f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = Original message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8082-9466-e3b8c2cc2e29" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>K</strong> = Secret key shared between sender and receiver</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8082-8e40-c448767174a6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>f</strong> = MAC generation algorithm</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80cd-a2b8-c49e7e328dda" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>MAC</strong> = Authentication tag</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8091-8546-c3c6758e707b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8099-a1c5-feffec968c15" class="">2. Purpose of MAC</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801f-ae76-df488e9d894c" class="">MAC provides the following security services:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-807c-a207-feff63920a9f" class="">1. Data Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8025-b81a-d0d32d9d5897" class="">Ensures that the <strong>message is not altered during transmission</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-806b-b593-eea4e86f59ac" class="">2. Message Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-bbe3-c6422e9aefa3" class="">Confirms that the message comes from a <strong>legitimate sender</strong> who possesses the secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-b8e8-cadb2d60e80f" class="">3. Protection Against Tampering</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8088-9f22-d882b9632f2d" class="">If an attacker modifies the message, the <strong>MAC value will change</strong>, and the receiver will detect the alteration.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-b71d-d7567893c132"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8030-9a8f-e15647be91f9" class="">3. Working of Message Authentication Code</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8082-9857-febbc677c93e" class="">The MAC process involves <strong>three main steps</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-801b-bdb2-f7aca279836b" class="">Step 1: MAC Generation (Sender Side)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-800e-a7ca-e54e3d9dad64" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The sender takes the <strong>original message (M)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-b542-fc41c7d9b00a" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>A <strong>secret key (K)</strong> shared with the receiver is used.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8061-802f-ed3afc4bee5e" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>A MAC algorithm generates the <strong>authentication tag</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8008-b628-ecb86e16129d" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8092-a24d-fca43f2ade52" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">MAC = f(K, M)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-bc50-e7807a417b72" class="">The sender sends:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8008-b1c8-fc0112ee903f" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Message + MAC</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-9227-c44c416283ef"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-af0f-e7caee43677b" class="">Step 2: Transmission</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8030-a95d-e39a121b623b" class="">The message and MAC are transmitted through the communication channel.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-800c-9b03-f3d3c882acf5" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">[MESSAGE] + [MAC]</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-806f-a9ec-fce2576888a6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-805d-8a66-c4f1e9e56977" class="">Step 3: MAC Verification (Receiver Side)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e4-8bec-c7aed0bb6607" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The receiver receives the <strong>message and MAC</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-9fd4-f015dd824022" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The receiver uses the <strong>same secret key</strong> to generate a new MAC.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-806d-a136-c2ea2ed70386" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">MAC&#x27; = f(K, M)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-808a-8bf1-ecf30e8d709a" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The receiver compares:</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-852c-cf497e83ff4b" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">MAC&#x27;  =  Received MAC ?</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e6-a772-db58767a07db" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If <strong>both match → message is authentic</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c6-9047-c1d29099f83b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If <strong>different → message is modified</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-9bba-ccc9d1d580b7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8032-ad9b-c94b74e66b14" class="">4. Types of MAC Algorithms</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8040-967c-d764f8b0001c" class="">1. Hash-Based MAC (HMAC)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ee-9f71-e1b0459971d9" class="">Uses <strong>cryptographic hash functions</strong> such as <strong>SHA-256</strong> or <strong>SHA-1</strong> combined with a secret key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8032-ac96-c732fd55e0f3" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8059-b1b7-cfe96c865dac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">HMAC-SHA256</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b6-8436-d5fcd246b89e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">HMAC-SHA1</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8071-89d6-dcd327198f87" class="">Used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-9c10-c10f1fd01f19" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">HTTPS</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80eb-9233-f3be343a6530" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">API authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801c-9b4d-dbfc0c555b5a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure communication protocols</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8099-82ff-f0e94e9c0f8d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8049-b666-f529082e26e4" class="">2. Cipher-Based MAC (CMAC)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803d-b240-cc5de1145ae8" class="">Uses <strong>block cipher algorithms</strong> such as <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong> to generate MAC.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8050-b801-d698bde8ead0" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-bdd2-d9543cddb565" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">CMAC-AES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8060-b638-d19ec7a72551" class="">Used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-88aa-ccb4991c7997" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure communication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8070-abaa-c0447182bc47" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Military systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80db-b3ab-e49261455818" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Payment systems</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-b357-fe4fce5c43d6" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8053-8a44-d1e410e99b42" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Authentication Requirements</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80db-9d38-eb04f4294282"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8092-a294-d4c579ab18ac" class="">Authentication Requirements</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805d-bb86-ea07057fc0eb" class=""><strong>Authentication</strong> is the process of verifying the <strong>identity of a user, device, or message</strong> in a communication system. In network security, authentication ensures that the <strong>sender and receiver are legitimate</strong> and that the <strong>data has not been altered during transmission</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808a-9a07-e5b80d51a8dd" class="">To achieve secure communication, certain <strong>authentication requirements</strong> must be satisfied.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-935c-d5d26ad3aed4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-808a-9f61-d359728d46e4" class="">1. Message Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80cf-a1b6-fca0c4d9a517" class="">Message integrity ensures that the <strong>content of the message remains unchanged</strong> during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80cd-b127-cf4510d31fde" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If an attacker modifies the message, the receiver must be able to <strong>detect the alteration</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-92c5-de37257c0fcc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cryptographic techniques such as <strong>Message Authentication Code</strong> and hash functions like <strong>SHA-256</strong> are commonly used to ensure integrity.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8078-8b22-e51681f54914" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-a733-cdf601ec1e43" class="">If a bank transfer message “Transfer ₹5000” is altered to “Transfer ₹50000”, integrity mechanisms detect the modification.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802a-96f3-d16845db19a5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8039-a9ca-f00ac41f4fb1" class="">2. Message Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804b-947f-d8e89e773665" class="">Message authentication ensures that the <strong>message comes from the legitimate sender</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8091-aa40-f35e73e6096a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">The receiver must be able to verify the <strong>identity of the sender</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-bc07-d89d896e017b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">This is achieved using cryptographic mechanisms such as <strong>Digital Signature</strong> and <strong>Message Authentication Code</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-b115-d6fc0daccfac" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-90ce-ef6b8ca20c42" class="">When logging into a secure website, the system verifies that the request actually came from the authenticated user.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8062-9d0f-dc81043edfbc"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-b360-f66817a17508" class="">3. Message Confidentiality</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8082-833c-ca5875c0b44e" class="">Confidentiality ensures that <strong>only authorized parties can read the message</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d2-a679-e357bab8440b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Even if an attacker intercepts the message, they should <strong>not be able to understand it</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80eb-aee6-f1d3f3d9e6f7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption algorithms such as <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong> and <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong> are used to protect confidentiality.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a8-af56-c5a3b5d9b826" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807a-bae7-e8d0971a7ae2" class="">Online banking systems encrypt transaction data so that hackers cannot read it.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-809e-af42-f516f001ea8b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-baa0-c0e939119772" class="">4. Non-Repudiation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806f-9a12-f51542f9e6fa" class="">Non-repudiation ensures that the <strong>sender cannot deny sending the message</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-989b-f78d9024fcd6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">It provides <strong>proof of origin</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80de-8a21-dde4439c5f3c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Usually implemented using <strong>Digital Signature</strong> and public-key cryptography such as the <strong>RSA Algorithm</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8008-9480-d5123e17415b" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803e-a021-ec56b7dd429b" class="">In e-commerce transactions, digital signatures ensure that the sender cannot later deny the transaction.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80fa-aa78-c8a73d1ea59a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8038-bf97-d7206ab60658" class="">5. Freshness of Messages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802b-aaf3-e08ce00166f6" class="">Freshness ensures that the message is <strong>recent and not a replay of an old message</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8054-a585-f72a03c94dd0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Prevents <strong>replay attacks</strong>, where attackers resend previously captured messages.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-84e1-fc288bb19061" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Achieved using <strong>timestamps, sequence numbers, or random numbers (nonces).</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c0-9f62-c84c864c78a9" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8070-a6db-ccd523728c37" class="">Authentication protocols in secure communication systems check timestamps to ensure that the request is new.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806a-88f6-f2a72f2eb91f" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8084-bf86-d781b5358087" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Authentication Functions</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-9323-c28209f17303"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8021-adbc-df0325312a66" class="">Authentication Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-bd67-ec16935bfaf7" class=""><strong>Authentication functions</strong> are cryptographic methods used to <strong>verify the identity of a sender and ensure the integrity of a message</strong> during communication. They help detect whether a message has been <strong>altered, forged, or sent by an unauthorized user</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8026-9793-f6adf2f816df" class="">Authentication functions play an important role in <strong>network security and cryptography</strong> to protect sensitive information during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-809c-a479-d9ee26d6cdd0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-802f-8623-c0d3ff07ef33" class="">Types of Authentication Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8027-848f-ef9ad1d8516e" class="">There are <strong>three main types of authentication functions</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c3-9cf3-c7ea2e05e046" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Message Encryption</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-807f-bde8-f926de287109" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Message Authentication Code (MAC)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-808d-97d5-d5a480b14228" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Hash Functions</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-a1a2-d3fdf7e2252e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-802b-b14a-d2150a2bbdf9" class="">1. Message Encryption</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8050-84f8-e355ba461448" class="">In this method, <strong>encryption itself provides authentication</strong>. The sender encrypts the message using a secret key, and the receiver decrypts it using the same key.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806a-a24b-d3057c70ad00" class="">If the receiver successfully decrypts the message, it confirms that the message was sent by the <strong>authorized sender</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805c-a975-ea439b9cd9d5" class="">Example encryption algorithms include <strong>Advanced Encryption Standard</strong> and <strong>Data Encryption Standard</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-802d-9850-d9e269a87591" class="">Working</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b6-802d-e124b4209886" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Sender encrypts the message using a secret key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e6-8ded-d7105164701c" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The encrypted message is sent to the receiver.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8076-9665-f45f80eb0cd3" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The receiver decrypts the message using the same key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803a-8558-cfdcc9e07786" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>If decryption is successful, the message is considered authentic.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8027-899e-fecd57117647" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-807b-a9c3-e3287e302ffa" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>confidentiality and authentication together</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8090-b1c5-f532fa315d87" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b8-bc58-da0a59f79300" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption is <strong>computationally expensive</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805f-841b-efef2bc9c68f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Not always efficient when only authentication is needed.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d9-87d2-d303f9d0a892"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-809d-9396-fd1b588fd8da" class="">2. Message Authentication Code (MAC)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8069-bc92-c5a75d1a394c" class="">A <strong>Message Authentication Code</strong> is a short cryptographic value generated from a <strong>message and a secret key</strong>. The MAC is sent along with the message to verify authenticity and integrity.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ed-8aac-ef1a605a192e" class="">Mathematical Representation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80cf-b288-dab34f2207de" class="">MAC = f(K, M)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80de-86e9-c181decd8ad3" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-899f-ff4af0538100" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = Message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8082-a9d0-dda1521ec282" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>K</strong> = Secret key</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-807a-b9da-ede92e35710e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>f</strong> = Authentication function</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a0-9606-e26587207629" class="">Working</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80dd-9ffd-fc18e669e507" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Sender generates MAC using message and secret key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-801d-9790-dcb752250976" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Message and MAC are sent together.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803c-a6e3-d210ddd206dd" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Receiver generates MAC again using the same key.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8073-ba02-d7f8e76cf411" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>If both MAC values match, the message is authentic.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8077-a947-cb0910c0a853" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f6-aec4-e47602fad0d7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Faster than encryption.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8033-be61-ebe72a2523b2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>message integrity and authentication</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d5-8d06-f89332c67f9e" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80fe-ae09-c266eeae9370" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Does not provide <strong>non-repudiation</strong> because the secret key is shared.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8065-8f00-d2527221075f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80fa-a370-fed24c4423b8" class="">3. Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8029-87b8-f6fcee7633c5" class="">A <strong>cryptographic hash function</strong> generates a <strong>fixed-length value (hash)</strong> from a message of any size.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a3-913d-c08bccf52ad6" class="">Common examples include <strong>SHA-256</strong> and <strong>MD5</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f8-80d8-d6e4f7be6c09" class="">Working</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8071-ada1-e31cc98a55ae" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Sender computes the hash of the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8072-ab20-e579579e0c41" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The hash value is sent along with the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80db-ba2c-eb1ff4e9fd3c" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The receiver computes the hash again.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ee-b876-cb28a97c2837" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>If both hashes match, the message is authentic and unchanged.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8041-aa03-f0acba4ea0e4" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8047-ae7c-e3c14ef0854a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Very <strong>fast and efficient</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8057-b19c-ebf8dcdec000" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Ensures <strong>data integrity</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-9068-cb1c47893f85" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8033-adf7-edc46580b53d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">By itself, it <strong>does not authenticate the sender</strong> unless combined with a key (as in HMAC).</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-bf77-f0c323439a43" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8043-a259-dff93e3dfa70" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Hash Functions</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-802b-89b4-e72b3f24a165"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-a6b8-e6196c59adcd" class="">Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80dc-a3cf-e973c86943f9" class="">A <strong>hash function</strong> is a cryptographic algorithm that <strong>converts a message of any length into a fixed-length value</strong>, known as a <strong>hash value</strong>, <strong>message digest</strong>, or <strong>hash code</strong>. Hash functions are widely used in <strong>data integrity verification, digital signatures, and authentication systems</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803c-bdb6-eedb6c7ee694" class="">Common examples of hash functions include <strong>SHA-256</strong>, <strong>MD5</strong>, and <strong>SHA-1</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808d-a281-cf14f46f07e6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8084-8a95-d2859521c130" class="">Mathematical Representation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805c-a3e6-e37df1192824" class="">H = h(M)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-b1e4-de51c4f9ba1c" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a1-9dde-e844713f80e9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = Original message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d9-acda-d8868f17c2b5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>h</strong> = Hash function</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-8c71-eeca4e12090b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>H</strong> = Hash value (message digest)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-803d-9075-d88727f23cbd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80cf-ac2b-ce985d65196c" class="">Working of Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-bc1f-e57e05d2afd2" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The sender takes the <strong>original message</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8049-a4b5-ef872b706aa4" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>A <strong>hash function</strong> processes the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8027-8b66-ffe02ea5f384" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The function produces a <strong>fixed-length hash value</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-804a-b719-fdec3ecb6f99" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>The hash value is sent along with the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-845a-cf36ebc3190d" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>The receiver computes the hash again and compares it with the received hash.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8041-bc29-f2eb6d558750" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li>If both values match, the message is <strong>authentic and unchanged</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8027-b201-efdec1e8f633"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-805d-90f2-f2baa902df8f" class="">Characteristics (Properties) of Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ca-87aa-e6a09cbbf8f4" class="">1. Fixed-Length Output</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805e-97af-f30e84ef7e96" class="">Regardless of the input size, the hash function produces a <strong>fixed-length output</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801a-bd5c-e72596fe01b6" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8029-9d69-f064c23b251b" class=""><strong>SHA-256</strong> always produces a <strong>256-bit hash value</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-b71b-d60df3dff2c5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-806e-bd79-ff405616bd63" class="">2. Deterministic</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-bc38-ef6c2f5f7c32" class="">The same input message always produces the <strong>same hash output</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8014-8aec-cdb4a78193c8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8028-a739-d78b68561661" class="">3. Fast Computation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8010-a4f2-c250d2fcfa32" class="">Hash functions are designed to compute the hash value <strong>quickly and efficiently</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8033-a210-e181963a74e3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-98c3-f3a8f20cf0b1" class="">4. Preimage Resistance</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fb-b6da-e43020b55102" class="">It should be <strong>computationally infeasible</strong> to determine the original message from the hash value.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8055-a2ca-c43fc5605ca6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-800d-ba05-e06bb552c14b" class="">5. Collision Resistance</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c3-a2b4-f140711f7d7f" class="">It should be extremely difficult to find <strong>two different messages that produce the same hash value</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-8747-c8de68e93bb5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-a65b-e40e8be9783e" class="">6. Avalanche Effect</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-bcdf-e6419e8ac49d" class="">A <strong>small change in the input message</strong> should produce a <strong>completely different hash value</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-aea8-c90376e95e57"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8043-a173-c44679dc75b0" class="">Types of Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-805e-88fd-e1f9b6166806" class="">1. MD5 (Message Digest 5)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8080-9403-d02df0d1acec" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Produces a <strong>128-bit hash value</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801d-88b1-fe1c4729e4ff" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Faster but <strong>not considered secure today</strong> due to collision attacks.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809e-8138-f8359c425098" class="">Example: <strong>MD5</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-937c-eb9db2e9a563"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-808d-b56d-d32d5a2877d7" class="">2. SHA Family (Secure Hash Algorithm)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805d-8ba0-c6f3604ca04d" class="">Developed for secure hashing and widely used in modern cryptographic systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8040-bba2-d8adc29dd3b2" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80eb-9495-fa6dfe147024" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA-1</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8057-a7d3-dfb91ace3791" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA-256</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8089-aa8c-db4e52666fb4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80c8-9f34-e324bab7a522" class="">Applications of Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b5-ab5a-dfae590cda3d" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Data Integrity Verification</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8016-bc65-e6ee00639e90" class="">Ensures that data has not been modified.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8051-a1f3-dd68322f8d26" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Digital Signatures</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807d-9b86-e97e54c6a266" class="">Used with algorithms like <strong>RSA Algorithm</strong>.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-a2d0-c15f22172f05" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Password Storage</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8052-a33f-f5182b84b67c" class="">Systems store hashed passwords instead of plain text.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ce-abe6-c6dfe51732a8" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Message Authentication</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803f-9b05-fad092f0ad90" class="">Used in techniques such as <strong>Message Authentication Code</strong>.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-801d-b6ac-cf0fbc5d3518" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Blockchain Technology</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ed-b493-e482b7f99da6" class="">Used to secure transactions and blocks.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8009-98e9-ed087fe0c30c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8070-aa83-c042a130e6c5" class="">Advantages of Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801c-a399-ee30bef13ba7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Ensures <strong>data integrity</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-ab79-c620925effcc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Fast and efficient computation</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8003-9153-cb2b069f2c4c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Useful in <strong>digital signatures and authentication</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-806e-a065-d0440ba1952d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Fixed-size output simplifies storage and verification</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-801b-99f2-c301869b7052"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-a506-fa91b4d26df3" class="">Limitations of Hash Functions</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8002-ac45-e8800d4f2f22" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Vulnerable to <strong>collision attacks</strong> if poorly designed</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-800e-81a3-cdce3bf1aa4c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8025-b8f9-c922506e524c" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-800c-83d0-dfadfb6616a5" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA)</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-8034-8d83-f25bb660486d"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-bbde-c434facdd203" class="">Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-b6d2-e8e6dde05513" class="">The <strong>Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA)</strong> is a family of <strong>cryptographic hash functions</strong> used to ensure <strong>data integrity and security</strong> in digital communications. It converts an input message of any size into a <strong>fixed-length hash value (message digest)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8061-9206-f80448b502e6" class="">SHA algorithms are widely used in <strong>digital signatures, password hashing, blockchain, and secure network protocols</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-ba1b-f0b363f89572" class="">Common members of the SHA family include <strong>SHA-1</strong> and <strong>SHA-256</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ee-8e17-ed01dc8e7bf2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-800e-a024-c2fad32c8dc9" class="">Mathematical Representation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8036-8f3f-c9e4aad6cd33" class="">H = SHA(M)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8003-b472-d7711ada7da4" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803e-b0e7-ef9994ad39a0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = Input message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a5-b35e-f6f21bc1ab22" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA</strong> = Secure Hash Algorithm</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805e-bd2a-f565346b50e1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>H</strong> = Hash value (message digest)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802b-86ad-d5082d6447e3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80ea-b464-e28679dfc4f3" class="">Features of SHA</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8005-97bd-d44f868d5ad5" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Fixed-Length Output</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b0-b1ff-cd47f4802152" class="">SHA produces a fixed-length hash value regardless of the message size.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8044-a34f-f79d6620777e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Deterministic</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8058-822b-d0a6062784fd" class="">The same input message always generates the same hash output.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80df-8252-d21c45dff089" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>High Security</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802b-ab18-d72022135f64" class="">It is computationally difficult to find the original message from the hash.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d6-87e9-f8ce7ec10075" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Collision Resistance</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c0-80cb-cc3d6164ad36" class="">It is extremely difficult to find two different messages that produce the same hash value.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-804d-9fd5-f3a86f5b0e62" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Avalanche Effect</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e4-ba9a-cc5031f74e3c" class="">A small change in the input message produces a completely different hash value.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8068-90be-f42e184835f6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-b1ae-f35002cdfe7f" class="">SHA Family</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fb-ad87-e9f41720d3f8" class="">1. SHA-1</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804e-bddb-fa05c22a5110" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Produces a <strong>160-bit hash value</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-802a-a21c-e368d486a15b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Once widely used in digital signatures and SSL certificates.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-9a7d-d0535f9f9d79" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Now considered <strong>less secure due to collision attacks</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-800f-91b7-e8f3d5cc3e5e" class="">Example: <strong>SHA-1</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ce-a338-c4ef39437f1e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fd-b138-e2759eb88dfb" class="">2. SHA-2 Family</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-85a5-dd79daceb3f9" class="">The SHA-2 family includes stronger algorithms such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8066-af78-ddf005c34dae" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA-256</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8002-b437-e686ecb2dd59" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">SHA-384</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e2-a5d2-c9c46bb924be" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">SHA-512</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8002-b895-c3dfde1182ff" class="">These algorithms provide <strong>higher security and better resistance against attacks</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808b-b3b0-fd4b071cc2ac"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-8fc3-e692c9bb11bc" class="">Working of SHA (Basic Steps)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8040-b8a6-d9d8b62123bb" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Message Padding</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809b-8d21-ec7ab49741f3" class="">The original message is padded to make its length suitable for processing.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-800b-9cec-c0af591f9499" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Divide into Blocks</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80dc-aca4-d9b5353ef271" class="">The padded message is divided into fixed-size blocks.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-b3d8-cb3361ff3398" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Compression Function</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8083-8f83-fc7cbb1d234b" class="">Each block is processed through mathematical operations and logical functions.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-805f-89fe-f12e71f5a394" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Generate Hash Value</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806c-84cc-f27898181899" class="">After processing all blocks, the final <strong>hash value (message digest)</strong> is produced.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8013-9f79-ce79fed80df5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-8719-f6f3aaf8f76c" class="">Applications of SHA</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-804f-a132-c1f16955ba41" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Digital Signatures</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fb-88a8-ee0a3509f806" class="">Used with algorithms like <strong>RSA Algorithm</strong>.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8063-bce8-e590cbc5627d" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Password Security</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803b-b635-dd1811cd5401" class="">Passwords are stored as hash values instead of plain text.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8064-9d76-e562feec4318" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Data Integrity Verification</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b1-9648-e2ebc3323aae" class="">Ensures that files and messages are not altered.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8021-a630-ce3199460ab6" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Blockchain Technology</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8013-8259-f360ad4eb514" class="">Cryptocurrencies use hashing algorithms for secure transactions.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e7-a464-c400f49899a0" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Secure Communication Protocols</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8060-a9be-eb34c19dfe84" class="">Used in HTTPS and SSL/TLS security systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-b0ee-dfb8871b136b" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8015-916f-cffda1fd8f0d" class="">Advantages of SHA</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d5-9a6e-c140efa95ea6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>strong data integrity</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-8201-d0e7747a3d05" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Resistant to many cryptographic attacks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80aa-93ec-dd16865cbd7e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Generates <strong>unique and fixed-length hash values</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b6-8f51-f69907f3494b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Widely used in modern security systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8086-bc4f-c6da21926c18"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8020-9946-e09192dc8a63" class="">Limitations of SHA</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80cd-ba7a-c450033276ad" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Older versions like <strong>SHA-1</strong> are vulnerable to collision attacks.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809a-bb5c-c585bdbc3063" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires significant computational resources for large datasets.</li></ul></div></li></ol></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-a123-d63410fbe6e6" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80fe-9065-d455ca6f687e" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Message Digest</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-8086-b422-f2796791c818"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8082-9bfb-e30b46c11eb4" class="">Message Digest</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-8007-d8cad99dc85f" class="">A <strong>message digest</strong> is a <strong>fixed-length hash value</strong> generated from a message using a cryptographic hash function. It acts like a <strong>digital fingerprint of the message</strong> and is used to verify <strong>data integrity</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803f-a388-c40324aa59ee" class="">The message digest ensures that the <strong>message has not been modified during transmission</strong>. Even a small change in the message will produce a completely different digest value.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804f-a5af-efd107c2e6ed" class="">Common algorithms used to generate message digests include <strong>MD5</strong>, <strong>SHA-1</strong>, and <strong>SHA-256</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-805b-a38e-d2b03c826ab6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8031-a4b1-e7b512b14a53" class="">Mathematical Representation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-84ef-edf3448dbcce" class="">MD = H(M)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-b8ac-edd2ffcc76ff" class="">Where:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805a-b30b-ecff641b50ea" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>M</strong> = Original message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80aa-b515-ca842ecf9762" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>H</strong> = Hash function</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8079-a633-ee1faa282307" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>MD</strong> = Message digest</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-800b-a3c6-cf1043dbe05e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80f1-83d6-fbe010d18e08" class="">Working of Message Digest</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-8059-d136b51a733f" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The sender takes the <strong>original message</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e5-92b0-edb973b8fee0" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>A <strong>hash function</strong> processes the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-802d-9a80-d8d4501bc515" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The algorithm generates a <strong>message digest (hash value)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8050-b20f-cf588269c2a4" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>The message and digest are sent to the receiver.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80cb-8c22-f42f7e1195c7" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>The receiver applies the same hash function to the received message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-8235-ef4e97e867df" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li>The new digest is compared with the received digest.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8083-8d12-e05fbc1ed87e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If both digests match → <strong>Message is authentic and unchanged</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8014-adc0-f62ac4391a0d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If digests differ → <strong>Message has been modified</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a1-b220-e5ebc003697f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80c4-ac29-cbb8dc9bfa1d" class="">Characteristics of Message Digest</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8035-a75f-df3d1eafe25d" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Fixed Length Output</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809a-b502-e8496d6328a6" class="">The digest has a fixed size regardless of the message length.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8031-b012-f58b4cf09990" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Uniqueness</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fa-99b3-e250ebb6f69c" class="">Different messages produce different digest values.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8035-9908-c0c9d098388c" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Fast Computation</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8098-b377-ebc21d2d01be" class="">Digest generation is quick and efficient.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-b9f2-c7bc330d59d0" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Avalanche Effect</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805d-b105-df4bc147eebf" class="">A small change in the message causes a large change in the digest.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-808b-adcf-fef9f4d1313c" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>One-Way Function</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801d-8d4a-c707bf2a245d" class="">It is extremely difficult to reconstruct the original message from the digest.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802d-842e-c1f610cd18f4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8050-9152-ce00581aea21" class="">Applications of Message Digest</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8029-ae41-d0d1d1e18729" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Data Integrity Verification</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8001-95a3-d1f5e6728889" class="">Used to check whether data has been altered.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8022-8ebc-ea3822242fd7" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Digital Signatures</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f8-8259-fef37e249a64" class="">Works with algorithms like <strong>RSA Algorithm</strong> to sign documents.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-800f-906e-ce7bd7402a78" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Password Security</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802e-8056-c9a7163b7865" class="">Passwords are stored in hashed form instead of plain text.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b7-b2fc-cbbaa8168c70" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Secure File Transmission</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8011-ba3a-d9a176533ec7" class="">Ensures files are not corrupted or tampered with during transfer.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-801d-9066-c6ba7cd0ba59" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Blockchain Technology</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c4-866d-f8c0007e053d" class="">Used for secure block identification.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8051-95cc-f92e464512df"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8029-9f1a-e8eef4b92ed4" class="">Advantages of Message Digest</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e2-8748-c39b0384bc3a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Ensures <strong>data integrity</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-800a-ad02-ef39f2de32ee" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Fast and efficient computation</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801a-8c4c-e0dac9382fbe" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Produces <strong>unique fingerprints for messages</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a3-9d16-c7a993496240" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Widely used in modern security systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-a627-e226955db7f6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-8b04-ff3ebee4bbef" class="">Limitations of Message Digest</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809b-ba90-d04d7fadf7e8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cannot provide <strong>confidentiality</strong> by itself</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805d-892a-f472a7e4be44" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Vulnerable to <strong>collision attacks</strong> if weak hash algorithms are used</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80cb-a5c2-d717105eb1fc" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-804d-b722-f5d25cba9f58" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Digital Signatures</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-bb6d-d8da9074fa1f"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8018-8e4a-efac6f6251c4" class="">Digital Signatures</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803a-af89-e7ff2a2f0b2a" class="">A <strong>Digital Signature</strong> is a cryptographic technique used to <strong>verify the authenticity, integrity, and origin of a digital message or document</strong>. It is the electronic equivalent of a handwritten signature but provides <strong>much stronger security</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-aa33-d534aa9f6a85" class="">Digital signatures ensure that the message was <strong>sent by the claimed sender and has not been altered during transmission</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804f-8fbc-f3b3a4611a59" class="">They are widely used in <strong>secure communications, online banking, e-commerce, and software distribution</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8030-b525-f00ce096d4a4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8022-b9de-e80707d14289" class="">Objectives of Digital Signatures</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a1-a706-d23756953410" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Authentication</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801f-96f4-f4c031c39b4c" class="">Confirms the identity of the sender.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8013-9e2f-d7433ef5e76c" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Integrity</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-8364-d8861cbc16f8" class="">Ensures that the message has <strong>not been modified</strong>.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8057-8e93-c6d8ecfdfb49" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Non-repudiation</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8064-9052-ed32aaf4e46a" class="">Prevents the sender from <strong>denying sending the message</strong>.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-b804-d2354d68311f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8043-99cf-ce71451d8853" class="">Working of Digital Signatures</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8081-9d85-f3fbd918f3fe" class="">The digital signature process involves <strong>two main phases</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8080-bdb6-e8b840cbcf92" class="">1. Signature Creation (Sender Side)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803b-a068-e3494729ecdd" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The sender prepares the <strong>original message</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8093-beed-d8e44cd531f3" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>A <strong>hash function</strong> generates a <strong>message digest</strong> of the message using algorithms like <strong>SHA-256</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d1-ab5e-c7fbce8950f3" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The message digest is <strong>encrypted using the sender&#x27;s private key</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8010-973d-dfa537e34bbb" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>The encrypted digest becomes the <strong>digital signature</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802b-bf68-f9dd2c9178e0" class="">The sender sends:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8015-8590-e25fb45fce2d" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Message + Digital Signature</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8068-abc6-c24f74252954"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-9958-f2c0429e4600" class="">2. Signature Verification (Receiver Side)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b7-8090-e83ed7e0203d" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The receiver receives the <strong>message and digital signature</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ee-b0c5-e7ecc80b3287" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The receiver decrypts the signature using the <strong>sender’s public key</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8094-b9ef-fdc876141a5c" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The receiver also generates a new message digest using the same hash function.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8084-8123-fd4be8f98f57" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Both digest values are compared.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-808f-be18-db4b5d6500ff" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If they <strong>match → message is authentic and unchanged</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f8-802f-d561cba6afa7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If they <strong>do not match → message has been modified</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8085-9e33-d1d9420099fa" class="">Digital signatures commonly use public key algorithms such as the <strong>RSA Algorithm</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8028-9175-fa05c834f8d1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80eb-a48e-ec589e36ae06" class="">Digital Signature Process Representation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-9695-f2203aca3432" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Sender Side:
Message → Hash Function → Message Digest → Encrypt with Private Key → Digital Signature

Receiver Side:
Digital Signature → Decrypt with Public Key → Message Digest
Message → Hash Function → Message Digest
Compare both digests</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8088-be7a-ccf426f037db"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-806f-9e60-c13a20439e86" class="">Advantages of Digital Signatures</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-ad04-e2d3c7d28c00" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>strong authentication</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806e-a030-ebd8f934f96f" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Ensures <strong>message integrity</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f7-bdde-d7e86ea20d2d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Supports <strong>non-repudiation</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-a4ec-c355b56e9c15" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Enables <strong>secure electronic transactions</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802a-b85c-c5db084aea12"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8043-8aa7-fc8d69437297" class="">Applications of Digital Signatures</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8097-bd82-c27cb26e259a" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Secure Email Communication</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-802d-99ca-f3a391d49844" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Online Banking and Financial Transactions</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8066-ba8d-ea2da44e4302" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>E-commerce and Online Contracts</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-9fe0-c87fb5ec087b" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Software Distribution and Code Signing</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8000-8bdb-d901b179ad07" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Government and Legal Documents</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8028-bf72-d6fa41eafb97"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-800d-824a-f1dd79df6964" class="">Limitations of Digital Signatures</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-8832-d3414c0562dc" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Requires <strong>public key infrastructure (PKI)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8003-a18b-ecfb1b0ae1e9" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Implementation can be <strong>complex and costly</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80df-9df1-cfc52a8e0e77" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>If the <strong>private key is compromised</strong>, the signature becomes invalid.</li></ol></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803e-934e-dfb97fa6c0ab" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8090-95c2-de1744a1c6c5" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">Digital Signature Standard (DSS)</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-802a-82a6-d9569d9d4f72"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-803f-a08d-ddbb6defa970" class="">Digital Signature Standard (DSS)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8028-bee6-e71a1f389943" class="">The <strong>Digital Signature Standard (DSS)</strong> is a federal standard used for creating and verifying <strong>digital signatures</strong> to ensure <strong>authentication, integrity, and non-repudiation</strong> of digital messages and documents.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80dc-83fd-fdc5b56c0cc7" class="">It was published by the <strong>National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)</strong> and adopted by the U.S. government for secure digital communications.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8082-b0a8-ee391a9ad19d" class="">DSS specifies the <strong>algorithms and procedures</strong> required to generate and verify digital signatures.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8060-b989-cb47ea81f297"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-807a-80f2-ca86f845573a" class="">Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-be8e-e2f3d8fb3ec8" class="">The primary algorithm used in DSS is the <strong>Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-ae8c-d3d1b853e2a2" class="">DSA is based on mathematical concepts such as <strong>modular arithmetic and discrete logarithms</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802d-af75-ec7b833c5bc0" class="">It uses a <strong>pair of keys</strong>:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-a5dd-f2cdf816d916" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Private Key</strong> → Used by the sender to create the digital signature.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8015-aaf3-e812fe5cca6d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Public Key</strong> → Used by the receiver to verify the signature.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806a-a96e-d0a96db4820e" class="">DSS often uses secure hash functions such as <strong>Secure Hash Algorithm</strong>, particularly <strong>SHA-256</strong>, to generate message digests before signing.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-b4d5-de89286fcf8d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80d5-8d57-ead049c40097" class="">Working of DSS</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8053-bc4b-f98a6d30f378" class="">The process of DSS involves <strong>two main phases</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-b7a8-c43e933fa79c" class="">1. Signature Generation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-804b-b297-e2e0ef7ab1fb" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The sender prepares the <strong>original message</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8092-8df3-f221b6fdc47e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>A hash function like <strong>SHA-256</strong> generates a <strong>message digest</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b5-987b-f62ef0038d6f" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The sender uses the <strong>private key</strong> and the <strong>DSA algorithm</strong> to generate a <strong>digital signature</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8096-963c-c80e92c8c9db" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>The message and digital signature are sent to the receiver.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80db-8968-ee5a6a82ed53"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-826d-e26210d107fb" class="">2. Signature Verification</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-baae-c2de1e8d68c9" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The receiver receives the <strong>message and digital signature</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80fb-8d11-e83c3cef4a4c" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The receiver generates a new <strong>message digest</strong> using the same hash function.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-80c5-cddfe95bbbc2" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The <strong>public key</strong> is used with the <strong>DSA verification algorithm</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806a-9e06-c2938f22b30c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>The computed digest is compared with the received signature.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8048-a137-f693d9c7856d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If both match → <strong>message is authentic</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804f-ae2d-dc12880972e7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If they do not match → <strong>message has been altered</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8057-85c8-c17fd0b29dc2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80e2-85b1-e3ce06706ba4" class="">Features of DSS</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8083-a2f3-d47b84862df9" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>authentication of the sender</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-8b40-c1c0d654ca33" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Ensures <strong>data integrity</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-adcf-f9ce6eef26c5" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Supports <strong>non-repudiation</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8090-bef4-c7f06604002b" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Uses <strong>public key cryptography</strong> for security.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8056-bf5d-e3a6aa96a9ab"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8013-bd97-cca378fea7bf" class="">Advantages of DSS</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8078-842b-c79abd7b5f11" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Highly <strong>secure and reliable</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b7-a12d-ca26f633faf8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Used in <strong>government and financial systems</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80df-b555-fdb4d254a246" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects against <strong>message tampering</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-807a-bbc1-c7d8523ae3af" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>legal validity for electronic signatures</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-82ca-c6741425ea20"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80dd-a01e-e3b648946d7c" class="">Applications of DSS</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806c-a3b8-f4e590297457" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Secure email communication</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8029-b971-d27fe0c796b1" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Online banking transactions</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-800e-8bfe-cbe6058e2359" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>E-commerce systems</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-a90a-fd216c5f80f4" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Electronic contracts and legal documents</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-88f6-e41466adb48c" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Secure software distribution</strong></li></ol></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8045-bcb8-e31df5de6561" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80c2-8c0e-cf4d4ed22a76" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">proof of Digital Signature Algorithm</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-8038-ab95-c073c1631216"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801b-a0dc-f2af81b3ed1b" class="">proof of Digital Signature Algorithm</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-8450-fa8c80f74ec7" class="">The proof of the <strong>Digital Signature Algorithm</strong> shows that if a signature is generated correctly using the sender’s <strong>private key</strong>, the verification process using the <strong>public key</strong> will produce the same value v=rv = rv=r. This confirms that the message is <strong>authentic and unchanged</strong>, ensuring secure digital communication.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804c-b82c-cb500d24ad88" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801e-9b86-f56ec8102017" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8071-bf95-d1f4b9ce9a89" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-803d-bf07-c6ba7a1d042f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8013-9085-cdcb95f8c12c" class=""><strong>Module 4: Web Security</strong></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8039-b242-d5c777ca76ba" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Authentication Applications: Kerberos</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8084-86f3-f08c574f22b8" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Overview of Access Control List (ACL)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8025-8f6d-ff22520350da" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Electronic Mail Security: Pretty Good Privacy (PGP)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c4-bb65-e22a7e692c3d" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">S/MIME.</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80dc-8dfe-c22f812e6637" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">IP Security: Architecture</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-802a-9f67-cd05a153b5cf" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Authentication Header</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-9bb6-f63e00502ecf" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Encapsulating Security Payloads</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ac-a5b8-d7eb89d06574" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Key Management</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80be-93e5-e5b8b470ccb6" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Introduction to Secure Socket Layer</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8087-b8de-e02d43e2db94" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Secure electronic, transaction (SET)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8078-a74c-cae341e7be8b" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">1. Basic Idea of Kerberos</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-8af8-ddc7c33ce4ee"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-8277-c550eaebb4a4" class="">1. Basic Idea of Kerberos</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8035-91b8-c669252cf5e9" class="">Kerberos allows a user to <strong>prove their identity once and then access multiple services without repeatedly entering passwords</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805b-ac0e-c5ead8721b4e" class="">This concept is called <strong>Single Sign-On (SSO)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807f-880e-daaf9c39e970" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-808f-8570-c9295fc9ad4e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A user logs into a university network.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-806d-82c9-f02bb6792ab2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">After authentication, they can access:<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-800c-8944-cdf6fb46907c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">Email server</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c6-965d-c3cc77dd5bfb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">Database</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8046-b2a4-ea9c40b69a40" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">File server<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808f-b3fc-d3a77af9106a" class="">without logging in again.</p></div></li></ul></div></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-809a-9fdb-e142ea3083f1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8063-8914-f7213bd84492" class="">2. Main Goals of Kerberos</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ac-b429-d56eace7bff7" class="">Kerberos provides the following security goals:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-809c-8210-c9bface4e1c1" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Authentication</strong> – verifies identity of users and services</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8032-b61e-ca62d7441d33" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> – protects credentials using encryption</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-94f5-da22a11c067d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Integrity</strong> – prevents tampering with authentication messages</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8086-ac9d-d6a236685a67" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Single Sign-On (SSO)</strong> – user logs in only once</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c0-b75c-c7ddac16a184" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Mutual Authentication</strong> – both client and server verify each other</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-804b-9a26-cd0c89d64102"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80b7-8242-d1ddf6e6bfbf" class="">3. Components of Kerberos</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80df-91a6-c08ddf74b623" class="">Kerberos architecture contains three main components:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-9cfe-e3f14a1198a0" class="">1. Client (User)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804c-ae8f-f14c127e4dfb" class="">The person or system requesting access to a service.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d6-bed4-cea0d57aee82" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-aac6-eef308d4670d" class="">A student logging into a campus network.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8057-b4b6-ce7ac640527c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80e8-80f2-fc32944ebd40" class="">2. Authentication Server (AS)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8011-9f53-d870229cfc33" class="">The server that verifies the user’s identity.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-a62c-efb05b8fdc4c" class="">Functions:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-b9f9-dab7af683e41" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Checks username and password</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8047-96af-c200a5a79a61" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Issues authentication credentials</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8053-aa90-d54ebe81d4ef"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-a64c-e6375677ab16" class="">3. Ticket Granting Server (TGS)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8070-bc38-d7110dcc5446" class="">Issues <strong>tickets</strong> that allow access to network services.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fd-8a6a-ce9f72b2cf76" class="">Instead of sending passwords repeatedly, users present tickets.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-803a-9e6a-cd9487644a20"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-800c-afc7-c1a8892aca05" class="">4. Service Server</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803d-a330-fe79bbcb5ae3" class="">The server that provides the requested service.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8081-9180-f3360e106f5d" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809d-a39b-f8e83627e19e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Email server</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-a12c-ea11ba95718c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">File server</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-9cac-f11551d86f82" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Database server</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8004-85c0-f8b6f1ae544c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8075-ae9a-db74c606a3f6" class="">4. Kerberos Authentication Process</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c3-98cb-c248c891fc1c" class="">The Kerberos authentication process happens in <strong>three main phases</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802d-9996-d8d45c27f1fd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8044-aac9-d51b80f5cc00" class="">Step 1: Initial Login Authentication</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-809a-b9ce-c9ed28b8d55f" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>User enters <strong>username and password</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e0-9f4f-cb3a492836cf" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Client sends request to <strong>Authentication Server (AS)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8076-adde-e1136eb4be46" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>AS verifies credentials.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8035-ba8c-f557dac0883c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>AS sends back a <strong>Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f2-9f31-f77f27d55926" class="">The TGT is encrypted so attackers cannot read it.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8052-80a1-d1ab12c59854"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8015-9503-f33196ac2f55" class="">Step 2: Ticket Granting Phase</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-804a-8659-dea3fa127b1a" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Client sends <strong>TGT</strong> to <strong>Ticket Granting Server (TGS)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8065-b772-f792bb81e1cf" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Client requests access to a specific service.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-809a-9ee0-cd55bdca272c" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>TGS verifies the TGT.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-805d-9090-e4af7e9ca27d" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>TGS issues a <strong>Service Ticket</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-807a-9244-c2f95bbadc8d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8085-a36e-c7e55057abf6" class="">Step 3: Service Access Phase</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80bf-96d4-c865d711c5cb" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Client sends <strong>Service Ticket</strong> to the target server.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8095-82fd-f1fa1519be6d" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Server verifies the ticket.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803e-b53e-ff309c530852" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Access to the service is granted.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8066-9590-cd694e00cd78"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-802e-b371-c403fb93317b" class="">5. Kerberos Architecture Diagram (Conceptual)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8069-a690-d6d2cbc320b9" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">User (Client)
      |
      | Login Request
      v
Authentication Server (AS)
      |
      | Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT)
      v
Ticket Granting Server (TGS)
      |
      | Service Ticket
      v
Service Server</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8020-addb-eeb0992ada7a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8001-aecf-c03e1ceb6e45" class="">6. Important Concepts in Kerberos</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80cb-ad77-d10782d4a8b4" class="">1. Ticket</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bd-b563-d4f2ea00bb53" class="">A <strong>ticket</strong> is an encrypted data structure used for authentication.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8094-98da-cd3e71593236" class="">Types:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8041-b67a-da8a63dba466" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>TGT (Ticket Granting Ticket)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-a2c4-cf9584d96539" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Service Ticket</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c2-92e2-d02a858eb489"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-b09b-c069b7b7b639" class="">2. Authenticator</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8090-b7f8-f8a3b6c64e9b" class="">A temporary credential used with a ticket to verify the client’s identity.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-a935-f02fe7c7daef" class="">It prevents <strong>replay attacks</strong>.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805e-b1dd-f7c4aa30c26e" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bd-a5bd-ddf0bc3086f6" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-97fa-fc0130614955" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">2. Overview of </mark><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>Access Control List (ACL)</strong></mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80bd-8b34-e89042629b33"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-807a-821a-eaa49c0a02ed" class="">Overview of <strong>Access Control List (ACL)</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8044-aa00-cc94493d4965" class="">An <strong>Access Control List (ACL)</strong> is a security mechanism used to <strong>control and manage access to system resources</strong> such as files, databases, networks, or devices. It defines <strong>which users or systems are allowed or denied specific permissions</strong> on an object.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-afab-d916c4cc59c3" class="">ACLs are widely used in <strong>operating systems, databases, and network security</strong> to enforce authorization policies.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8014-80fb-ff65c58e0b6e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8020-9156-d1967a25479c" class="">1. Definition</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-8dad-c91028af4263" class="">An <strong>Access Control List (ACL)</strong> is a <strong>list of permissions attached to an object</strong> that specifies <strong>which users or system processes are granted access to that object and what operations they can perform</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8069-8a60-d91599a4de7a" class="">Example of operations:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8058-8843-e3b6d028aef6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Read</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-800e-9ebc-f434c2a572fb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Write</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803b-a2f8-fb9e927d340c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Execute</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8013-a908-e7ffca2de0f3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Delete</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8057-ae28-e928c33087da" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Modify</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-befb-de577cedbb7f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-9101-cf39d27e31a4" class="">2. Basic Concept</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-800a-ad9f-d4c8e4b65f0c" class="">ACL works on the principle:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-83b4-caf675d39a7d" class=""><strong>Object → List of Users → Permissions</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8058-b5ef-d45372e74d39" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="322f2fef-a309-80e4-8e81-d5188287bdac" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-8013-8727-e8d8062b0c16"><th id="gzS}" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">User</th><th id="U&lt;x~" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Permission</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-8011-804c-fde097f88ae0"><td id="gzS}" class="">User A</td><td id="U&lt;x~" class="">Read</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-80f9-bdb0-c168e40ed03d"><td id="gzS}" class="">User B</td><td id="U&lt;x~" class="">Read, Write</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-8034-b699-dc0dd4b32545"><td id="gzS}" class="">User C</td><td id="U&lt;x~" class="">Denied</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8045-b4ae-dae9877390d2" class="">This means different users have <strong>different levels of access</strong> to the same resource.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8092-9d63-d5e9ba9b1ca2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8037-a496-ec8889acc1f8" class="">3. Components of an ACL</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-802c-86c9-c11d7b803855" class="">1. Object</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bf-ad2c-fbefae6746df" class="">The resource being protected.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8073-ab46-ce5d226ef671" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8007-8577-dd2799965d4c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-802b-a72b-d047e64cdc9c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Directories</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8003-8e45-fd25bee41a55" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Databases</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-ae97-da1631f10aa4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Network devices</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-bc95-ebf0135f4c41"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8058-8084-e8480a40c317" class="">2. Subject</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80db-b0cb-f06b9248e575" class="">The user or process requesting access.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8085-ab83-c51758ab7055" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8014-9fa2-ff3db30b7b24" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">System user</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8082-888a-e762193e177c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Application</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803a-8c0e-c6228d7a7fbc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Service</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8050-bcef-d382eb24eccc"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8069-b72a-f20a41374431" class="">3. Permissions</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802f-9256-c538a564976a" class="">The operations that the subject can perform.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-800b-952d-d60c2a8304bf" class="">Common permissions include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805e-b619-f96327f6b959" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Read (R)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d2-acdd-c8832dd362f2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Write (W)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809f-aee0-def93a1c020f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Execute (X)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8044-9d8b-e5436bcce62c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Delete</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d1-b6ae-fb3ca0e6c43e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Modify</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8063-ba70-cf653957900b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8039-94cc-f743abdd9631" class="">4. Structure of an ACL Entry</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c0-aef4-ef9c536fb454" class="">Each entry in an ACL is called an <strong>Access Control Entry (ACE)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ec-9d54-f2cc16928c06" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8084-8da6-fb793be4d7d4" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Subject → Permission → Allow/Deny</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-afaf-f09bcc791060" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-809e-b466-d8c83e297eaf" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">User1 → Read → Allow
User2 → Write → Allow
User3 → Execute → Deny</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ea-9435-f96f97f3b2be"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-809f-86d4-e3bc2c96ab33" class="">5. Types of Access Control Lists</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fd-a2c8-ed80ab055cda" class="">1. Standard ACL</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8003-81ed-dd8fedfc8bee" class="">Controls access <strong>based only on source IP address</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8065-ab7b-deb1c718ab0e" class="">Used mainly in <strong>basic network filtering</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8003-bf3a-c05db95448c6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-ac9f-cd59ff933abe" class="">2. Extended ACL</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809a-86d0-fde2a1d1cf78" class="">Provides more detailed control based on:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-a021-c45dd9debfcf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Source IP</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8050-87fd-cbd18c21cd50" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Destination IP</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ea-b4f4-c410edcb721b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protocol</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-86ee-db2f8d37c27f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Port number</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807f-b889-f8afa7370314" class="">Used in <strong>advanced network security policies</strong>.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8053-9fed-f2411cd6c294" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8086-9ba3-f95965182a5b" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">3. Electronic Mail Security: </mark><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>Pretty Good Privacy (PGP)</strong></mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-a576-cddd8a035548"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80fd-8087-d4d17e647dae" class="">Electronic Mail Security: <strong>Pretty Good Privacy (PGP)</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808c-8c05-cb4b18bc7ec7" class=""><strong>Pretty Good Privacy (PGP)</strong> is a widely used <strong>encryption program for securing electronic mail communication</strong>. It provides <strong>confidentiality, authentication, data integrity, and compression</strong> for email messages and files.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-b8f8-d6d45b6ff511" class="">PGP was developed in 1991 by <strong>Phil Zimmermann</strong> and is based on a <strong>combination of symmetric encryption and public-key cryptography</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808d-93a1-cb514c043988"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-803f-b2c6-f0749d3fa736" class="">1. Purpose of PGP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804b-ba15-fffc9e883577" class="">PGP is used to protect email communication from unauthorized access. It ensures that:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809b-8ddd-fa1058e60ce5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Only the intended recipient can read the message.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c0-bcbb-fab195cb7df2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">The receiver can verify the sender’s identity.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8099-b537-c101bc5705a7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">The message has not been altered during transmission.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8039-bf4a-c7d912e7f21b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-800e-abbe-db47457b2313" class="">2. Security Services Provided by PGP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8082-b1cd-fb5ba7e9b708" class="">1. Confidentiality</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-ab54-d01587e06005" class="">PGP encrypts the message so that only authorized users can read it.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80bd-b7e4-d26367ce71a9" class="">2. Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8084-a56d-eb90e6de0ae8" class="">It verifies that the message is sent by the legitimate sender.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-8d01-c72b0597c7c2" class="">3. Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8021-8354-d2d13f792096" class="">Ensures that the message has not been modified during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8036-b73c-f331432bcbc1" class="">4. Non-repudiation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8056-8c8e-df335623b7d7" class="">The sender cannot deny sending the message because of the digital signature.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-807c-8d34-c6ab1241b513"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8034-a9e2-e52b176a5be0" class="">3. Key Features of PGP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8033-a2c4-fa11ab239c8f" class="">Important features include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-95cd-f53d33246674" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Hybrid encryption</strong> (combines symmetric and public-key encryption)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8095-90f8-eb7aed9a785a" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Digital signatures</strong> for authentication</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-807b-a0be-d64a7ba4417f" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Compression</strong> to reduce message size</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-bb7a-f15bc3cf3f2a" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Email compatibility</strong> using ASCII conversion</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8034-b8c0-dec022d1ebc2" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Message integrity verification</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8041-9fbe-dd256333f39e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8095-8787-ed5e3278d1c8" class="">4. Components Used in PGP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-aea7-c6e63f3fc795" class="">1. Symmetric Key Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-b849-dabc7ed9c1a5" class="">Used for <strong>fast encryption of the actual message</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805b-98b7-ce9282da7efd" class="">Example algorithms:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8019-ab0e-d9d576be54b1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">IDEA</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-ad91-d5e2a780b459" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">AES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8037-b052-d03a1d6034a2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Triple DES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-805d-a110-d0b55057fca0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8056-a19e-c53cd010813c" class="">2. Public Key Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-8fc7-fa99aba382bf" class="">Used to <strong>encrypt the session key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808e-8e0b-c20bd3d9dccf" class="">Example algorithms:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8063-88dc-cfd2af92cdc3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>RSA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8037-b545-f765ee50894a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8035-8900-cb2496354f7a" class="">3. Hash Function</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802f-b0cc-e09df5af239c" class="">Used to create a <strong>message digest</strong> for integrity verification.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-9e62-f5da18d2b0ad" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801a-b62a-d41c167a7752" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-9724-c6f4db7e634a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-806d-9bfa-dd079a0809c0" class="">5. Working of PGP (Encryption Process)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8086-b6ab-f354c4bb9e86" class="">The PGP process involves several steps.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-804f-b5dd-ca9b1f7cb838" class="">Step 1: Message Creation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8087-a8ff-d53e0cd43980" class="">The sender writes the email message.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-95cf-e64de5a1c121"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-800c-b9a5-dcf9ea3aba40" class="">Step 2: Hashing</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8043-a65b-f3ce1926abb3" class="">A hash function creates a <strong>message digest</strong> of the message.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-991e-edcc3d5fa2a9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8081-b695-d5d055cda89f" class="">Step 3: Digital Signature</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8088-83f0-d84657fe6040" class="">The sender encrypts the message digest using their <strong>private key</strong> to create a <strong>digital signature</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8059-b05f-c2037936f72f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-a93a-cb0c275498db" class="">Step 4: Compression</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805a-95ec-c544a6869638" class="">The message and signature are compressed to reduce size.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-a96b-c87b07bdbb23"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-a498-f35b3cee1399" class="">Step 5: Session Key Generation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806f-92d9-feed303d895e" class="">PGP generates a <strong>random session key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-a08d-c2ae7b711871"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-8b51-e02ee16096d3" class="">Step 6: Message Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8039-a623-d6ea3fce0c86" class="">The compressed message is encrypted using the <strong>session key (symmetric encryption)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8065-a0bb-e26a98c5876a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a5-b947-c1cb12d0c7fe" class="">Step 7: Session Key Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809f-8a44-e43876760871" class="">The session key is encrypted using the <strong>receiver’s public key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-86ea-fec3b6aa8df7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8036-aeed-f64f30af08a6" class="">Step 8: ASCII Armor</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8096-8fa0-ebffc96b0e89" class="">The encrypted data is converted into <strong>ASCII format</strong> so it can be transmitted through email systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80e7-b067-fc8994a66dd6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80a0-993e-da8558e29978" class="">6. PGP Decryption Process</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8036-82a8-e41cfec2402a" class="">The receiver performs the reverse steps:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-9a55-f74346bc0d62" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Convert ASCII message to binary.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-802a-97fb-e387297364f8" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Decrypt session key using <strong>receiver’s private key</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b8-87c3-ea83324584bf" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Use session key to decrypt the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-805a-a21f-cea9f23683d7" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Decompress the message.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803c-bde6-f3f674b08d70" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Verify digital signature using <strong>sender’s public key</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-94ad-f87fcb4e0708"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80bd-af5a-ef55da53e9f5" class="">7. PGP Message Structure</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8077-bb71-cfc01386dbea" class="">A typical PGP message contains:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-9206-fbf4e8a0344c" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Encrypted session key</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-b349-fbe7777ea2c4" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Encrypted message</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8033-9cf0-ee120c9e82a7" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Digital signature</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8057-88ea-e8d9c07024e9" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Message digest</li></ol></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8003-bc62-d2d545f13dd0" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8014-9a6d-ed6254d8aff0" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">4. Electronic Mail Security: </mark><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>S/MIME</strong></mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-8032-958b-c02ab7b15d5e"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-808b-a110-e2f93e6d4529" class="">Electronic Mail Security: <strong>S/MIME</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8083-9335-c0ada4f3909c" class=""><strong>S/MIME (Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions)</strong> is a <strong>standard protocol used to provide security for electronic mail communication</strong>. It enables <strong>encryption and digital signatures</strong> for email messages so that communication over the internet remains <strong>secure and authenticated</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807d-a799-c8aa89bbbd6d" class="">S/MIME is an extension of <strong>MIME</strong>, which allows email to support multimedia content like images, audio, and video.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808a-805e-ce156b85f126"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8024-8ad5-fbff6e41cdcc" class="">1. Purpose of S/MIME</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806c-a1b1-f34e9a4f1ab1" class="">The main purpose of S/MIME is to ensure <strong>secure email communication</strong> by protecting messages from unauthorized access and modification.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8094-9b2e-f14563b08af6" class="">It provides:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8064-b2f6-da44b428be67" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Confidentiality</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-9c27-ee5154e567ac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ec-8bdc-ec66aa3f2c75" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data Integrity</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e8-993b-ee7e4a0a6a42" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Non-repudiation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-9118-c1d518679b53"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8092-ae82-f38ea64a6ed4" class="">2. Security Services Provided by S/MIME</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ca-ac53-e5bf4046abb4" class="">1. Confidentiality</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8015-a081-d0e107e35366" class="">Email messages are <strong>encrypted</strong> so that only the intended recipient can read them.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8041-8a8d-c4867d938b7e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8058-8a87-eff652a5929b" class="">2. Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8056-a50a-fbe80b898262" class="">The receiver can verify the <strong>identity of the sender</strong> using digital signatures.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8003-af01-d6dabe7111af"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8004-9245-e4ea745a702f" class="">3. Data Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-800b-f173cd5c3c73" class="">Ensures that the message <strong>has not been modified</strong> during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-804a-8e22-cf9443b36a47"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8050-86b4-fff193622bcf" class="">4. Non-Repudiation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-b7f4-c72ef08e9645" class="">The sender <strong>cannot deny sending the message</strong> because of the digital signature.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-801c-b23f-fc5eb43f1311"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80fb-883d-d57c3e306c4b" class="">3. Cryptographic Techniques Used in S/MIME</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8043-8e33-c63071262562" class="">S/MIME uses a combination of cryptographic methods:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a5-a451-facbdb656861" class="">1. Public Key Cryptography</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809f-b732-f7147d2e5a64" class="">Used for <strong>key exchange and digital signatures</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c2-895e-c3adbdb12896" class="">Example algorithm:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f8-8703-d389c286345f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>RSA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8012-8d22-f7cbbc1283e4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-808a-881e-e700f8e8d314" class="">2. Symmetric Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804f-91ef-effe21c9b5c3" class="">Used to encrypt the <strong>actual email message</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f8-b29c-d8c465752251" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-800c-860c-d2c4390ae7fd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">AES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8039-b49e-d32ffb70fe02" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Triple DES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-bc9e-f237b145f68b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8046-9341-e919148deddf" class="">3. Hash Functions</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808c-b84f-e92aa930ad19" class="">Used to create a <strong>message digest</strong> for integrity verification.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8080-8efc-e9c67f2c185e" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8048-ac60-e83d40ba5dd7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-807f-b6c2-f0b10e51e0d3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80f6-8d9b-ffc2a1145ab1" class="">4. How S/MIME Works (Process)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80b1-8baa-ec92cf13d536" class="">Step 1: Message Creation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8080-a9a4-e02fdf173d04" class="">The sender writes the email message.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8090-985e-eaa9113fa4e5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-807d-bbde-e056f02f28fe" class="">Step 2: Message Digest</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-babf-e670770ce9fe" class="">A hash function creates a <strong>digest of the message</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-801f-841b-d3e78813d267"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80a6-bd2e-e6fd22bd5411" class="">Step 3: Digital Signature</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-93f4-fa0abe04763b" class="">The digest is encrypted using the <strong>sender&#x27;s private key</strong> to create a digital signature.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-915f-d9c5ec319c67"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-801d-8d9e-f32891f83f61" class="">Step 4: Message Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8053-977f-f03bf6958d46" class="">The email message is encrypted using a <strong>symmetric session key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-800f-9c87-c4f6b4881365"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8056-873a-f89441814845" class="">Step 5: Session Key Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-9764-cdb007e9f079" class="">The session key is encrypted using the <strong>receiver’s public key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80bf-93cb-ec21b1651f65"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8092-aba6-e8d99ec7b253" class="">Step 6: Message Transmission</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-800a-970c-fe69ed784b7a" class="">The encrypted email and digital signature are sent to the receiver.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802c-b15b-d474ec8416d8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8019-8c51-d2e25796b5af" class="">5. S/MIME Message Structure</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ab-9495-c8652be7ecfe" class="">An S/MIME message contains:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-809d-a3fc-ea003ae238f6" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Encrypted message</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ce-b518-fdfdf6f4fbca" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Digital signature</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8086-8333-d60f5e0f2145" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Encrypted session key</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803e-ad78-f82100e9e536" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Certificate of the sender</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8059-b3bb-f24ee9c46dab" class="">The certificate is usually issued by a <strong>Certificate Authority (CA)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8022-8b80-e119428f3317"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-804d-908c-e6f16f7df1b8" class="">6. Types of S/MIME Messages</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80b6-9c67-c7710ebd5977" class="">1. Enveloped Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808f-b5f7-f722f0685eec" class="">Used for <strong>encrypted email messages</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808c-997c-e1b1e9fb8a29"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8008-a756-f3a7df08a7ff" class="">2. Signed Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-a33b-f83c91e6cffa" class="">Used to <strong>verify the sender&#x27;s identity</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8053-b438-c6c01ebc548a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d1-90db-e5a362f20f0c" class="">3. Clear Signed Message</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80cb-8887-e736948cd5a5" class="">Message is readable but contains a <strong>digital signature for verification</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8004-ae59-fe25785d330b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8054-bc21-da46b8978290" class="">4. Signed and Enveloped Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e5-95bf-dce552f2ba79" class="">Provides <strong>both encryption and digital signature</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80fa-b6bf-c69fbef7ba03"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-b144-e9f6de66571f" class="">7. Advantages of S/MIME</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8054-bcd5-f5bfa787efd9" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>strong email security</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8047-99a3-c9d795afe9dc" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Uses <strong>public key infrastructure (PKI)</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-bd2b-c45dcd6f041e" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Supported by many email clients</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-a28c-dd4050d81faf" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Ensures <strong>authentication and confidentiality</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-806b-a8e3-da9eb06f8da1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80c0-8df2-f487dd9b5789" class="">8. Limitations of S/MIME</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80bf-8b69-f6363c5065f4" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Requires <strong>digital certificates</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c2-8d17-de74731a4ee8" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Certificate management can be <strong>complex</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c4-b866-ed7b7f390716" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Not all users configure it properly</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8019-9cf4-de3b87afbe78"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8028-98a5-f70816fe2270" class="">9. Applications of S/MIME</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8032-bbda-db6b73910cbe" class="">S/MIME is supported by many email systems such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80df-a463-c82da00f8c24" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Microsoft Outlook</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-807a-a191-e31afd6120ad" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Apple Mail</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-bff3-e4f686e2d632" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Mozilla Thunderbird</strong></li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d6-be4a-cf432816676d" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-8f51-ca49d6352f4b" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">5. IP Security: </mark><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>IPsec Architecture</strong></mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-8034-88f5-e56e2912e708"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8007-ae65-f8b3194b33a8" class="">IP Security: <strong>IPsec Architecture</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8055-8f48-e2daab887d7c" class=""><strong>IPsec (Internet Protocol Security)</strong> is a <strong>network layer security protocol suite</strong> used to provide <strong>secure communication over IP networks</strong> such as the Internet. It protects data by providing <strong>authentication, confidentiality, integrity, and secure key management</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8044-8e3a-f1e070f54df7" class="">IPsec operates at the <strong>IP layer (Layer 3 of the OSI model)</strong> and is commonly used in <strong>Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80cf-8636-dfb35d09c2a9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-809a-b96f-cf09adb106d7" class="">1. Objective of IPsec Architecture</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80db-9211-f47c8af7666b" class="">The architecture of IPsec defines the <strong>framework and mechanisms used to secure IP communications</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8094-b205-ecdb8e101475" class="">Main goals include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-9a9d-d328e740dbaa" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data confidentiality</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805d-af5d-ced5d0800a33" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data integrity</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-8947-d8416ec27cd6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8089-ad97-c32cb94b6841" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protection against replay attacks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-9748-c1a167631700" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure key exchange</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8090-a795-d41cda006557"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80b8-a4be-ee8cc166efd1" class="">2. Components of IPsec Architecture</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-9f0e-d87998173f1a" class="">1. Security Protocols</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8091-a5ce-da9e60da5381" class="">IPsec uses two main protocols:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-802d-a36a-f7f1e2e5f083" class="">(a) <strong>Authentication Header (AH)</strong></h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8093-b09e-d6197617d2ab" class="">Provides:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-b4cb-de416192b629" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data integrity</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8046-9e6c-d8a51d4b0df0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data origin authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-806a-bd84-c633346e14d8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protection against replay attacks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8000-b29c-e2c0be4bcddd" class="">It does <strong>not provide encryption</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808f-807f-cf761ab0dd25"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-949a-d598c14d8c70" class="">(b) <strong>Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)</strong></h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8014-81b0-f6d822d296ce" class="">Provides:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8000-8181-eab489fff584" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data encryption (confidentiality)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-9baf-e8d434159163" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8007-ae5a-ee845ce957c1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data integrity</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-b82e-dbf57a9cdc66" class="">ESP is the <strong>most commonly used IPsec protocol</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8028-8da9-c57a90e5f9a3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-801f-8dcb-ef6708efdc86" class="">3. Security Association (SA)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8026-aef4-c123371b2c2f" class="">A <strong>Security Association (SA)</strong> is a <strong>set of security parameters</strong> used to establish secure communication between two systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805c-a4d5-e5b0ae6e90de" class="">Each SA defines:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8059-a28a-f48ccdc5c0fd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption algorithm</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80af-9a68-d66d09879020" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authentication algorithm</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c8-952c-cfa43bdb69a8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Key information</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8028-b696-c39a09b741dd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Lifetime of the connection</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-b906-cf23205632f2" class="">Important characteristics:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-a115-c2abd560ea90" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Unidirectional</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-802c-ac94-f065e9b0982e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Identified by <strong>SPI (Security Parameter Index)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80be-941d-c64ecbc19732"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8005-aefd-e222ccc9b9ad" class="">4. Security Policy Database (SPD)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803b-8094-fa89cc8f735c" class="">The <strong>Security Policy Database (SPD)</strong> contains <strong>rules that determine how IP packets should be protected</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804f-8b16-eb30e6a8b2ce" class="">The SPD decides whether a packet should:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805e-9cc8-e0a533981b8d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Be <strong>protected by IPsec</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f6-849d-ccd14063d642" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Be <strong>discarded</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d2-b4f2-f0ce1d2f7ec2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Be <strong>sent normally</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803d-8afc-c492cd00082c" class="">Example policy:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="322f2fef-a309-802a-9331-df6b266ac104" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-802d-8263-e9d68997b69f"><th id="ESuL" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Source</th><th id="OEG&gt;" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Destination</th><th id="YNwb" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Action</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-8016-bd05-fedee9885f63"><td id="ESuL" class="">Host A</td><td id="OEG&gt;" class="">Host B</td><td id="YNwb" class="">Apply IPsec</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-8001-9f59-df8e077c7115"><td id="ESuL" class="">Host C</td><td id="OEG&gt;" class="">Host D</td><td id="YNwb" class="">Discard</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="322f2fef-a309-803d-bf84-c61a9a7767c8"><td id="ESuL" class="">Others</td><td id="OEG&gt;" class="">Any</td><td id="YNwb" class="">Allow</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-8f51-d63e28cb926f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8087-a7e5-d849a780cabd" class="">5. Security Association Database (SAD)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8078-8de0-c7986077b110" class="">The <strong>Security Association Database (SAD)</strong> stores all <strong>active Security Associations</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8085-b118-c835650698d7" class="">Each entry contains:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8079-bedb-fdb9ebfc6a85" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">SPI (Security Parameter Index)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-bc93-d4d33a33dca8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Destination IP address</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804c-b129-e11d702ba8f2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Security protocol (AH or ESP)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803c-ac88-fd38636a9c5d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption and authentication keys</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-9beb-eb17471ce431" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">SA lifetime</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-aba3-cf8a2bf1f8cb"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-801c-afed-d4e6bae88ac2" class="">6. Modes of Operation</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808b-a185-c5ec052a6ec1" class="">IPsec works in two modes:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-8f26-ff5aaea35e9e" class="">1. Transport Mode</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80cf-9ec8-ea9ddd193cd1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects only the <strong>payload of the IP packet</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8094-9475-e7d815a98598" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Original IP header remains unchanged</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-aa3b-cd7c97b5cc7f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Used for <strong>host-to-host communication</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-944c-d2116d842745" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-800b-bade-cb1067304b84" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">IP Header | AH/ESP | Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8089-af92-db123bf4e747"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80be-bea9-c777656dc793" class="">2. Tunnel Mode</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804f-91a1-e4f13c2fc181" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encrypts the <strong>entire IP packet</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8008-acbb-f50bffd9a59f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A new IP header is added</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-b2ae-c4607a2433f2" class="">Used in <strong>VPN communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804a-aae4-c308e22963b9" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8011-8a98-f41d9e258a85" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">New IP Header | AH/ESP | Original IP Header | Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80af-ad6c-de87c42f0261"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80be-b56c-fa798196576a" class="">7. Key Management in IPsec</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-b986-c000fe533645" class="">Keys used in IPsec are generated and managed using:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8026-8795-e55eb429c5af" class=""><strong>Internet Key Exchange</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-98b2-fab10a35fad2" class="">IKE performs:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8070-b34b-c4111aa77560" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authentication of communicating parties</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-a7aa-dca1cede4954" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Negotiation of security algorithms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8007-b5e5-e96421c0ab37" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure key exchange</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-800d-8595-d281098d10cd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-88b4-e71c276f18a3" class="">8. IPsec Architecture Workflow</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f9-851e-c2522b7b9dd8" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Sender creates an IP packet.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-802f-9734-ec094d6c98ff" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>System checks <strong>Security Policy Database (SPD)</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8015-b466-c452f2147fd4" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>If protection is required:<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8014-9cb4-dc6cae4acc58" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Security Association (SA) is selected.</li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e2-b5e7-e296f90bc001" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Packet is processed using <strong>AH or ESP</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8023-b88b-cf2f12c51a38" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Packet is transmitted securely.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a1-a308-dc2db84ab8f2" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li>Receiver verifies and decrypts the packet.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-806d-fdf48e0f5fa7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-8005-c551d1a9384e" class="">9. Advantages of IPsec</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8084-81e0-f15192aac97a" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>strong network-layer security</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-800e-ab56-e538e8b0693d" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Works with <strong>IPv4 and IPv6</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-809a-9b5f-ea8f8b9389b6" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Supports <strong>VPN implementation</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8073-ba6c-f3559a79c6a6" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Transparent to applications</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b1-95e0-c64cd6ef5596" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Protects all IP traffic</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80f9-b4e9-e1f7db276b86"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8008-b4a2-c93e2a9a3cc4" class="">10. Limitations of IPsec</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8027-b834-c6ea546be18d" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Complex configuration</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e2-a5c5-f19f00b0b8fb" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Additional <strong>processing overhead</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8025-a623-efbc1b5217cb" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Requires proper <strong>key management</strong></li></ol></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-9df6-cb6e88c1478b" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-807b-810f-d0fda73f0ddd" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>6. Authentication Header (AH)</strong></mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80a3-b763-ce361204d63a"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80af-b721-e1cf966a4c6d" class=""><strong>Authentication Header (AH)</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f1-bd15-c08071e3fdde" class="">The <strong>Authentication Header (AH)</strong> is a security protocol used in <strong>IPsec</strong> to provide <strong>authentication, data integrity, and protection against replay attacks</strong> for IP packets.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8087-b515-ef01f8109563" class="">AH ensures that the <strong>data has not been modified during transmission and that it comes from a legitimate sender</strong>. However, AH <strong>does not provide encryption</strong>, so the data remains visible.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8068-b6ba-d558eae33ee4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-800f-9ec4-edd78c54f7f3" class="">1. Purpose of Authentication Header</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80de-a1c7-de8440c4fa49" class="">The main purpose of AH is to provide:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8048-aa81-d8c5ac972b43" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Data Integrity</strong> – ensures that the message has not been altered</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8005-99e8-e6418a00105b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Authentication</strong> – verifies the identity of the sender</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80be-ac26-d2284bab953f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Replay Protection</strong> – prevents attackers from reusing captured packets</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8012-8bb9-c7e88a45a9ed"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-804c-ba48-e354213bb659" class="">2. Services Provided by AH</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80cd-a9f0-ea7e27e262e0" class="">1. Data Origin Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8055-b48f-d7c3693067ab" class="">Confirms that the packet is sent by the legitimate sender.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-a05a-db8f97ea0f05" class="">2. Data Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a3-91a7-fa842e093edb" class="">Ensures that the packet contents have not been modified.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8040-ab25-fd87feaf5feb" class="">3. Replay Attack Protection</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8040-9336-f0adb28b34a9" class="">Uses a <strong>sequence number</strong> to detect duplicated packets.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-809d-994d-e652b237761b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ab-bf37-c192ba1628d4" class="">3. Services Not Provided by AH</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8019-a663-f990f8f1c452" class="">AH does <strong>not provide confidentiality</strong> because it does not encrypt the data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807c-a538-c0a13d5ed2f9" class="">So:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805b-8cf3-dc7d40a2adf5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Anyone can <strong>see the data</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80fa-9f3c-d4357ce73114" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">But <strong>cannot modify it without detection</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-807d-956a-f87a3350091d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-a6ec-f77bdbeab43e" class="">4. Position of AH in IP Packet</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8049-81ff-f5921e0c46d8" class="">The Authentication Header is inserted between the <strong>IP header and the payload</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8070-97dc-d64dcb04c684" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-a74a-c9a9c0ff5f4d" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">IP Header | AH Header | Data (Payload)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-800b-877e-ed960028e248"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8064-ab92-f8a3414f0a89" class="">5. Fields of the Authentication Header</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8003-bd89-f8a42cbe3daf" class="">The AH header contains several fields.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80db-ac3e-f1c5e2b56ef4" class="">1. Next Header</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8014-aab9-f8f791f49914" class="">Identifies the type of payload that follows AH.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-800d-913a-e4beb6fefc9f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ed-b7c3-fd432460e64e" class="">2. Payload Length</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804d-9311-cf8b84d60fa0" class="">Indicates the length of the AH header.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8044-a153-ca07214d312e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-ae64-ec627921ce48" class="">3. Reserved Field</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807f-9e8a-c48bbe125520" class="">Reserved for future use.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-803e-820b-c21154f140f9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-824b-d346d90acfe4" class="">4. Security Parameter Index (SPI)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8067-a5e7-c7e42fa40ced" class="">Identifies the <strong>Security Association (SA)</strong> used for the packet.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8024-a581-de77eff1cb3e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-a54c-ef15358d5f5a" class="">5. Sequence Number</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80dd-bf0b-ff27cfb63536" class="">Protects against <strong>replay attacks</strong> by ensuring packets are processed in order.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8078-b79d-d8bcb42f5d99"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f2-b21d-ddc4ab6e7b26" class="">6. Authentication Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8005-bfc3-de695006750d" class="">Contains the <strong>Integrity Check Value (ICV)</strong> created using a hash function.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8025-8c4a-dc67e97b7a8b" class="">Common algorithms include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8077-8b15-e7960c7da516" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-801d-9dd5-c2b6e097a906"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8074-87a8-c8067b386377" class="">6. Modes of Operation</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807b-80db-db0f425a1369" class="">AH operates in two modes.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8090-a029-f25be2c1a64d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80bd-a0f1-ff4fb620d9b2" class="">1. Transport Mode</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8075-8cbe-e7e3d032ebd5" class="">Only the <strong>payload of the IP packet is authenticated</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e1-9f74-dcfe977ab954" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-809d-b2d8-fb134082afce" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">IP Header | AH | Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a2-8b75-fc89485a6c19" class="">Used for <strong>host-to-host communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8033-b21d-e8c017b3ec07"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-801f-8e0b-dad5696b9f02" class="">2. Tunnel Mode</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80aa-b60a-f433a94d1855" class="">The <strong>entire original IP packet is authenticated</strong>, and a new IP header is added.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80df-a89e-d29cb6544c88" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80ca-94de-ea9ea3442f27" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">New IP Header | AH | Original IP Header | Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8081-9ab9-ed2c5cbdc277" class="">Used in <strong>VPN communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8006-9b7e-f1ee2469cf30"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8042-8299-c26dbed2ed77" class="">7. Working of Authentication Header</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-b96d-f90dfb35b7cf" class="">Step-by-step process:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806b-8203-ccb30c0be7ca" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Sender creates the IP packet.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806f-a90a-f7f3a82613e8" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>A <strong>hash value</strong> is calculated over selected fields.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-bbdc-c79ca706219f" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>This hash value becomes the <strong>Authentication Data</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ca-b288-e53c621b52c2" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>AH header is inserted into the packet.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8054-96c8-cddfef0a3a36" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Packet is transmitted.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e4-841e-d56dce63618c" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li>Receiver calculates the hash again.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-807c-8be3-cf573e6ac147" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li>If both hashes match → packet is authentic.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8093-ab07-d5137e1db8ec"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-a514-c37f112b13ef" class="">8. Advantages of AH</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8074-8224-d574f3d63afe" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>strong authentication</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8054-9a6b-dc78591667ca" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Ensures <strong>data integrity</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8049-be5d-cb8a0646e955" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Protects against <strong>replay attacks</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806d-8606-efd44505a3fe" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Works at the <strong>network layer</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8047-a2af-f35ad81f8f97"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8096-8e41-d448fbbec53d" class="">9. Limitations of AH</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8063-a8b7-e26aa03525db" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>No data encryption</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8098-be5f-f23b1e3c795c" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Less commonly used compared to ESP</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-801a-879f-e36168aebaff" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Not compatible with some <strong>NAT devices</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-86f4-eefcd4bf4979"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8002-b853-c76bcf371eeb" class="">10. Applications of AH</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-a988-f8dc6296b602" class="">AH is used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8062-b28c-e1c897206dde" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure IP communication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8096-8322-e9ae67e35702" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Network authentication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-83dc-c953ddd8bf70" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure VPN architectures</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8005-952c-df059d6238b7" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8051-9087-db3c48835cf6" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>6. Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)</strong></mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-808e-9574-dc74217e4a3b"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8020-a126-f4b1abb1cfc3" class=""><strong>Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8028-abd3-deb7c449c8a3" class=""><strong>Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)</strong> is a security protocol used in <strong>IPsec</strong> to provide <strong>confidentiality, data integrity, authentication, and protection against replay attacks</strong> for IP packets.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8097-8862-d05c0283a766" class="">Unlike the Authentication Header (AH), ESP <strong>encrypts the data</strong>, ensuring that unauthorized users cannot read the contents of the packet.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-803e-a4ac-d4216c94b478"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-897e-efc03cbd49a0" class="">1. Purpose of ESP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8059-b1bb-d68000355b26" class="">The main purpose of ESP is to <strong>secure IP communication by encrypting and protecting data packets</strong> transmitted over a network.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8004-9093-e25b805657f4" class="">ESP provides the following services:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8087-af61-c02bb8ee830a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Confidentiality (encryption)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b0-b400-fe8ff3be41f9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Data integrity</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8062-93f0-fe75d7e7c62d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Authentication</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d6-80e2-eface82693de" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Replay protection</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a5-af5c-c66b82cc37d3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-8d37-f4a38f871e2b" class="">2. Services Provided by ESP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-8bba-eb71f3ecd516" class="">1. Confidentiality</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d5-b080-cf37eadd56a1" class="">ESP encrypts the data using symmetric encryption algorithms so that only authorized users can read it.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803e-b0fb-f241ebeef613" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e0-88ca-f926d0cfcaac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">AES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8012-a5f1-cf6edbdc6ce6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Triple DES</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-9eeb-c5e2b8941218"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8049-80ec-e538e0838ab4" class="">2. Data Integrity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8032-bb33-ec2709f3c41e" class="">Ensures that the data has not been modified during transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8074-9d63-e318423ba266"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8016-9263-c58929e9763a" class="">3. Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804f-b52e-fa223955161c" class="">Verifies the identity of the sender.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8071-9bea-c0a3e287e660"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-8318-d86d75faf584" class="">4. Replay Protection</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809e-a3ab-ea15ac7b0be4" class="">Uses sequence numbers to prevent attackers from resending captured packets.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-805f-bb3b-f261e20f2771"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80db-b4cb-fa4822a453de" class="">3. ESP Packet Format</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8064-92a9-e4126f72e32e" class="">An ESP packet contains the following components:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-bb44-dbdaf6fc3fb3" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">ESP Header | Encrypted Data | ESP Trailer | Authentication Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8072-8eba-f7d1acebbf69" class="">Explanation:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8044-9ad0-f5bb4289c6cd" class="">1. ESP Header</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-ac1f-f355ecb04011" class="">Contains:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8042-8fe1-e266192b9c97" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Security Parameter Index (SPI)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803d-961c-ce645a256b39" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Sequence Number</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a0-b7eb-f3f1a4292866"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ec-ba4d-e87f9b89dc1d" class="">2. Encrypted Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-800f-8f1f-f480793da225" class="">Contains the <strong>actual payload data</strong> and sometimes the original IP header.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8075-8109-e2d179f9846c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8073-97ba-c4e4765c16b5" class="">3. ESP Trailer</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809d-a1db-de471aff877e" class="">Contains:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8096-93f1-cff1cb6f4ba4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Padding</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-8ba1-c109c485e436" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Pad length</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80a1-b6e3-da1232ab1bf8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Next header information</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8044-8c4c-ea72691c7cad"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-808f-904a-de96378c65c1" class="">4. Authentication Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806c-9bf2-efd42ad05438" class="">Contains a <strong>hash value used for integrity verification</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f1-9a74-f1a7814cba70" class="">Example hash algorithms:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-b7b6-e03b46f4b287" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808d-88fb-ffb9525b4479"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-800b-8157-d3d0e8bb0e3d" class="">4. Modes of Operation</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d2-a0c0-df644bf971f3" class="">ESP works in two modes.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c2-b584-e6bcce2d1294"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8072-8164-fac5bdec5532" class="">1. Transport Mode</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8058-8388-fafd2ae1a1c1" class="">Only the <strong>payload of the IP packet is encrypted</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8022-b079-f57f0530c24e" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-85d1-eb54a224e620" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">IP Header | ESP Header | Encrypted Payload | ESP Trailer | Authentication Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805c-82a4-de17fa8c0892" class="">Used for <strong>host-to-host communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-baf0-f246c0ecb961"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-802c-aed9-d4d9cf88dcdb" class="">2. Tunnel Mode</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8021-857d-e5ad004e44d2" class="">The <strong>entire IP packet is encrypted</strong> and a new IP header is added.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807b-bcf1-f816eaf741a1" class="">Structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-ad7e-e8622323a57f" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">New IP Header | ESP Header | Encrypted (Original IP Header + Data) | ESP Trailer | Authentication Data</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805b-9ca3-e552b299571b" class="">Used in <strong>VPN communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80fe-a854-dabe7b5e4269"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-805d-87b1-da2f1053a9e1" class="">5. Working of ESP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8089-a57b-fba38b6b5b16" class="">Step-by-step process:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-a62a-de2c67dd2952" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Sender creates the IP packet.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c3-8595-fb8c8adb60da" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>ESP generates a <strong>sequence number</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8072-a5cc-c16ff36c736a" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The payload data is <strong>encrypted using a symmetric key</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8086-8071-d3cac6adf59f" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Authentication data (hash value) is generated.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80a8-a0f9-e79aff47931c" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>ESP header and trailer are added.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8015-9fb5-c3c138401a13" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li>Packet is transmitted.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8008-8ed8-cbe60508f397" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li>Receiver decrypts the packet and verifies integrity.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8036-881d-c5f1adf8d783"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8086-b98c-d0f4b5ac135d" class="">6. Advantages of ESP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8091-b3d7-d9bcd78dcc55" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>data confidentiality</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f4-99c1-cf6b1963889f" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Ensures <strong>authentication and integrity</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8091-b7ce-df61a09f3652" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Protects against <strong>replay attacks</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f0-8906-e345666d6bc5" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Widely used in <strong>VPNs</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8050-a470-d57e323ece9e" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>More secure than AH because it supports encryption</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80da-917a-d30230e105f7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-90f7-e565909f8acb" class="">7. Limitations of ESP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803c-baf3-f8052af713fb" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Requires <strong>additional processing for encryption and decryption</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-921a-c2552cc0a7c6" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Slight <strong>increase in packet size</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8046-be85-cad01e8a8880" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Needs <strong>key management mechanisms</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80df-9039-f2ad74530272"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-802e-a836-c70bfe7cddeb" class="">8. Applications of ESP</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8072-9b42-ea6abbdfc07d" class="">ESP is widely used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804c-88f3-c4b21aaca5f6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-b47d-e3fdc03eea2e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure remote access</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-8607-da3540060e0f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Corporate network security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8003-b054-e354c2242e23" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure communication between gateways</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803a-b867-d0cccdcefefa" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8073-b6a8-f1d2e2b6db56" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">7. Key Management in </mark><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>IPsec</strong></mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-80e1-b591-e8e77bbc5123"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80cc-8894-f16b8926e90d" class="">Key Management in <strong>IPsec</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f2-a5b3-df0991133ab8" class=""><strong>Key Management</strong> refers to the process of <strong>generating, distributing, storing, updating, and deleting cryptographic keys</strong> used to secure communication between systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ec-8959-d9a90a2e91ca" class="">In IPsec, key management ensures that <strong>both communicating parties share the correct encryption and authentication keys securely</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8093-8cba-d35c6a9cafca" class="">Without proper key management, secure protocols like IPsec cannot function effectively.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8037-b17d-f5abb38bb11a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80c6-9d73-ce2dd684bb9a" class="">1. Objectives of Key Management</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8096-ae13-c7a2ae46f70d" class="">The main objectives are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803b-a56e-d6bf2fd1e528" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Secure key generation</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8004-9d58-c9276f3894ca" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Safe distribution of keys</strong> between communicating parties</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ed-bbdd-c89fcfdd052f" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Periodic updating of keys</strong> to maintain security</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8031-ad47-e35529e846af" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Protection of keys</strong> from unauthorized access</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80c4-b05f-efc143e9cda1" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Secure deletion of expired keys</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80fb-8425-cac9455cb379"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8031-a5a3-c605d217802c" class="">2. Types of Key Management</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8010-8c7e-cde97ea06921" class="">Key management in IPsec can be performed in two ways:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80e3-b827-d95946821db0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80f5-b793-e63591a9af96" class="">1. Manual Key Management</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80cb-a5f1-fa2e0dcc3af8" class="">In this method, the <strong>system administrator manually configures the keys</strong> in each device.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8042-a69c-e28938bcecd3" class="">Characteristics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809a-afbd-cea4e2ca1a15" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Keys are entered manually</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8017-b21a-d38962edd55f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">No automatic key exchange</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e8-8ad7-cf03d7298de3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Suitable for <strong>small and static networks</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-804e-a55a-f17e9e3f1684" class="">Limitations</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8050-93e1-c1dd488e3156" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Difficult to manage in large networks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-805d-b22f-f3ac79517587" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">No automatic key update</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804f-8a1e-f75e4c9d0f3c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Less secure compared to automated methods</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-809d-b75d-ca749e15350c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8038-8690-e7a6860035f7" class="">2. Automatic Key Management</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-806c-a5e2-d03582c99d47" class="">In this method, keys are <strong>automatically generated and exchanged between systems</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-800a-b6e1-fe15bb407e99" class="">This is done using the <strong>Internet Key Exchange</strong> protocol.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8068-8e59-cfb6dc97df77" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8058-b111-c67bbab63f88" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Automatic key generation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8004-94dc-f0869dbe16a1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure key exchange</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8038-97ee-ff13b4608ad1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Supports dynamic network environments</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-807f-8f7e-cb5fe5e2b09d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8015-a01c-c1962bec6eb5" class="">3. Internet Key Exchange (IKE)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ba-a29e-cae89218b74a" class=""><strong>IKE</strong> is a protocol used to <strong>establish, negotiate, and manage Security Associations (SA)</strong> in IPsec.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-801f-98f0-ddfbdb7673a1" class="">It performs the following functions:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8025-8606-cbd020a6752b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authentication of communicating parties</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8093-a8b7-f066cd7e4c6d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Negotiation of encryption algorithms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8095-bdf4-cbcc55494108" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure exchange of cryptographic keys</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b9-b93b-d0cbb83685b5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Creation of Security Associations</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8092-9793-ee918b43f1bf"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80d2-8013-f19c88cd265c" class="">4. Phases of IKE</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ed-ad82-d90ea2caf77d" class="">IKE works in two main phases.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8085-ab81-d785a3222873"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-807d-8149-d74e4ed617d3" class="">Phase 1: Establishing a Secure Channel</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a5-9717-f26869c1f19f" class="">In this phase:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803f-affc-d0af424632ed" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Two systems authenticate each other.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801c-a332-f8e6d964c15f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A <strong>secure communication channel</strong> is established.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80da-8d95-cc2b7643689b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A <strong>Security Association (SA)</strong> is created.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80df-80a9-f39382c7d69b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-b4aa-e650ebabed8f" class="">Phase 2: Establishing IPsec Security Association</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-803b-89cc-e9c5da095957" class="">In this phase:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c1-85fb-c7b73f92a282" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Parameters for IPsec communication are negotiated.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-804f-9626-c843d229c1ec" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption and authentication algorithms are selected.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8063-acbe-dee86b2c4cc6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Session keys are generated for data protection.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808d-b647-c11895c945ca"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8039-8064-fe2483af0d5a" class="">5. Key Exchange Techniques</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80af-9122-d68ac369f591" class="">Some cryptographic techniques used in key management include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8085-8e17-eb2acd5199e9" class="">1. <strong>Diffie–Hellman key exchange</strong></h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-805c-8249-f967cac64b2e" class="">Allows two parties to <strong>securely generate a shared secret key over an insecure channel</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8020-bbdb-d941bc202180"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-804b-99aa-c5b53b3a1b44" class="">2. Public Key Cryptography</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80aa-99e8-e5710fffce70" class="">Used for authentication and secure key exchange.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8054-bc5b-fc8f41be9982" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8014-b892-d731d367f90c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>RSA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a8-b09c-f4563a60e812"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-800e-9bb9-d2abb865d5f2" class="">6. Key Lifetime</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-9542-f7a7a8f4d9a0" class="">Every cryptographic key has a <strong>limited lifetime</strong> to reduce security risks.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8013-ae8f-cb3af2f9ff59" class="">After the lifetime expires:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-a4c5-d2d166220dba" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A <strong>new key is generated</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8063-9e7e-d23d1a302ba9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">The old key is <strong>discarded</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8014-a4e1-eac1074a155d" class="">This process is called <strong>key renewal or rekeying</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8074-973d-c7680f3042ee"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8082-9fa2-d691d956e1a5" class="">7. Importance of Key Management</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8030-bc28-edf7e756e41f" class="">Proper key management ensures:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f1-bf31-e4a53b0f629d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure communication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8097-8627-f5971540d64c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protection from key compromise</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8038-b3fc-fd93d832d562" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Prevention of unauthorized access</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d4-b0e2-fe183fc1fc64" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Long-term system security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8081-b2e3-e4e0ebd85b8d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8016-b041-d7b14735ee1b" class="">8. Applications of Key Management</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-9e50-fe770e736857" class="">Key management is used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8060-b288-e2bc702b03b4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>IPsec VPNs</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801a-9f92-f8151b205ab5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure email systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-948c-edc78c8d67e2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure web communication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-bb7f-c6e50212bc79" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Corporate network security</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807f-8a70-f02ebf69e8ef" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-b83d-e5e18afabcf2" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8017-b1e5-fc232317d975" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">8. Introduction to </mark><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>Secure Sockets Layer</strong></mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-8031-906c-d3e72b9ac8ba"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-99d2-c0ba0a6f3400" class="">Introduction to <strong>Secure Sockets Layer</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b6-82c9-c8f55a37311d" class=""><strong>Secure Sockets Layer (SSL)</strong> is a <strong>security protocol used to establish an encrypted connection between a client and a server over the internet</strong>. It ensures that data transmitted between them remains <strong>private, secure, and protected from attackers</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ca-b336-e1fba8398266" class="">SSL was originally developed by <strong>Netscape</strong> to secure web communications.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a0-acbc-cb9517c938dd" class="">Today, SSL has largely been replaced by its successor <strong>Transport Layer Security</strong>, but the term <em>SSL</em> is still commonly used to refer to secure web connections.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8076-8739-cc0f4db89181"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80a4-8924-ef70751cc874" class="">1. Purpose of SSL</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8006-a27b-fcdaae1ea66e" class="">The main purpose of SSL is to <strong>protect sensitive data during transmission over networks such as the Internet</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e1-83fd-dd6e2dc708e1" class="">It provides:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8070-a652-d5ef820014d3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Confidentiality</strong> (data encryption)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80af-881f-c2ba27467e6c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Authentication</strong> (verifying the identity of the server or client)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8048-8444-e89bc0fbebe5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Data Integrity</strong> (ensuring data is not modified)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80c4-8797-c525ce70f4aa"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8017-8247-de9b1e181fa5" class="">2. Working Principle of SSL</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80ab-88b9-df3ded82d569" class="">SSL works by creating a <strong>secure encrypted connection between a client (browser) and a server (website)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808f-b425-fb5d5ab3c0f6" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8049-a2ee-dea82532eeda" class="">When you visit a secure website such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8073-964a-f6268786edc3" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">https://example.com</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8000-b419-e13df072a720" class="">The <strong>HTTPS protocol</strong> uses SSL/TLS to secure the communication.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8083-a68c-f7aa0300679a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-911e-ef77c10463b6" class="">3. SSL Architecture</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-802c-be16-f0b2f69146c7" class="">SSL operates <strong>between the application layer and the transport layer</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-804f-b596-da6c411b2b5c" class="">Layer structure:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><pre id="322f2fef-a309-8078-b866-ccfc6cd11739" class="code code-wrap"><code style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">Application Layer (HTTP, FTP, SMTP)
          |
          v
SSL Layer
          |
          v
Transport Layer (TCP)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8028-b3bd-c523f139c373" class="">This allows SSL to <strong>secure many internet applications</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8028-9d3a-e2b0f3067bd9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-806d-9f05-f6c198057843" class="">4. Components of SSL Architecture</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8053-910b-f6a8dc78ff7a" class="">SSL architecture contains two main layers.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80f2-b75a-f2de05f51f56"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8053-bf3f-f4f6b5064d7e" class="">1. SSL Record Protocol</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8034-a46b-df3d2c4e8515" class="">The <strong>SSL Record Protocol</strong> provides basic security services such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801f-8f11-f2c93d5f3344" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encryption of data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809a-866e-df511204b3f6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data integrity</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8021-a6f8-f474dc10be41" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Fragmentation and compression</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80cd-a11a-e4646941c3e0" class="">Functions:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-a6a0-fdbf48d3be52" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Fragment data into blocks</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8003-b688-d0340ccb9a62" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Compress data</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8038-b5fb-d4a8cb5c898c" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Add message authentication code (MAC)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-803d-999e-da4483bcec35" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Encrypt the data</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8023-a1a9-ceab69e92776" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Transmit securely</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802e-b522-f51ba7d30abe"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-9c16-eaefaea272c0" class="">2. SSL Handshake Protocol</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c8-a83b-dcd03de3e72a" class="">The <strong>SSL Handshake Protocol</strong> is responsible for:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f7-a08f-e7241a04db8d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Authenticating the server (and optionally the client)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803d-a8ae-c989d860c2de" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Negotiating encryption algorithms</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8084-9ae0-e3b697d9dc12" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Exchanging cryptographic keys</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b5-88ea-fe15d0a703f7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-805b-8478-e0f91b58c670" class="">5. SSL Handshake Process</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b1-b05d-f9cdfc35bb53" class="">Steps involved:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8018-8a4a-c41d7d9a6694" class="">Step 1: Client Hello</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c9-8522-ca9c0f328d34" class="">Client sends:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-9464-ea664d5789bd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">SSL version</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-809c-a0ba-e9fb9d5c6a01" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Supported cipher suites</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-abc7-f5d879fb60cd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Random number</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-807f-8ff7-f8ac901e7530"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fe-ad3e-dbf942c7f52b" class="">Step 2: Server Hello</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fa-b637-c680f2d5f042" class="">Server responds with:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-851c-c90b49b9e3ec" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Selected cipher suite</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8030-8a01-ef52efcf763d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Digital certificate</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8026-82c9-e3d90561d868" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Random number</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80a9-8b72-f7eb126eb42a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8074-90f6-f2bb9703f75f" class="">Step 3: Certificate Verification</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d7-b28b-edabc2c96c23" class="">Client verifies the server&#x27;s certificate issued by a <strong>Certificate Authority (CA)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8095-af3e-fbd19ef5cf5e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8054-b9cb-e3dcbc321815" class="">Step 4: Key Exchange</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8081-ac9f-fd382b09beaf" class="">Client and server exchange keys to generate a <strong>shared session key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-808d-9c1a-d862c4f434d3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80f6-aa26-ecfb00d2bdf8" class="">Step 5: Secure Communication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e7-8e94-c4a2dc67aef1" class="">All communication is encrypted using the <strong>session key</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8073-885b-d4f04382caed"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-806e-ba00-e09928e1653d" class="">6. SSL Protocol Components</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-b733-e2569512818d" class="">SSL architecture consists of several sub-protocols:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-bc00-c32af65575fb" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Handshake Protocol</strong> – establishes secure session</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f2-a62d-f34795c272f7" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Record Protocol</strong> – provides encryption and integrity</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-804f-a17e-ca9e8e6542ec" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Alert Protocol</strong> – reports errors and warnings</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d9-8ed2-f5c6ecb375a9" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Change Cipher Spec Protocol</strong> – activates encryption settings</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8054-81b2-c40d0109130c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ff-96f0-f1cd8b5d74fc" class="">7. Advantages of SSL</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-bc7c-f8d75ce7f79b" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Protects <strong>sensitive information</strong> like passwords and credit cards</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80de-b414-e8726daf282e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Ensures <strong>secure communication over the internet</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-808e-807b-c415e51eef9e" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Provides <strong>authentication of servers</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80da-a2ca-f3abcd57bf56" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Prevents <strong>data tampering</strong></li></ol></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809b-87e0-ebf6bf0cbe83" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-8051-b7dd-cc23e2e7dd59" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue"><strong>10. Secure Electronic Transaction</strong></mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="322f2fef-a309-800b-831d-d00b724d9e1c"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="322f2fef-a309-80a3-a93d-c0a0a37d0841" class=""><strong>Secure Electronic Transaction</strong></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a5-b93a-d730f7933bca" class=""><strong>Secure Electronic Transaction (SET)</strong> is a <strong>security protocol designed to ensure safe and secure payment transactions over the Internet</strong>, especially for <strong>credit card payments in e-commerce</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808c-962e-e66635a66cca" class="">SET was developed jointly by <strong>Visa</strong> and <strong>Mastercard</strong> to protect financial information during online transactions.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-807d-ba1e-ccae7111de51" class="">It uses <strong>encryption, digital certificates, and authentication mechanisms</strong> to ensure that payment information is transmitted securely.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8072-9027-e1ee36564844"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80ae-aa05-dde1679f331e" class="">1. Objectives of SET</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8001-a045-dab47e8c9ddc" class="">The main objectives of Secure Electronic Transaction are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-807a-ba6a-e397645abd89" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Confidentiality of payment information</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-af30-c0fc49eb7bc7" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Authentication of cardholder and merchant</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8096-9223-f139d7a25118" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Data integrity</strong> during transmission</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8015-b06b-fb9458426c86" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Secure payment processing</strong> over the internet</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8088-a2e9-dd26ea1b40ad"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80de-b533-cc6afdd352bd" class="">2. Participants in SET System</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-afaf-f8230480e628" class="">1. Cardholder</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-a9cb-ce77d619ff2f" class="">The customer who wants to purchase goods online using a credit card.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-a2f1-e344ddfc7236"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8011-90c0-eccfb142a787" class="">2. Merchant</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8044-b9be-e417d299dc22" class="">The seller who provides goods or services through an online store.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b0-a913-f2b03f0b5159"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8020-8d6c-dee9d48b6950" class="">3. Issuer</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-b8d3-ca0892ddf271" class="">The bank that issued the customer’s credit card.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8052-b804-ecca140f2e44"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8010-bc03-f816f8eae70d" class="">4. Acquirer</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80e0-8f96-d20ec7eff445" class="">The bank that processes payments for the merchant.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8093-90ec-e7ea8e779192"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-ab68-e60120c401a2" class="">5. Payment Gateway</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80bb-b34d-da44638500aa" class="">A system that connects the merchant with the bank to authorize transactions.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80f1-ad1b-c363297445fd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80c5-8944-e3fa22e2aeaa" class="">6. Certificate Authority (CA)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8026-a16c-c88128286b3e" class="">A trusted organization that issues <strong>digital certificates</strong> to verify identities.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8041-ac21-f6f6c3365dc5"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-808a-bc60-c98f47ec119a" class="">3. Key Features of SET</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-805e-ad75-f26a05b9ab24" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> – payment information is encrypted</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-a66c-eadbe6596118" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Authentication</strong> – verifies identity of customer and merchant</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80e4-81f6-c21a29ddfcb0" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Integrity</strong> – ensures transaction data is not modified</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-8f11-f69018dc552c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Interoperability</strong> – works across different systems</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80ac-9801-e9cfb3f3cd2f" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Digital Certificates</strong> – used for identity verification</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ff-8dd7-f6cdfdb04b16"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-805a-8a52-c06e8edd9c23" class="">4. Cryptographic Techniques Used in SET</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8028-b671-d31cd971a3f5" class="">SET uses multiple cryptographic techniques such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80e9-8766-cb1b51120b70" class="">1. Symmetric Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8095-a85e-c16980ed9c4d" class="">Used to encrypt transaction data efficiently.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8077-9e31-c8ec0d4c1395"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8097-a643-c22c7665c06f" class="">2. Public Key Cryptography</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8070-9e08-fe10199b88fd" class="">Used for secure key exchange.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-809e-b1f7-c4041573294c" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-a083-f87ddc82cecd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>RSA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802e-afd5-c53249dfc00c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80c2-9027-daef5d1406ee" class="">3. Hash Functions</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f7-b243-d815e8c19f7d" class="">Used to generate message digests.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80a7-924c-d9af422434c1" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80b0-a1b8-e95a0b0f5c4d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>SHA</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8098-9ddc-d3a2acd47442"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8020-b01c-ec013234209c" class="">4. Digital Signatures</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b7-827a-d3a394cb866a" class="">Used to verify the identity of the sender and ensure message integrity.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80d0-aa15-cf2d3ce7146f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8062-9023-e6e3f7015ebb" class="">5. Dual Signature Concept</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8039-9430-ed0a0e7ef8fb" class="">SET introduces an important concept called <strong>Dual Signature</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8054-a0bd-e40c5b364175" class="">It links two important pieces of information:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-806e-8ec2-f2247cb22e41" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Order Information (OI)</strong> – details of the purchase</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80bc-9c99-ffea330f8694" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Payment Information (PI)</strong> – credit card details</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8078-a608-dbeb54c8fd0e" class="">The dual signature ensures that:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-8024-a8fa-cf1adc37304e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Merchant <strong>cannot see credit card details</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801d-aed8-f8a328cf8414" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Bank <strong>cannot see order details</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-808f-b5b4-d7430fb45fc6" class="">This protects user privacy.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80e1-a662-ec92610f2d74"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-a5c2-e3f99ab61e79" class="">6. Working of SET (Transaction Process)</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d8-9dad-c18e465da4fc" class="">Step 1: Customer places order</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c6-85a3-d3510305b255" class="">Customer selects items from the merchant website.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8044-a686-d2a6ffb104f2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80c3-9543-c88639d89013" class="">Step 2: Customer sends order and payment information</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80fc-8867-fa79ce080125" class="">Order and payment data are <strong>encrypted and digitally signed</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-802d-a55a-d56cef671bc3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-8058-a1b5-e0c8f36b1aa7" class="">Step 3: Merchant verifies the customer</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80c7-a50e-e2ae309c89b3" class="">Merchant verifies the customer’s digital certificate.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8092-9ecc-dc9be6ec951e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-92e0-e1fac5904bfe" class="">Step 4: Merchant sends payment request</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-8028-9e98-e58f746d5cdc" class="">Merchant sends payment details to the <strong>payment gateway</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80b2-82f0-f90a8602f99c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-801c-8cc7-f5ed373cedc0" class="">Step 5: Bank authorization</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f3-9647-eaf138e06600" class="">The bank verifies the transaction and authorizes payment.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-8040-ab6b-f33f5ac9481c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="322f2fef-a309-80b3-bc91-d21d5be36bfc" class="">Step 6: Transaction completion</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80f7-b398-f93efa1ba361" class="">Merchant confirms the order and delivers goods.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80af-85c1-d16907e96e42"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8022-b11a-f0e816637498" class="">7. Advantages of SET</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8096-ba34-c7fed6ddf300" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Provides <strong>secure online payment transactions</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80f2-89aa-c8eb60c062e2" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Ensures <strong>authentication of both buyer and seller</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-808c-befd-cc3db3a6ccf7" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Protects <strong>credit card information</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8060-9217-d8d816f100a5" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Maintains <strong>privacy of transaction data</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ef-a155-d869779fd6af"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-8031-9832-e31837bcef18" class="">8. Limitations of SET</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-808a-a9ea-ed63dc4a0be6" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Complex implementation</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-80d3-8413-c5b1e664c865" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Requires <strong>digital certificates</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8010-9af8-c871d0d0802b" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>High <strong>computational overhead</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="322f2fef-a309-8006-a5a8-c6c0332a9b4d" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Less widely used today compared to modern payment protocols</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="322f2fef-a309-80ad-bb99-d38296a20289"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="322f2fef-a309-80d5-a2dc-cba39c3025a5" class="">9. Applications of SET</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="322f2fef-a309-80b4-a656-e1c004c31ea9" class="">SET is mainly used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-803c-be12-c9736aae8466" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Online credit card payments</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-801b-83f0-e6f9e1845806" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">E-commerce systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="322f2fef-a309-80f8-bb2c-ecbfeb8b7c31" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure financial transactions</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-802e-b50b-d69c047589f6" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-803d-8161-f33a1b9721ad"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-8078-9cd7-fd54ebc26a24" class=""><strong>Module 5 : Computational Learning Theory</strong></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d0-b18e-deccd5f0b86f" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8030-90bf-fa233c0d82ad" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">System Security: Introductory Idea of Intrusion</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8092-a4e0-cca0aa5ba93f" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Intrusion Detection Techniques</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-809e-9d55-ee2a74935bd7" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li> <mark class="highlight-blue">Viruses and Worms</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-803d-a396-f40929738db0" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Firewalls</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8067-b093-f41e9804811d" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Design Principles of Firewalls</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-83b5-c195c5579aa4" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> IT Acts and Cyber Laws (Global Standards)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8058-9181-da671a2bdc45" class="numbered-list" start="7"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Virtual Private Network</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8022-b630-c4ef579ec369" class="numbered-list" start="8"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Recent attacks on networks (Packet Analyzer, Buffer Overflow and Port Scanning)</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8041-a847-c76eb6e78938" class="numbered-list" start="9"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Blockchain Technology</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-804d-a33e-fd2a8b825c34" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="10"><li> <mark class="highlight-teal">Preventing Privacy and Piracy on Internet</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-803e-9957-de4751e38bea" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="11"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Brief Overview of Cyber Forensic</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8006-9fb3-dba4fe0e263c" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="12"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Cyber Forensic Processes</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8012-ba6c-e6861dccfff5" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="13"><li><mark class="highlight-teal"> Cyber Forensic Applications and Cyber Forensic Tools</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8059-808b-dc7da4c4a684" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="14"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Digital Evidence</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-801c-afcc-f325200d3b45" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="15"><li><mark class="highlight-teal">Investigating Windows and Unix Systems</mark></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-b8a5-f76a66aef693" class="numbered-list numbered-list-digits-2" start="16"><li>Email Tracing.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-806e-9387-e0c2a7931622" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-80df-a7de-d032b2c98918" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">System Security: Introductory Idea of Intrusion</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-80ed-9b0e-d82bb36964bf"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8031-9b8b-f140110a41ab" class="">System Security: Introductory Idea of Intrusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-806f-9e44-ecae7085b1ef" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-806b-83f9-fc22b8a4d974" class="">In computer and network security, <strong>intrusion</strong> refers to any unauthorized activity that compromises the <strong>confidentiality, integrity, or availability of a system or network</strong>. An intrusion occurs when an attacker gains access to a system without permission and attempts to misuse resources, steal information, or disrupt normal operations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8029-9ec3-cc535138ec38" class="">Intrusions are a major concern in modern information systems because organizations store sensitive data such as financial records, personal information, and intellectual property in digital form.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8064-b77f-eb0ad6093daf"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-807d-acc5-d4c624c06b66" class="">2. Meaning of Intrusion</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8055-9d27-c540dc26145c" class="">An <strong>intrusion</strong> is defined as <strong>any attempt to access a computer system, network, or data without authorization, or any activity that violates the security policy of the system</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80fa-b8b2-cd7d06d233c6" class="">The person who performs such activities is called an <strong>intruder or attacker</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8006-95d8-c7855504a5db" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8021-a9a5-fb43184d0a58" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Unauthorized login to a system</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80bc-9b73-c7d6dce8d6c2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Stealing confidential data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801d-acf3-e6e8762a2d0a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Modifying or deleting important files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8042-a3da-fabd86c37c6e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Installing malicious software</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8046-b736-e348b5f87854"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8088-b684-eacdf40bd81d" class="">3. Types of Intruders</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803f-b195-e1de29707205" class="">Intruders can generally be classified into three main categories:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80c1-8b54-db334f76a04f" class="">1. Masquerader</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a6-8d05-e2f7e73b40cd" class="">A <strong>masquerader</strong> is a person who gains access to a system by pretending to be an authorized user.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8042-9826-e76dca967387" class="">Characteristics:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-805a-8b4e-fc4140d7c75a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Uses stolen login credentials (username and password).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-800a-ab56-c9a48bc8f27d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Accesses the system illegally.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-a5ad-ed464568f823" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Usually an outsider.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8016-91db-e02046ccadf7" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8048-9320-d7c50bce88a6" class="">A hacker using another employee’s login credentials to access company data.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-808f-be92-d977199b8d0d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8086-ac92-f83864301913" class="">2. Misfeasor</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8072-9466-e80926e0c02f" class="">A <strong>misfeasor</strong> is a legitimate user who accesses data or resources <strong>beyond their authorized privileges</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80dc-a33e-f09fe3f6398a" class="">Characteristics:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8090-ab4f-c9509f66b753" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Already has a valid account.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80af-aa51-cb0bca94fda6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Misuses system privileges.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8096-90f6-cb5107be43b4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Usually an insider.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805a-8124-d7040165f0cf" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80f0-a745-e6a4cae1faa7" class="">An employee accessing confidential financial records that they are not permitted to see.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f1-83d2-c40575827a22"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-802d-85b3-c45a51d45553" class="">3. Clandestine User</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-808f-a197-ccc45659a99c" class="">A <strong>clandestine user</strong> is an intruder who gains <strong>administrative control of a system</strong> and hides their activity.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8074-9b24-ef7f26dd128b" class="">Characteristics:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ae-83d7-d706cc12cdbb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Gains supervisor or root privileges.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8070-bd41-c0ebeb113684" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Can bypass security controls.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-805e-ab63-caabc04e5a6a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Can hide intrusion activities.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8005-be4d-c81e3b605e36" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ed-84c1-ca35a1328f94" class="">An attacker installing a backdoor to maintain secret access to a system.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c5-bc09-c52616945378"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80d9-9cec-ff5531facf08" class="">4. Techniques Used by Intruders</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-806f-babd-d863a4f961aa" class="">Intruders use various methods to attack systems, such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80cd-90bc-cbe2b8cf4dee" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Password Guessing</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8003-8c70-d4fd6b33d44b" class="">Trying different passwords until the correct one is found.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80e9-91c9-c2b9b2a0d05e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Social Engineering</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b3-ac05-e8a9330e09b3" class="">Manipulating people to reveal confidential information.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-805c-a5c8-d13a6fbb9ff1" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Malware Attacks</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8021-9860-f645fcc4e1ef" class="">Installing viruses, worms, or trojans to gain access.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8010-9080-fd17daa44265" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Exploiting Software Vulnerabilities</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801f-93d7-e83579224b66" class="">Using weaknesses in software to enter a system.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80a5-8883-d9d02360ff55" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Network Attacks</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-9676-feb82d1bffe3" class="">Intercepting or modifying network traffic.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8012-b520-e7b3a654a866"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-8e6e-cf9ef5f91a42" class="">5. Intrusion Detection System (IDS)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807f-a8e8-c445120e6ce2" class="">To detect intrusions, organizations use an <strong>Intrusion Detection System (IDS)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803b-860e-f63232b1eef6" class="">An IDS is a <strong>security system that monitors network or system activities to detect suspicious behavior or policy violations</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-808e-9d72-f2e1bc26caac" class="">Types of IDS:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80f5-843f-e87699dc8433" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Host-Based IDS (HIDS)</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8018-94ea-e0fa20015ae2" class="">Monitors activities on a single computer or host.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8012-82a0-fbe513c96bd9" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Network-Based IDS (NIDS)</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ad-9a67-d414042e8d25" class="">Monitors network traffic to detect attacks.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80a5-8c1c-c1cf31e632fd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8021-bad3-e775fdd8aaf0" class="">6. Importance of Intrusion Detection</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805e-bbce-de266cfb59f1" class="">Intrusion detection is important because it helps to:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a8-86e8-d748bf4ebfc6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Detect unauthorized access to systems</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d6-8d62-c6b92d4c8931" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protect sensitive information</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c6-a5c2-fb9e54d677ff" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Prevent system damage or data loss</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e5-a27d-d5d02d4583a8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Maintain network security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b2-a19e-ccf2fe594099" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Alert administrators about suspicious activitie</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8039-a8e7-e360b09a909d" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-8036-a8c9-d893fb618883" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">2. Intrusion Detection Techniques</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8054-af69-d5afe15a00fd"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80c5-bbb5-d6f582c7e638" class="">Intrusion Detection Techniques</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-803e-bce6-c1fdaee86946" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d6-b3aa-e50d9017e509" class=""><strong>Intrusion Detection Techniques</strong> are methods used by an <strong>Intrusion Detection System (IDS)</strong> to identify unauthorized activities or attacks in a computer system or network. These techniques monitor system behavior, network traffic, and user activities to detect security violations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80f5-90ec-c3f997de6288" class="">The main goal is to <strong>identify suspicious activities and alert the system administrator before serious damage occurs</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8027-af0a-f1d65b3c29c8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8004-ae4f-eabc2b722332" class="">2. Types of Intrusion Detection Techniques</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-802d-b845-cc14c73a42e1" class="">There are two primary techniques used for intrusion detection:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-bb1d-eaa7df2f701f" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Anomaly Detection</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80cc-a101-e12d8ece35d6" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Signature (Misuse) Detection</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80be-bf5b-e2f491f2ca46"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8093-9071-ca060d1061bb" class="">3. Anomaly Detection Technique</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8016-abe6-f3a594813080" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8087-8672-ec5e2c023c24" class=""><strong>Anomaly detection</strong> identifies intrusions by detecting <strong>deviations from normal system behavior</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80db-98e5-ddfa88c41962" class="">In this technique, the system first learns the <strong>normal behavior of users, networks, and systems</strong>. If any activity significantly differs from this normal behavior, it is treated as a potential intrusion.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-808c-90ac-d02edb1d6748" class="">How it Works</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8045-879e-fac139ea1c8e" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Establish a profile of normal system behavior.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-805b-a114-fb23dd9ab75e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Monitor current activities.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80b6-9bf5-d451c7dac853" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Compare current behavior with the normal profile.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8096-b6c7-c49dcad2ea2e" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>If there is a large deviation, an alert is generated.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-807d-8776-f153865006f1" class="">Example</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8075-aa3c-e07fa8228be7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A user normally logs in between <strong>9 AM and 5 PM</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f3-9e95-de6633de430f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">If login occurs at <strong>3 AM</strong>, the system flags it as suspicious.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-800c-a663-eb1de9a12fbe" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ae-9fce-e995a5ee30fe" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Can detect <strong>new or unknown attacks</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802d-8f8c-cbac8a9a53a7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Useful for identifying unusual patterns.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-809e-9aa9-c2bb2381e6a6" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804c-b618-da94c0578405" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">May produce <strong>false alarms</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-b0ff-e87fbf41df3a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires time to build normal behavior profiles.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80bb-801e-e732c31e4f39"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80bb-b3fe-dbed2afe1f97" class="">4. Signature-Based Detection Technique</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8050-a4c9-cc93e7d29d93" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8073-94b1-ce43393d6f73" class=""><strong>Signature detection</strong> identifies intrusions by comparing system activities with a <strong>database of known attack patterns (signatures)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ce-9b04-ced4553c7447" class="">If the system finds a pattern that matches a known attack signature, it triggers an alert.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8039-ada2-f49673beba7d" class="">How it Works</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8069-8864-d96c413b526e" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Maintain a database of known attack signatures.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8036-a256-e3f9d24c279f" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Monitor system or network activity.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80c1-8243-dbfe7e2c0f9f" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Compare activity with stored signatures.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8011-a1b9-f9006877d715" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Generate alert when a match is found.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80b0-966e-dfa3d9d1ed8a" class="">Example</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-802c-99fe-f4d9112faf96" class="">If a network packet matches the pattern of a <strong>known malware attack</strong>, the IDS detects it immediately.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-800a-8165-f13ed3a3e4bb" class="">Advantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f6-8a20-e45b74815496" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Highly accurate</strong> for known attacks.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8096-9163-c4307d476b6d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Produces <strong>fewer false alarms</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8019-bc5f-f83809760a45" class="">Disadvantages</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808b-841b-d4fa3e899c85" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cannot detect <strong>new or unknown attacks</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8047-99dc-dbab8c93e3f4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires <strong>regular updates of the signature database</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-801e-8ecd-fef8ab39aec9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80de-afab-dc2b84fcf36c" class="">5. Hybrid Detection Technique</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8062-b614-e5533588e9d4" class="">Many modern IDS systems use a <strong>hybrid approach</strong>, which combines <strong>anomaly detection and signature detection</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-802f-8d51-ef8aabe64d73" class="">Benefits</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8051-b48b-e028aec994da" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Detects <strong>both known and unknown attacks</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d8-96b9-ceec64d9860c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improves overall security.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f6-9f8b-f02650701253"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80d7-a2b0-c6a76b067d5a" class="">6. Comparison of Intrusion Detection Techniques</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="323f2fef-a309-80e9-b819-f9896dbeba19" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8047-9e02-e848624c363c"><th id="vb_e" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Feature</th><th id="{Dqm" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Anomaly Detection</th><th id="z^&gt;C" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Signature Detection</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8048-8fa4-c5ff5214d62d"><td id="vb_e" class="">Detection Type</td><td id="{Dqm" class="">Detects unusual behavior</td><td id="z^&gt;C" class="">Detects known attacks</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-80d9-bbe7-e7b08864fed8"><td id="vb_e" class="">Ability to detect new attacks</td><td id="{Dqm" class="">Yes</td><td id="z^&gt;C" class="">No</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-80bc-aaa6-f0fc5a7d3c96"><td id="vb_e" class="">False alarms</td><td id="{Dqm" class="">Higher</td><td id="z^&gt;C" class="">Lower</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-80b1-bb0e-d762d4f73845"><td id="vb_e" class="">Database requirement</td><td id="{Dqm" class="">No</td><td id="z^&gt;C" class="">Yes</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80cd-8d24-d2cd8ec0ec4a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-8632-dfedd499339e" class="">7. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805b-8e4c-e007da672d49" class="">Intrusion detection techniques play a vital role in system security. <strong>Anomaly detection</strong> identifies unusual behavior, while <strong>signature detection</strong> identifies known attack patterns. Modern security systems often combine both techniques to improve accuracy and provide better protection against cyber threats.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8096-ad32-c2a5d5a2f72a" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803f-9f60-dda08cc69a50" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-8087-8f1f-f4d59eb703fc" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">3. Viruses and Worms</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-80e8-9dab-f68ceb3c36be"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8088-9c78-c6b079fccadc" class="">Viruses and Worms</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8024-bb76-d23cf32670cc" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805b-a51f-ef731a3fb4e0" class=""><strong>Viruses and worms</strong> are types of <strong>malicious software (malware)</strong> that spread through computer systems and networks. They are designed to <strong>damage systems, steal data, or disrupt normal operations</strong>. Both are major threats to computer security, but they differ in how they spread and operate.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8095-b970-f8864684c262"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-bfb8-fb709baddc81" class="">2. Computer Virus</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8030-8e51-d018c99b2cbc" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8010-a6ee-e5748ef96c4f" class="">A <strong>computer virus</strong> is a malicious program that <strong>attaches itself to a legitimate program or file</strong> and spreads when the infected program is executed.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8061-a410-e9ce1a367606" class="">Like a biological virus, it requires a <strong>host program</strong> to activate and replicate.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-808b-9214-d216fb19a77d" class="">Characteristics of a Virus</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804f-a452-d9fabf440d66" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires a <strong>host file or program</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8079-9c53-d4b0aea369ce" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Spreads when the infected file is executed.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c2-a364-e2d987fe80dd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Can <strong>modify or destroy data</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8021-a1ab-dddea4e9ecf4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Often spreads through <strong>email attachments, software downloads, or infected USB drives</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8044-9008-c1b43f9940af" class="">Types of Computer Viruses</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8052-b2df-e735ad70ac83" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Boot Sector Virus</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80c4-a71a-ed681c0db309" class="">Infects the boot sector of a hard disk.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8083-88f2-dbf450d92794" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>File Infector Virus</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b9-bc6e-c01de2d71270" class="">Attaches itself to executable files such as <code>.exe</code>.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-8f26-c47d96d8dd87" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Macro Virus</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8082-ab6b-c84b03d09651" class="">Infects documents that use macros (e.g., Word or Excel files).</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-809a-b683-ec02cb7d9d0a" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Multipartite Virus</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805b-a913-d171d01a6306" class="">Infects both boot sectors and files.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80d6-afa7-e2e7578dc4cf" class="">Example of Virus Activity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80be-8789-f2d0e07dec97" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Deleting files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80af-aa52-dddececf935d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Corrupting data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b5-8efe-f4946c197377" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Slowing down system performance</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8063-afd0-cd2fcfb66bb2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Displaying unwanted messages</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c2-b674-ce3b4090ed26"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80b9-b00d-f990659cb7a6" class="">3. Computer Worm</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80c6-baad-d246836ae746" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a9-812e-d1921c4abaab" class="">A <strong>computer worm</strong> is a type of malware that <strong>replicates itself automatically and spreads across networks without requiring a host program or user interaction</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8064-89cc-f79a203afbfd" class="">Characteristics of a Worm</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d3-b4dd-f66a8796aeac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Self-replicating</strong> program.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80dd-b30e-f1946fd97a82" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Does <strong>not need a host file</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8000-bebc-c8db5711ae8d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Spreads automatically through <strong>network connections</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-8fde-ca08c9496a2a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Consumes <strong>system resources and network bandwidth</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80e6-8586-dbbef3967988" class="">How Worms Spread</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f8-a4d6-f9e41346dc39" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Through <strong>email networks</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808b-9b59-c6bf4c49233f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Through <strong>security vulnerabilities</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8040-9018-c7acbfb124e2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Through <strong>shared networks or the internet</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80db-83d3-ee4d3b3ac911" class="">Effects of Worms</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8059-abfa-f40fbb9a441e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Slowing down networks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d2-b813-c93bc76978c6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Crashing servers</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-ad46-cd04b81c6117" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Consuming bandwidth</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8041-96de-ea9916af7cd6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Installing additional malware</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-808e-905e-cde79d0ac2f8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80a1-a817-e03f1a987cf8" class="">4. Difference Between Virus and Worm</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="323f2fef-a309-807c-aa85-ef95aa9df4ae" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8056-8f48-c2e59815352e"><th id="G_wX" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Feature</th><th id="[?wN" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Virus</th><th id="XTof" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Worm</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8025-ac29-dde6e8304b70"><td id="G_wX" class="">Dependency</td><td id="[?wN" class="">Needs a host program</td><td id="XTof" class="">Independent program</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-803d-90ec-d38af4a511a6"><td id="G_wX" class="">Activation</td><td id="[?wN" class="">Requires user action</td><td id="XTof" class="">Spreads automatically</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-80c3-a764-f5780cc5e652"><td id="G_wX" class="">Speed of Spread</td><td id="[?wN" class="">Slower</td><td id="XTof" class="">Faster</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-801a-a6f8-d92759ce079e"><td id="G_wX" class="">Method of Spread</td><td id="[?wN" class="">Through files or programs</td><td id="XTof" class="">Through networks</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8061-b33b-c47b51b46a0b"><td id="G_wX" class="">Impact</td><td id="[?wN" class="">Corrupts or modifies files</td><td id="XTof" class="">Consumes system and network resources</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8037-a0a2-c554e021fbed"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80f8-88d1-dc8d3b13f79d" class="">5. Prevention Methods</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-800b-addf-d6d7eecdcf07" class="">To protect systems from viruses and worms, the following security measures should be used:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8029-96ae-c7e02f0edaa9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Install <strong>antivirus software</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80be-a28f-c1695f1f24e6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Keep <strong>operating systems updated</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80bd-9b0a-ef9478aa43c1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Avoid opening <strong>unknown email attachments</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f7-aee5-c2062419d0ed" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use <strong>firewalls</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806c-a4cb-d5406fd192b7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Regularly <strong>scan the system for malware</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-805e-88ed-d1d69482b222" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Download software only from <strong>trusted sources</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80e8-9a96-f1a9e270d743"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8041-9955-caf11bb134ba" class="">6. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803a-9fcd-ed93b59424ab" class="">Viruses and worms are dangerous forms of malware that can harm computer systems and networks. A <strong>virus requires a host program to spread</strong>, while a <strong>worm spreads automatically through networks</strong>. Understanding these threats and implementing proper security measures can help protect systems from attacks.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8001-bc5d-fad7eb8c9e6b" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803d-8507-d9cda7c4c783" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-80ae-a041-edfe56a8d00e" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">4. Firewalls</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8015-8728-e57ca3d7f788"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80ef-b478-ce8d8235a85f" class="">Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8013-ab6f-ecdeb591db31" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a4-9a03-ccaa58df357d" class="">A <strong>firewall</strong> is a network security system that <strong>monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic</strong> based on predefined security rules. It acts as a <strong>barrier between a trusted internal network and an untrusted external network</strong>, such as the internet.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80fa-b009-efb4d9c414eb" class="">The main purpose of a firewall is to <strong>prevent unauthorized access while allowing legitimate communication</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8007-9c51-d4da4203a6c1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8017-af97-c3a225bdc67b" class="">2. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801c-96e8-e2b19ba3731c" class="">A <strong>firewall</strong> can be defined as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><blockquote id="323f2fef-a309-8003-aaf4-e3fc556bfb74" class="">A hardware or software security system that protects a computer network by filtering traffic according to security policies.</blockquote></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80ff-9150-f59fa0077956"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-808a-9585-ecee6c5f6563" class="">3. Objectives of Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805a-abf6-e16fb24f7d5e" class="">Firewalls are designed to achieve the following objectives:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-807a-97e7-e639ba58fdc4" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Access Control</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803d-8f75-f897b0f2b169" class="">Allow only authorized users and services to access the network.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-803a-8e00-e4ba80c3b7d5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Network Security</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80de-9785-d8e2e9168bf1" class="">Protect internal systems from external threats.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-807e-8494-f4f7d40adc5d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Monitoring and Logging</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8095-8d2e-c1d6b17ce086" class="">Record network activities for security analysis.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ad-8284-e48875d02f70" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Prevent Unauthorized Access</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809d-b32a-ee0877923302" class="">Block malicious traffic from hackers or attackers.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-bdf8-d0f66e6dd9d9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80dd-9218-d3faf10ccd5c" class="">4. Types of Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8074-8fb6-c2cdeb88a6bc" class="">1. Packet Filtering Firewall</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8051-86ea-f146a65f8615" class="">This is the <strong>simplest type of firewall</strong>. It examines packets of data and allows or blocks them based on rules such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-800a-bad3-d9e52b7bf0d1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Source IP address</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-a89a-ee56b4dff4fa" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Destination IP address</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8072-a86a-dcc6bf7e21a8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Port number</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f8-afc3-ffacd1d5869a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protocol type</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ad-bb32-e556c51fe036" class=""><strong>Advantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8013-8ee2-e457314ed4d4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Fast processing</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a2-9f7f-fef8298e2816" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Simple to implement</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8095-9cd8-c62a00c44978" class=""><strong>Disadvantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8099-8a00-cc38491d26a4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Limited security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8049-9af5-f5d83b0ada91" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cannot inspect packet content</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-806f-9ff0-ec0ba7e13906"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-af0f-f9d9a7949e19" class="">2. Stateful Inspection Firewall</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8067-b200-f07f6ace381a" class="">This firewall <strong>monitors the state of active connections</strong> and makes decisions based on the connection state.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8009-9d66-f08eb99860ad" class=""><strong>Advantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ed-8f89-eaadfad927df" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">More secure than packet filtering</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806a-aef6-e5846c34ebaa" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Tracks active sessions</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8090-a675-eeef166e54fb" class=""><strong>Disadvantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8037-9d9c-ced94e8082f3" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires more processing power</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-b021-e94840a0f6a2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8002-aa02-ecff87fe042d" class="">3. Application-Level Gateway (Proxy Firewall)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8066-b4ad-c1639452ac40" class="">This firewall operates at the <strong>application layer</strong> and acts as an intermediary between users and external servers.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e0-b4cb-d67b54b36263" class=""><strong>Advantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808e-b5b4-ec21dafee429" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides high security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80cb-8faa-d97a2a9b7c92" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Can inspect application data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-8578-c9f28e0c0ff2" class=""><strong>Disadvantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e3-9edc-d7efe8de0acf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Slower due to deep inspection</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8082-842e-ccc0f8203edd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80b7-8d52-d465bfb868f5" class="">4. Circuit-Level Gateway</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8062-8943-c5e310ea3b45" class="">This firewall monitors <strong>TCP handshakes and session connections</strong> to ensure that sessions are legitimate.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8053-8bf1-ebfbb1db09af" class=""><strong>Advantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8084-bd98-dfd45c4fe04a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Faster than proxy firewalls</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8046-9c46-cb1bc980e043" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides session-level security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-bcdb-debe3ddd5354" class=""><strong>Disadvantages</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-809e-9736-dd84f298cb00" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Does not inspect packet content</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-9008-f0969321398a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80e8-acf9-fbbe61884794" class="">5. How a Firewall Works</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8081-b0c8-c0c585173018" class="">The firewall works by <strong>filtering network traffic according to security rules</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8070-a2b0-ddcf2c02ac0e" class="">Steps:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-803f-864f-c01dc4dec0c1" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Data packets attempt to enter or leave the network.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-804c-b5b0-caa5d026e6d9" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The firewall checks each packet against security rules.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8092-9dcb-f678199ef8f0" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>If the packet follows the rules, it is <strong>allowed</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8041-addb-e27bacabb4c3" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>If it violates the rules, it is <strong>blocked</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80d7-abad-df43bc7a55b3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8055-9566-ea7303b2993a" class="">6. Advantages of Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8048-9979-e3f755330466" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects networks from hackers</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e9-9a28-eac0048caaa9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Controls network traffic</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-b9d4-f8de4f4e3933" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Prevents unauthorized access</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-a252-f65eb2c497d9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Monitors and logs network activities</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-ac9d-c10c37ac5386" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improves overall system security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8099-b40a-f524fea4179a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-808a-8dd4-cae916e83045" class="">7. Limitations of Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8006-83fc-c91ccdc27151" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cannot stop attacks from inside the network</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8031-ab63-ec783786cb8f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cannot protect against viruses in trusted files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80cc-8df1-e7f7b3ede806" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">May slow network performance</li></ul></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801b-967f-ff3953812e43" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-8054-8bd3-de42b3497f54" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">5. Design Principles of Firewalls</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-808c-a8fd-d2fd3375470a"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8058-89a5-d418dd3ece69" class="">Design Principles of Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8073-98f3-c2628b81e405" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8014-85eb-f94e7dce7092" class="">A <strong>firewall</strong> is a critical component of network security that protects internal networks from unauthorized access. The <strong>design principles of firewalls</strong> define the basic rules and guidelines that must be followed to ensure effective protection of computer systems and networks.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8092-beeb-face13405218" class="">These principles help in <strong>controlling network traffic, preventing attacks, and maintaining security policies</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8059-95f8-e39630ffec50"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8048-b996-cea28e8168e6" class="">2. Basic Design Principles of Firewalls</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b5-8b42-e5124226a552" class="">There are <strong>three fundamental design principles</strong> of a firewall.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8002-a6be-cb8a9d5a8ce1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-809f-a130-e9da0873e4f4" class="">1. All Traffic Must Pass Through the Firewall</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805d-a363-c0ae1526a248" class="">The first principle states that <strong>all network traffic entering or leaving the internal network must pass through the firewall</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8042-9371-f0506cf66c4a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">No direct communication should occur between internal and external networks without firewall inspection.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80dd-8388-cc073b4e6633" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">This ensures that <strong>every packet is checked according to security rules</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80fd-b4cf-d8d838ce0a13" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80de-be3e-e1a095822744" class="">If an external user tries to access a company server, the request must go through the firewall before reaching the server.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-95cc-f9bb6c5be9f0" class=""><strong>Benefit:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809f-a216-cb0928b8bb1f" class="">Prevents attackers from bypassing security controls.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80ed-98c1-c5eb01f0fc58"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-809b-80ba-faee91bee92e" class="">2. Only Authorized Traffic Is Allowed</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8059-9c0c-f80954264ec7" class="">The second principle states that <strong>only traffic that is explicitly permitted by the security policy should be allowed</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80fc-924e-fc9e891a7cb7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Any traffic that does not match the defined rules must be <strong>blocked</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8095-a790-db101290ac77" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">This principle is often called the <strong>“default deny policy.”</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a0-b1aa-c3b5216915dd" class=""><strong>Example:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8092-8fd7-f5472b8eb12f" class="">A firewall rule may allow:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803b-a014-f877c0393326" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">HTTP (port 80)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b2-bfee-c47581f257ac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">HTTPS (port 443)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8090-aec3-d17d1c2ed3d4" class="">All other ports will be <strong>blocked by default</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8032-89c0-d5337dcab173" class=""><strong>Benefit:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809d-8094-d5dfc72c42e8" class="">Reduces the chances of unauthorized access.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f9-93f1-c0b9a2128fba"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-bb92-ddb4533dd38f" class="">3. Firewall Must Be Secure Itself</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8035-85ee-e34c089e9f0c" class="">The firewall system itself must be <strong>highly secure and resistant to attacks</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8043-b9f5-f67a3ff5b707" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Only authorized administrators should manage it.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-807f-bf6a-c79459f5dc6f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">The firewall software should be regularly updated.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8059-a9e5-debbfe0ab5b2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">It should run on a <strong>hardened operating system</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803b-8713-ca848b6fd810" class=""><strong>Benefit:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8056-b178-ed85040338cc" class="">Ensures attackers cannot compromise the firewall and bypass security controls.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80a2-b234-c65427bf0d40"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-807d-baf8-ebc3f40439e7" class="">3. Additional Firewall Design Considerations</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80cc-8699-d8f183b51cbf" class="">1. Simplicity of Design</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807d-8561-f51ac1d46c7d" class="">Firewall rules and configurations should be <strong>simple and easy to manage</strong> to reduce configuration errors.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-808a-ab35-ed80682d473f" class="">2. Logging and Monitoring</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8032-b6ff-e857ccfae320" class="">The firewall should <strong>log all network activities</strong> for monitoring and security analysis.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80de-84b0-f5fa398ccea1" class="">3. Scalability</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801a-8024-d14d8580a2aa" class="">The firewall should support <strong>network expansion and increasing traffic</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80c7-ac6c-cf3e7f99fcad" class="">4. Performance Efficiency</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809b-a4b9-d458bfe7d6f5" class="">Firewall processing should <strong>not significantly slow down network performance</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80d3-b435-fd88a9c7317f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-800d-b261-e858e7fc5969" class="">4. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e9-b0e6-c158236c1acf" class="">The design principles of firewalls ensure that network security policies are effectively enforced. The three key principles are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-802c-b7c9-c7a306846630" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>All traffic must pass through the firewall.</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80f0-b303-fe0a283ba2c5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Only authorized traffic should be allowed.</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ea-a3d0-f0c372614d4a" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>The firewall itself must be secure.</strong></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80eb-af4d-f77bf680de0f" class="">By following these principles, organizations can create a <strong>strong defense against cyber threats and unauthorized network access</strong>.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809e-8b70-ed14f8e02566" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-80f3-98d2-f1e35c6b223e" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">6. IT Acts and Cyber Laws (Global Standards)</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8072-b6f0-de30b8a47e62"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8059-a939-cd9c945869de" class="">IT Acts and Cyber Laws (Global Standards)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8005-94fc-ee8195fbd8d0" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d6-8b69-c941e25af698" class="">With the rapid growth of the internet and digital technologies, <strong>cyber crimes</strong> such as hacking, data theft, identity fraud, and online fraud have increased. To regulate online activities and protect users, many countries have introduced <strong>IT Acts and Cyber Laws</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803b-9841-e41b610632f2" class="">These laws define <strong>legal rules and regulations for the use of computers, networks, and digital communication</strong>, and they help in preventing and punishing cyber crimes.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-801f-bff4-c12d891c3348"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8045-99bd-efedbc27e4c2" class="">2. Meaning of Cyber Law</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e6-ba58-fb0e94121527" class=""><strong>Cyber law</strong> refers to the <strong>legal framework that governs activities in cyberspace</strong>, including the use of computers, the internet, electronic communication, and digital transactions.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b0-821e-ddc01fb84593" class="">Cyber laws deal with issues such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c2-b4bf-f4bc41e1988a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Online fraud</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8098-ae2c-f26aaa094502" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hacking</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804b-9865-c795f7ef6d7f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data protection</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8011-b38a-c2816265d757" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Digital signatures</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a4-ac29-dcc8b9d825a9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Electronic contracts</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8002-b55e-c09c35414c9e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cyber terrorism</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-803d-8550-c5491cdfbb24"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-807c-a374-fc630f3fa89c" class="">3. IT Act in India</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-a5dc-d3592bdae751" class="">In India, cyber activities are regulated by the <strong>Information Technology Act, 2000</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80f0-b6de-ca467b89a4db" class="">Objectives of the IT Act</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801e-954a-ece7de3a6ded" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide <strong>legal recognition to electronic records and digital signatures</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b4-9bef-c4bceea0efe9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Facilitate <strong>electronic commerce (e-commerce)</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8071-926d-ccc2388a317e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Prevent <strong>cyber crimes and hacking</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802f-bd96-e3fdc51e25f6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Promote <strong>secure online transactions</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-bbc8-dc0e85cb6185" class="">Important Features</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-b22d-ccf8dfbafb5a" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Legal recognition of <strong>electronic documents</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80af-bb9e-e3e06477b56b" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Legal recognition of <strong>digital signatures</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80c4-918f-f17b4d440be6" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Establishment of <strong>Cyber Appellate Tribunal</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8033-a7f8-ca1b90631066" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Penalties and punishment for cyber crimes.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8005-b696-dca5f6395d89" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>Protection against <strong>data theft and hacking</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80b1-957c-da24f88ee954" class="">Amendment</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80eb-9eb0-ef8f213e77ca" class="">The act was later updated through the <strong>Information Technology (Amendment) Act, 2008</strong>, which introduced stricter rules for cyber crimes and data protection.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8003-ba0e-e78e3e26e923"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-807a-91c5-ef51151ee175" class="">4. Global Cyber Law Standards</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8056-9ee4-d76b31724856" class="">Many international organizations and countries have developed cyber laws to ensure <strong>global standards for digital security</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8084-a8d3-efa404aff89b" class="">1. Budapest Convention on Cybercrime</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-98c9-d26bc05537a8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">First international treaty addressing cybercrime.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8092-a442-fe089cc44891" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Helps countries cooperate in investigating cyber crimes.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e7-9cae-da1251a43621" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Covers offenses like hacking, fraud, and child exploitation online.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c2-93b3-f0b287859df1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8009-be29-fa422c4607b1" class="">2. General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808c-bd15-dc6c536fe68d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">A strict data protection law implemented in the European Union.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803e-bf15-dc65e7d995fc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects <strong>personal data and privacy of individuals</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80de-8ab7-f1249a20f4ae" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires organizations to handle user data securely.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8073-8818-e05c405d3ae3" class="">Key principles:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801c-b94e-e7deb79e0530" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Data protection</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804a-a7de-d8720ade22f6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">User consent</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8027-81b6-ece1900c1d30" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Transparency</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8040-81a6-db63e112f31a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Right to be forgotten</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-801c-9487-ca0bc5eac657"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8083-a558-c3ac03114906" class="">3. Computer Fraud and Abuse Act</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a5-942d-d8d160342cc6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Major cybercrime law in the United States.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-807e-a3ab-ce814c9b3256" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Deals with <strong>unauthorized access to computer systems</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8070-85bb-d1a2c90b2dfa" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides penalties for hacking and computer fraud.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-804a-a4df-f9454346de28"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8088-94bd-ec5ffeff20ec" class="">5. Common Cyber Crimes Covered Under Cyber Laws</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ae-aa5f-df81b82f62cd" class="">Cyber laws usually address crimes such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8004-9bfb-e914ee2a7f87" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Hacking</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b9-b8aa-ea929cc3891e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Identity theft</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8093-9169-c05fa622a890" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Online fraud</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80af-afa8-fe7d37ef3871" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Cyber terrorism</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8041-99b8-cba98bce9861" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Phishing attacks</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-b21c-c7eb554666ef" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Cyber stalking</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8081-a83b-cf4237cf16b1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Data breaches</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-802f-9cd9-dfa89d80f378"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80fa-818d-c1389f6ea161" class="">6. Importance of Cyber Laws</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-808d-90fb-cbb2ff9adefc" class="">Cyber laws are important because they:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8057-b561-dd2ccf955bf2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protect individuals and organizations from cyber crimes</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-807d-b251-d3c5b7209eb0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Ensure secure <strong>online communication and transactions</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801a-b518-dd75447aea86" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protect <strong>privacy and personal data</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803d-a3a2-fc20e953572c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encourage the growth of <strong>e-commerce and digital services</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8017-9b10-e2395aaa042c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide <strong>legal action against cyber criminals</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-aa23-d8d22cea8073"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80f5-829a-f69c877d7ed8" class="">7. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8040-befa-ca65fadcf2e7" class="">IT Acts and cyber laws provide a legal framework to regulate activities in cyberspace and protect digital systems from misuse. Laws such as the <strong>Information Technology Act in India</strong>, the <strong>GDPR in Europe</strong>, and international agreements like the <strong>Budapest Convention on Cybercrime</strong> help establish global standards for cybersecurity and digital rights.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8051-ba6e-cc6b4f02ee36" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8037-b3ab-dbc03223427d" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">7. Virtual Private Network (VPN)</mark></h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8046-bb56-d45f197b143f"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8026-935c-c4116bf4a1de" class="">Virtual Private Network (VPN)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-ba97-d6ddadb69641" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80cd-ab63-dbd950387684" class="">A <strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN)</strong> is a technology that allows users to <strong>securely connect to a private network over the internet</strong>. It creates a <strong>secure and encrypted connection (tunnel)</strong> between the user and the network, protecting data from unauthorized access.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d4-a0cc-fee0bbdf53d6" class="">VPNs are widely used by organizations and individuals to <strong>maintain privacy, secure communication, and access remote networks safely</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80d5-95cf-fa4c9e8c955b"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8094-92cc-ff1887e15a9b" class="">2. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80f7-8da7-ebca61e8fc31" class="">A <strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN)</strong> can be defined as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><blockquote id="323f2fef-a309-8001-ad3b-f54e7c2aa799" class="">A secure network connection that uses encryption and tunneling techniques to transmit data over a public network such as the internet.</blockquote></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80ca-a96f-c268dd63f637"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8098-ae46-f4dab2867c91" class="">3. Need for VPN</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-b879-e01a884c1a2a" class="">VPNs are used because the internet is a <strong>public network</strong> where data can be intercepted by attackers. VPN provides security by:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c1-9deb-cbe4dca37373" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encrypting data during transmission</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ca-b6da-ee904c7d788e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protecting sensitive information</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802e-89df-d165fa5c7b9f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Allowing secure remote access to private networks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8000-9686-e44d3d998a05" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protecting user privacy online</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e6-aa18-fb4925aa6d7f" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e7-ad59-e4c509566e2f" class="">Employees working from home can use a VPN to <strong>securely access their company’s internal network</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8015-8151-f7810f65b07c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80c7-8374-c0cde67d233f" class="">4. How VPN Works</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8067-ad3d-c7d13378479e" class="">A VPN works using <strong>tunneling and encryption</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8028-8748-e8b3d92d6230" class="">Steps:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-807c-926f-e0f4934cf6c2" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The user connects to a <strong>VPN server</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8064-a225-ec3be12f66e3" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The VPN software encrypts the user&#x27;s data.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8034-b3c7-c27d13c28f8b" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The encrypted data travels through a <strong>secure tunnel</strong> over the internet.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-809e-b824-e0532a5da896" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>The VPN server decrypts the data and sends it to the destination.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-8a61-c0e0b7e4673f" class="">This process protects data from hackers and unauthorized users.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8094-95bc-fe42d3182997"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8023-a2e3-de0a88365466" class="">5. Types of VPN</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8084-bd34-e8cf14dd961c" class="">1. Remote Access VPN</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-9dfc-eefe2ef02716" class="">Allows individual users to <strong>connect to a private network from a remote location</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807d-b16f-d8349d340ac6" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8001-8538-e960a82ccfbf" class="">An employee connecting to an office network from home.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80b2-9c04-c14bb62ae6db"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8045-ba43-d47ab0c4d21d" class="">2. Site-to-Site VPN</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ef-9af0-db94dfcf8940" class="">Connects <strong>two or more networks located at different geographic locations</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8025-aa1c-e0cc5c6a7490" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8006-9c6b-c42b18bcab01" class="">Connecting the networks of a company’s <strong>head office and branch offices</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c0-bfec-ffa186cbf756"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8034-b6d4-f3d9caaad55c" class="">6. Advantages of VPN</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8047-b096-c15cbe0badb6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provides <strong>secure communication over the internet</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e4-b604-f7d5d19e5025" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects <strong>sensitive data using encryption</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802d-8cbd-c83b10548e8f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Enables <strong>remote access to private networks</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d2-8c4a-dd60c2324778" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improves <strong>online privacy</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80fd-ba19-f9461358df52" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Reduces cost compared to private leased lines</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-804c-9809-efe65e88f58f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8078-889c-cc07374e98d0" class="">7. Disadvantages of VPN</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-805e-b20e-e7d68525a4bc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">May reduce internet speed due to encryption</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806c-97fa-cfdad8ef755e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Requires proper configuration and management</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801c-84a4-cced4f18b219" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Some VPN services may log user data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8004-b7f0-c5d7b393b133"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80e4-9f47-eaa6ed1b5265" class="">8. Applications of VPN</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8068-b908-fca7ca35473c" class="">VPN is used in:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803d-8425-dd170507bb9f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Corporate networks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8028-acfc-d08a6a24a090" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Remote working environments</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80af-a9cb-ce039f320b44" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure data communication</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8071-a33e-d42e6bc3cc66" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Online privacy protection</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8037-a4bb-ca666f051707" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Accessing restricted network resources</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-809d-b6cb-cbfafc49c72c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8047-81be-e16135021ae9" class="">9. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-ab05-c37a00206ce3" class="">A <strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN)</strong> is an important security technology that provides <strong>secure and private communication over public networks</strong>. By using encryption and tunneling methods, VPN ensures that sensitive information remains protected from cyber threats.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8004-b21f-e46631fb8b96" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-802f-9aec-e5cf1bcda199" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">8. Recent Attacks on Networks</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8093-becf-cb13ac34dc4d"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80d6-a6ca-ca3ff3d886c6" class="">Recent Attacks on Networks</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8033-9b02-d0ee2b742a14" class="">(Packet Analyzer, Buffer Overflow and Port Scanning)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-806d-9ef5-d3ffdeff985a" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8012-9772-c60f95943b8e" class="">With the increasing use of computer networks and the internet, <strong>network attacks</strong> have become very common. Attackers use different techniques to <strong>gain unauthorized access, steal sensitive information, or disrupt network services</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-800b-9b90-d31a66dcef85" class="">Some commonly observed attacks on networks include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8012-aa8b-f8d1ddfea1bf" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Packet Analyzer (Packet Sniffing)</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80e8-94cf-f68c9e3f233e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Buffer Overflow</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-808a-8b0a-d6e31312cdb9" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Port Scanning</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8045-a8fb-f7bb3da6a858"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8033-8572-db04fd35fafe" class="">2. Packet Analyzer (Packet Sniffing)</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-807c-a814-d6e5cb8a2c70" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80c6-8bc6-eb174040cc7e" class="">A <strong>packet analyzer</strong> (also called a <strong>packet sniffer</strong>) is a tool used to <strong>capture and analyze data packets traveling across a network</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8061-b9b5-e696a0a2d571" class="">While packet analyzers are used for <strong>network troubleshooting and monitoring</strong>, attackers can misuse them to <strong>intercept sensitive information</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8069-823d-d7b7f93e9dc4" class="">How the Attack Works</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8032-a15a-f09ccb074fc9" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The attacker installs a packet sniffing tool on a network.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ae-8033-f46d9b1dbdd6" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The tool captures packets transmitted over the network.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-807c-baf9-fca187081e8a" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The attacker analyzes the packets to extract sensitive data such as:<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c1-b2e4-cec72e4b85d7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Usernames</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804f-8de5-c487cda8cd15" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Passwords</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d5-bcac-c51bac985348" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Email contents</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8074-bd16-f0057c08db6e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Financial information</li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-807f-bee7-f48d6ca6c13b" class="">Example</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8085-bcda-ddc5adede466" class="">An attacker connected to the same Wi-Fi network captures data packets and retrieves login credentials from unencrypted traffic.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8044-b6ec-e78dbccc8eec" class="">Prevention Methods</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c9-b9ff-da8b41529f69" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use <strong>encryption protocols (HTTPS, SSL/TLS)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806d-a914-cf67d1807d91" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use <strong>secure network authentication</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804f-96fd-e67fb8bca3cf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Avoid using public Wi-Fi for sensitive transactions</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-809d-8e21-dc8c5a2d3210" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use <strong>VPN for secure communication</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8031-a103-ce1355d4a0b8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-803f-9d60-f4855631263e" class="">3. Buffer Overflow Attack</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80fa-8055-d68cbe9c98b1" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-804c-85bd-e9e02ef9d213" class="">A <strong>buffer overflow</strong> occurs when a program <strong>writes more data into a memory buffer than it can hold</strong>, causing the extra data to overwrite adjacent memory locations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e1-947a-c553a37a4ac3" class="">Attackers exploit this vulnerability to <strong>execute malicious code or gain control of the system</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8058-a9ae-ef0d086ebed3" class="">How the Attack Works</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-ac4f-dd4faf651b38" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>A program allocates memory space (buffer).</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ca-9e49-d1d62834ed62" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The attacker sends more data than the buffer size.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8013-8cc1-ee72c0165689" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The extra data overwrites important memory areas.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80c2-ad61-d4e7725f0c1e" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Malicious code is executed.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-804e-8283-dfab9e326cf5" class="">Effects</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8000-8077-fb7e3357345d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">System crash</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80df-91c8-cbbb08f95739" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Unauthorized access</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ee-818f-f1a746db23cf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Execution of malicious programs</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c9-92b5-d0b12c16e743" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Privilege escalation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8038-9fd0-f4898451fabd" class="">Prevention Methods</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8034-b66e-df2964f9a220" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Input validation</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8009-8cb0-ded845b71002" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Secure programming practices</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8052-b70f-ce9360e9df26" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use of modern programming languages with memory protection</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8064-9404-e8d2a66ee0bc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Regular software updates and patches</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8084-8e4b-d8fea30d3a0a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-96f4-c4e8ffb5f024" class="">4. Port Scanning</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80e7-8b31-cc04c363312e" class="">Definition</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80dd-ae44-d3723614b47e" class=""><strong>Port scanning</strong> is a technique used to <strong>identify open ports and services running on a network or computer system</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80fd-829c-ca054e8147f8" class="">Attackers use port scanning to find <strong>vulnerable services</strong> that can be exploited.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8034-a70f-e1190bf9abfa" class="">How the Attack Works</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8078-b494-d556ab7154ee" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>The attacker sends requests to different ports on a target system.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80d4-bbf0-eca14bb9b6e5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The system responds based on whether the port is <strong>open, closed, or filtered</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-800e-8219-dfdc0d41dd61" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>The attacker identifies open ports and attempts to exploit services running on them.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-803b-85d8-d9a7fb781991" class="">Example</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80be-95b2-c698e9fc7286" class="">An attacker scans ports like:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80cc-b6ed-f36158d3bdd1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Port 80</strong> (HTTP)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d0-add8-fa4afb1c046d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Port 443</strong> (HTTPS)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-807c-92b1-d93612bafc8f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Port 21</strong> (FTP)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808f-bd61-d404f8c03e67" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Port 22</strong> (SSH)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-806a-aa6e-c03a7c5996c1" class="">Prevention Methods</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804d-93ca-e5d12c9374b2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use <strong>firewalls</strong> to block unnecessary ports</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ff-80c3-dc907778ee1d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Disable unused services</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8019-b60b-d8e168d2d545" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Implement <strong>Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS)</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f0-a829-c4a0dbc0cfdd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Monitor network traffic</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8058-a4da-c64bf50352dd"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8009-8d4e-f4c165706ec9" class="">5. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8095-bda5-d9ec847f414d" class="">Network attacks such as <strong>packet sniffing, buffer overflow, and port scanning</strong> are common techniques used by attackers to compromise systems. Understanding these attacks and implementing proper security measures such as <strong>encryption, firewalls, secure coding practices, and intrusion detection systems</strong> can help protect networks from security threats.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803e-827b-da7c90cdd657" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-8085-a205-c275ce11d802" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">9. Blockchain Technology</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8038-9a6b-d674d2930820"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8004-99dc-d5512c50a6e3" class="">Blockchain Technology</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8014-8d12-c1e433ddd6f7" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807a-9f39-ce56aa12718b" class=""><strong>Blockchain technology</strong> is a distributed and decentralized digital ledger that records transactions across multiple computers. It ensures that the recorded data is <strong>secure, transparent, and cannot be easily modified</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e2-b277-de5e8af3061d" class="">Blockchain gained popularity through cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin, but today it is used in many areas like finance, supply chain, healthcare, and digital identity systems.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8023-b58f-fa5a24b92a96"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8069-98ca-f092c6347d69" class="">2. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e6-9312-f453e488b513" class=""><strong>Blockchain</strong> can be defined as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><blockquote id="323f2fef-a309-8051-af47-e4796db7068a" class="">A decentralized digital ledger that stores transaction records in blocks that are linked together using cryptographic techniques.</blockquote></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-808d-8ecb-f97770bba754" class="">Each block contains a group of transactions and is connected to the previous block, forming a <strong>chain of blocks</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80bd-962f-c32b7dc1626c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8091-ae7c-c792adfffa4a" class="">3. Key Features of Blockchain</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8099-8960-c0af85f4f748" class="">1. Decentralization</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b0-b8a6-dc8373c78908" class="">Data is not stored in a single central server. Instead, it is distributed across many computers (nodes).</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80ee-aa36-c4f98a15582d" class="">2. Transparency</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d4-8231-e3f6c41df3b5" class="">All transactions recorded on the blockchain are <strong>visible to all participants</strong> in the network.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-802a-9c58-e65b6dad3a1f" class="">3. Security</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80f9-9d5b-dd32235870fc" class="">Blockchain uses <strong>cryptography and hashing techniques</strong> to secure data and prevent tampering.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-801c-bfd5-c6fa6cddde52" class="">4. Immutability</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8075-9cd5-ec1f22fc2e82" class="">Once data is recorded in the blockchain, it <strong>cannot be easily changed or deleted</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8089-bf74-fb63362ad1d8" class="">5. Consensus Mechanism</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80cc-9831-da90cff2fa0b" class="">Participants in the network must agree before a new block is added to the chain.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8021-90ec-d0e18c199e03"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-808f-8027-c9955c99ab3f" class="">4. Structure of Blockchain</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80eb-9807-f88b61788e46" class="">A blockchain consists of <strong>blocks</strong> that store transaction information. Each block contains:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8020-b869-d2abf5619fca" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Transaction Data</strong> – Information about the transactions.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8000-be6e-f3fde1218123" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Hash Value</strong> – A unique identifier of the block.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-8f2e-d9c9d43faf34" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Previous Block Hash</strong> – Links the block to the previous block in the chain.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-800b-9237-ed7d1fc64cb8" class="">This structure makes blockchain <strong>secure and tamper-resistant</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-800e-a1b0-e992f791ca2d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8077-9b98-ee68e9a2e50c" class="">5. How Blockchain Works</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80fd-839b-e4cc4b1a9961" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>A user initiates a <strong>transaction</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-809a-b7fc-f3690d6e246a" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>The transaction is broadcast to the <strong>network of nodes</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80b9-9ab7-d1a1ca570a8a" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Nodes validate the transaction using a <strong>consensus mechanism</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8027-97e9-f455a9ec3392" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>A verified transaction is added to a <strong>new block</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ee-be0b-e7c726e1c606" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li>The block is added to the <strong>existing blockchain</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80e7-bc85-eeec278efedd" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li>The transaction becomes <strong>permanent and secure</strong>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8051-b9a7-de28481b0a57"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8002-bde6-d3e9ba396678" class="">6. Types of Blockchain</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-803a-84a9-d42c985eed86" class="">1. Public Blockchain</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e9-98ae-dce9a90eb722" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Open to everyone.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8080-92bc-ede5d86fff85" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Anyone can participate in the network.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8017-b189-cd46fb239ec4" class="">Example: Bitcoin</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8098-b63f-d9fa2af2c6d6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80d6-90d4-e85536f15759" class="">2. Private Blockchain</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8037-be03-ca97b27aaf8f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Controlled by a single organization.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8078-b85e-c03c6050827c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Only authorized participants can access the network.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-802e-8100-cd75a9935103"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-801e-a79c-f535ce2a5780" class="">3. Consortium Blockchain</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801c-bd05-cc258f304839" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Managed by a group of organizations.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8057-a3d5-da7a60d8d29c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Often used in business collaborations.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8030-b8ba-dc04fbe8537f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8069-8b82-e2ede5267916" class="">7. Applications of Blockchain</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809d-b54f-c228ce46b4f4" class="">Blockchain technology is used in many areas:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808b-a9e7-c31a34515f5e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Cryptocurrency transactions</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f5-8c2f-c6e4dba98cf8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Supply chain management</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808d-a8e4-d780834eaf5b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Healthcare records management</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8007-a093-c2ffb061141f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Digital identity verification</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a9-9022-dbb139cb015b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Smart contracts</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8062-ad79-c120f82385df" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Online voting systems</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805c-b8de-ea526d226d26" class="">Example: Platforms like Ethereum enable developers to build decentralized applications.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-803d-9d03-df6dff71953d"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8073-8c08-e945a9810827" class="">8. Advantages of Blockchain</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808c-9747-e1a5eb5c9881" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">High level of <strong>security</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b3-a2d3-e563211fefe4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Transparency</strong> in transactions</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8035-8c4e-e04c2735d4e9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Reduced fraud and data tampering</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e5-a479-e810f7298ecc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Decentralized control</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b7-89e7-db83cd5008c0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Lower transaction costs</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f3-9f9d-f9aaaab04b53"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8049-9d3e-e4574e9ee722" class="">9. Disadvantages of Blockchain</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803e-bfa2-e22505860d37" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">High <strong>energy consumption</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8058-be83-f406a721ae06" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Scalability issues</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8087-b953-c56337633973" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Difficult to modify recorded data</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808d-b276-f3b8e711e9d5" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Regulatory and legal challenges</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80bc-ad3c-e8c8d8db8737"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-804f-a24b-cf1634a4b845" class="">10. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8067-9ad3-ce0018f1dbe1" class="">Blockchain technology is transforming the way digital transactions are recorded and verified. Its decentralized, secure, and transparent nature makes it useful in various industries beyond cryptocurrencies. As technology evolves, blockchain is expected to play a major role in <strong>digital finance, secure data management, and decentralized applications</strong>.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80df-b367-c6279bcc1d84" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-80a6-9986-d9e0119d1a93" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">10. Preventing Privacy and Piracy on Internet</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8022-b84f-ef92e108b1d5"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8044-a100-d4e624d15dbf" class="">Preventing Privacy and Piracy on Internet</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-801d-a5b7-c14e5af34975" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8064-adb2-fbb57d35e8d5" class="">With the rapid growth of the internet, issues related to <strong>privacy violations and digital piracy</strong> have increased significantly. Privacy refers to the <strong>protection of personal information</strong>, while piracy refers to the <strong>illegal copying, distribution, or use of digital content</strong> such as software, music, movies, and books.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805e-bab3-e7f291faef41" class="">Preventing privacy breaches and piracy is essential to <strong>protect users’ data, intellectual property, and online security</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f5-8b65-d1fcf04e49ab"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8055-b150-d870aaa63d5f" class="">2. Privacy on the Internet</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-803b-b0bf-e070ab02fa2b" class="">Meaning</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8057-800c-f598cc3a6151" class=""><strong>Internet privacy</strong> refers to the protection of <strong>personal information and online activities from unauthorized access or misuse</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809b-9998-daf1f51ef3cd" class="">Examples of personal information:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8034-8073-cba3a8430f28" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Email addresses</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80fe-a016-e9fac227a25f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Passwords</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-800e-b263-f4122c4f6469" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Banking details</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808e-b049-e15ab5bc8768" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Personal photos and documents</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80e0-ab86-c126eee63654" class="">Privacy Threats</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80df-acd6-f9f5f67063fa" class="">Some common threats to online privacy include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8034-8689-c2c1125b8eb1" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Hacking</strong> – Unauthorized access to systems or accounts.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-805b-8a77-da8ed3bc3134" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Phishing</strong> – Fake websites or emails used to steal sensitive information.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8028-bb93-ebcba2739262" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Tracking and Data Collection</strong> – Websites collecting user data without consent.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80b2-b365-d99f2b08f571" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Malware and Spyware</strong> – Software that secretly monitors user activities.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80b6-8fd2-c702223ad7c8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-800c-9736-f8003a6bbf10" class="">3. Methods to Prevent Privacy Issues</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8098-88d2-d083b0b1d715" class="">1. Strong Authentication</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-804e-97bc-c7b7fef5ab90" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use <strong>strong and unique passwords</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b0-89d2-e73787eab17f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Enable <strong>two-factor authentication (2FA)</strong> for accounts.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80ac-a29d-eb9e3e50637b" class="">2. Data Encryption</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8096-a7da-c3e3851b8da0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Use encrypted connections such as <strong>HTTPS</strong> to protect data transmission.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-802f-b534-c81ed2fae678" class="">3. Use of Firewalls and Antivirus</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-800b-b0e5-d408254a623f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Firewalls and antivirus software help prevent unauthorized access and malware attacks.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-809c-afad-c842289651de" class="">4. Privacy Settings</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8035-a324-ee954f13d656" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Adjust privacy settings on social media platforms to control who can see personal information.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8024-878f-f76ec0036e12" class="">5. Use Secure Networks</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806a-8542-f6b27795183a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Avoid performing sensitive activities on <strong>public Wi-Fi networks</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80b2-b6bd-e7054b8ac9b9"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8033-917e-d7d3076245bb" class="">4. Internet Piracy</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-9b21-ffaa2160412d" class="">Meaning</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a3-877d-ef4df92754ef" class=""><strong>Internet piracy</strong> refers to the <strong>illegal copying, downloading, sharing, or distribution of copyrighted digital content without permission from the owner</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8078-836d-d66d7964095b" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d7-80f7-e8ddd30f9f6a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Downloading pirated movies or music</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8097-a986-e6ec5cbaf06c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Using cracked software</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-809e-b478-f712ec0e4850" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Sharing copyrighted books or videos illegally</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8064-8945-dd77165c20d1"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8059-a43a-f72fb8847efa" class="">5. Types of Internet Piracy</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8076-82b5-d9239ca17815" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Software Piracy</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b8-8c89-f04c5a5ec8b9" class="">Illegal copying or distribution of software programs.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8085-aff7-c588c160e92e" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Movie and Music Piracy</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d5-9b73-fa2a6f647fe1" class="">Unauthorized downloading or sharing of movies and songs.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8014-88e0-ee0dfbdf8c63" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>E-Book Piracy</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80af-8535-d128051f1143" class="">Illegal sharing of digital books.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-9dbc-eaeb7adbd9d1" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Game Piracy</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-8236-c99dfb183fe7" class="">Unauthorized copying or distribution of video games.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-807d-84ca-e24b3f924b4e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80cc-8b32-c4158134c84e" class="">6. Methods to Prevent Internet Piracy</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8096-b9bc-df7affb02616" class="">1. Copyright Laws</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8072-947d-d5ec6a67da38" class="">Governments enforce laws to protect intellectual property rights.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80bb-bbb8-e74058cfd94f" class="">Example:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8056-8d9f-f59a438f7d5d" class="">In India, piracy and cyber crimes are regulated under the Information Technology Act, 2000.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8043-8634-ca339903e4c0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-808a-a15a-e3d671c44f3c" class="">2. Digital Rights Management (DRM)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8084-95af-f597ce87342f" class="">DRM technology restricts <strong>unauthorized copying or distribution of digital content</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c0-a6cd-e93762bb0953"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-804f-ac32-d9557570c69b" class="">3. Licensing and Legal Software</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-9a08-d323d1ef5174" class="">Users should purchase <strong>licensed software and legal digital content</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c8-b046-dfde710c0877"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8018-9f00-e1320fa1c5ce" class="">4. Watermarking</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-802e-bcb3-ee616985aec5" class="">Digital watermarking helps identify the <strong>original owner of digital content</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8028-8089-e8f26e040654"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-800a-b382-d04d35f042bc" class="">5. Monitoring and Enforcement</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8055-b6fe-e533bf2e2dfb" class="">Organizations monitor the internet to detect and remove pirated content.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8013-ab2a-f59d2c0b22f3"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8087-9197-d412a483065a" class="">7. Importance of Preventing Privacy Violations and Piracy</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a3-bdb6-e5abe3d95a3e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects <strong>personal information and identity</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8078-a483-c65b68730b3a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Protects <strong>intellectual property rights</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806b-972a-f2879e53dc2e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Encourages <strong>legal use of digital content</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f5-96be-eb7844dbb9ee" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Promotes <strong>secure online environments</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f9-bd08-e5d537f75e54" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Reduces financial losses for creators and companies</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-803e-a46e-cd6b9628cb39"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-805d-8122-f43cbdec7b41" class="">8. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-800a-af7a-fceb3dde7cb7" class="">Preventing privacy violations and internet piracy is essential for maintaining a secure and ethical digital environment. By using security technologies, following cyber laws, and promoting awareness, individuals and organizations can protect personal data and intellectual property from misuse.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8070-a56d-eff27ab9b0c8" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-806b-9210-eff26eb330f8" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">11. Brief Overview of Cyber Forensics</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-801a-8832-eced429d49b1"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80a8-9fac-e5561c74a528" class="">Brief Overview of Cyber Forensics</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8086-936a-cd4e7222dd80" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8073-8722-e7140afe6c47" class=""><strong>Cyber forensics</strong>, also known as <strong>digital forensics</strong>, is the process of <strong>collecting, analyzing, and preserving digital evidence</strong> from computers, networks, and other electronic devices to investigate cyber crimes.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b3-9a47-fc6261ca1982" class="">It is widely used by <strong>law enforcement agencies, cybersecurity experts, and legal authorities</strong> to identify attackers and present digital evidence in court.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8015-a89d-fcba05412a2a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8080-8d84-f21c55055056" class="">2. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807b-9e72-f196aa5e0946" class="">Cyber forensics can be defined as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><blockquote id="323f2fef-a309-803c-a5e7-c67b6e2e4ee4" class="">The scientific process of identifying, collecting, examining, and presenting digital evidence from electronic devices to investigate cyber crimes.</blockquote></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8075-900b-e3c07874f26f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80bf-ab8d-fd2d231704a0" class="">3. Objectives of Cyber Forensics</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80af-a498-e88cd416aa06" class="">The main objectives of cyber forensics are:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e6-b208-e56333a7af77" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Identify cyber criminals</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80fc-b556-de87d20bf5b1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Recover deleted or hidden data</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8035-9164-d5bc718f9655" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Analyze digital evidence</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8013-b602-d74713303ffc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Understand how a cyber attack occurred</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8007-9062-eda047eb765c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Present evidence in legal proceedings</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80fc-ba48-f97d6e566fcf"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80a4-b608-ea2e8b4a6f3b" class="">4. Types of Cyber Forensics</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8021-9a5d-d9f242e997c0" class="">1. Computer Forensics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-805e-b180-e7a585e4671e" class="">Focuses on collecting and analyzing evidence from <strong>computers and storage devices</strong> such as hard drives and USB drives.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-801a-bee6-c40c98c500d6" class="">2. Network Forensics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80c3-96d1-ccf5b7dc8396" class="">Analyzes <strong>network traffic and logs</strong> to detect suspicious activities or attacks.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80e3-a1a5-c5fcbb50e917" class="">3. Mobile Device Forensics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8073-b3f1-c47e152292d1" class="">Involves extracting and analyzing data from <strong>smartphones and tablets</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-ae25-faba451ca3ba" class="">4. Database Forensics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8051-b88d-fb4af2a7bed7" class="">Examines <strong>database systems</strong> to identify unauthorized access or data manipulation.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-807b-9dcc-e37b7f92b12a" class="">5. Email Forensics</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8054-8bb3-f1fd7b1e3676" class="">Investigates <strong>email communications</strong> to detect fraud, phishing, or spam activities.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-bb93-c100cc57151f"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80e0-bc3b-fa44a8ab44cb" class="">5. Steps in Cyber Forensics Process</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803f-8f09-c19c2f2d85b0" class="">The cyber forensic investigation generally follows these steps:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ff-b06c-d312fc1b40d7" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Identification</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8090-8b11-fab6fd5a5274" class="">Detecting potential digital evidence.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8026-b924-c300aacafefc" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Preservation</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8048-86c2-faf841d49340" class="">Protecting data from modification or deletion.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8098-bb8b-f52824cad8ff" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Collection</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8092-b8b9-e919f10ebf7e" class="">Gathering digital evidence from devices.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-806b-a95d-e054074a2c6b" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Examination</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8084-8f16-ed1a42f206fc" class="">Analyzing the collected data.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ea-bd79-d6ca6adb155a" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Analysis</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-806e-a9fe-ebd9895a8b5e" class="">Interpreting evidence to understand the incident.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8036-ab3f-cf5541daeb42" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><strong>Presentation</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a3-8a6b-e6c70bc739e1" class="">Presenting findings in a legal or investigative report.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-800a-ae7e-eed73b42cc21"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80ad-8602-c1c5c5d0a5f9" class="">6. Tools Used in Cyber Forensics</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a2-8c8e-d7cadc03ea37" class="">Some common digital forensic tools include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808f-9822-fd5038e22eb4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">EnCase</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8005-9f02-fc0110c1cc46" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">FTK (Forensic Toolkit)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80ca-ac77-d7057c60be98" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Autopsy</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802e-ac74-eb1964101978" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Wireshark</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b6-9002-da1b2e544015" class="">These tools help investigators <strong>recover deleted files, analyze system logs, and examine digital evidence</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8077-974a-f591b01ed7ff"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80b4-ad43-d3e9f80e5a1b" class="">7. Importance of Cyber Forensics</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8082-b718-ca159379d79f" class="">Cyber forensics is important because it helps to:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8033-be3b-c3007b0b509e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Investigate <strong>cyber crimes</strong> such as hacking and fraud</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f3-86f1-ca0b859ecea7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Recover <strong>lost or deleted data</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b3-8ed0-df9ddffb66e9" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Identify <strong>attack methods and vulnerabilities</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f8-9460-c0937b15201b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide <strong>legal evidence in court</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8033-b602-db287389c9ed" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improve <strong>cybersecurity measures</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8024-a083-cf63e501dc9c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8022-9fb9-d1367c7a0bf1" class="">8. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ab-84e4-c3297f321e9d" class="">Cyber forensics plays a crucial role in combating cyber crimes by collecting and analyzing digital evidence. It helps investigators understand how cyber attacks occur and provides reliable evidence for legal actions, making it an essential field in modern cybersecurity.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8009-8db6-f38dc8015d80" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-802a-8531-e9bf3b0c4bcb" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">12. Digital Evidence</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-80dc-b43a-fda9879b27a2"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8049-bac4-c0d1b41a1ea6" class="">Digital Evidence</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8067-acde-d021db26aa59" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809b-8535-de9c1290aa62" class=""><strong>Digital evidence</strong> refers to any information stored or transmitted in <strong>digital form</strong> that can be used during an investigation or in a court of law. With the increasing use of computers, mobile devices, and the internet, digital evidence has become very important in <strong>cyber crime investigations and digital forensics</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8002-a618-f211ff7f6169" class="">Digital evidence helps investigators <strong>identify cyber criminals, understand how a crime was committed, and prove the crime in court</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8061-b776-f2833cf60940"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8088-869d-cc717086bcab" class="">2. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-809a-8ff9-dbd9e15454fe" class=""><strong>Digital evidence</strong> can be defined as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><blockquote id="323f2fef-a309-8053-9320-e667a0b93e4a" class="">Any information of probative value that is stored or transmitted in digital form and can be used as evidence in a legal investigation.</blockquote></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80a6-8e57-c1d165ab9521"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8012-9fdf-e040e51fcd54" class="">3. Sources of Digital Evidence</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803d-9b70-ef46aba70bfd" class="">Digital evidence can be collected from many electronic devices, such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8020-836d-eeaf97f900d7" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Computers and Laptops</strong> – files, documents, browsing history.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8040-bdcf-f0b9f0c8a6c4" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Mobile Phones and Tablets</strong> – messages, call logs, photos, and apps.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80db-b376-f1995aafb7c3" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Email Systems</strong> – email messages and attachments.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8035-a897-c215dccc00ce" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Network Devices</strong> – routers, servers, and firewalls.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-b446-d92cbaef6168" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Storage Devices</strong> – USB drives, memory cards, and hard disks.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8018-8a7b-c1f1f9372911" class="numbered-list" start="6"><li><strong>Cloud Services</strong> – online storage and accounts.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80b8-9124-fa3bac25bf87"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8056-ac44-dde7244582f7" class="">4. Characteristics of Digital Evidence</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80db-a1b0-f46b89d9628a" class="">Digital evidence has several unique characteristics:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80f7-948d-e1a2d02271bc" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Fragile</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-800f-a4e0-fb5077aec4a6" class="">It can be easily modified, damaged, or deleted.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-802d-adaf-c7d3dc267a74" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Easily Duplicated</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e9-9a83-dc3375758291" class="">Exact copies of digital data can be created without changing the original.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80d0-9032-e37316a26ced" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Large Volume</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807a-8946-f4342df5e6f4" class="">Digital devices can store large amounts of data.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ac-aa2a-d37604ead5f5" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Invisible Nature</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8028-bec6-dd65e9fdaa69" class="">Digital evidence cannot be seen directly without using special tools.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80d7-81ef-d7ced4d5378e" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Requires Special Tools for Analysis</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80fb-8bc3-c851d88d20bb" class="">Investigators use forensic software to analyze digital data.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80ce-afbf-fd29710563df"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80b1-bac4-e492de63d105" class="">5. Types of Digital Evidence</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80fa-bd03-dfd22c500fa7" class="">1. Volatile Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80bb-adbf-d96a0f307e7e" class="">Data that is <strong>temporary and lost when the system is turned off</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-808f-9c4c-d7a2d8aedc0a" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a0-ae6e-f54ccb0a3fbb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">RAM contents</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8035-a8fc-dda330bac54d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Active network connections</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d2-b69e-dd0c95cb9d5f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Running processes</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80c9-b816-eb15bc9a3c3e" class="">2. Non-Volatile Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803e-8cbc-e127cf899244" class="">Data that <strong>remains stored even when the device is powered off</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801e-a67a-d7802f0cd732" class="">Examples:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8044-8208-fad946ff1dac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hard disk files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80b2-b210-ef861cca595f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Emails</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80af-b0b5-d850dbb1a57c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Documents and images</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80bd-a105-c57da88d5bb8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8051-a69c-c9354ab2ea8b" class="">6. Collection of Digital Evidence</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803b-b4f1-ee6f2ec46961" class="">The collection of digital evidence must follow proper procedures to maintain its integrity.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80c7-a6fd-fd635f6094d3" class="">Steps include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8029-87cf-fc6bdce84c51" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Identification</strong> – Identify potential digital evidence.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80f7-b78f-dae48d603849" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Preservation</strong> – Protect evidence from modification.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80ea-873f-c1bec5851a2a" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Collection</strong> – Gather data from digital devices.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8080-a283-f1e39863b4a6" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Examination and Analysis</strong> – Analyze the collected data.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80c4-9c12-dea303e945d4" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Documentation</strong> – Record all findings for legal purposes.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8018-886b-c29d810490b2"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8015-b124-caddd1e22b12" class="">7. Importance of Digital Evidence</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8053-abf2-e669f0b512a5" class="">Digital evidence is important because it helps to:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-800a-93c0-f0089bd06e31" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Investigate <strong>cyber crimes and fraud</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a2-9e9c-d34063ca9930" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Identify <strong>attackers and malicious activities</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80f9-b27a-d44afdc601ea" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Recover <strong>deleted or hidden data</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801a-a15a-eb005cd9c97f" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide <strong>legal proof in court cases</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80e4-974b-f45a53af7505" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improve <strong>cybersecurity practices</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8008-9cb4-c887e5fbe8ec"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8090-8e32-cf6950cf4845" class="">8. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a9-a3ee-f3535cd6ab8c" class="">Digital evidence plays a crucial role in cyber forensic investigations. It helps investigators collect and analyze digital information to solve cyber crimes and present reliable evidence in court. Proper handling and analysis of digital evidence are essential to maintain its integrity and legal validity.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-8056-95d1-f81fd4c9c0e6" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">13. Investigating Windows and Unix Systems</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-8017-989c-d664485937d8"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8073-8681-e3c1701a1c0f" class="">Investigating Windows and Unix Systems</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8040-998f-f30e73a502ae" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8004-a90f-f3b7816424a5" class="">In <strong>digital forensics</strong>, investigating operating systems such as <strong>Windows and Unix/Linux</strong> is important to identify cyber crimes, unauthorized access, or system misuse. Investigators analyze system logs, files, and configurations to collect <strong>digital evidence</strong> and understand how an incident occurred.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801f-9395-db19218293e8" class="">Both operating systems store important forensic information that helps in <strong>tracking user activities and system events</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-8f2a-e906ee860175"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-805b-8400-d2a9283ca415" class="">2. Investigation of Windows Systems</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80fe-a514-e299d8c0a21c" class="">Windows systems store a large amount of information useful for forensic investigation.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-800e-9d54-c391c86ac5c3" class="">Important Areas Examined</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-805a-9ab7-d831c6d977ec" class="">1. Registry Analysis</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8024-afe0-c8c003b41ed2" class="">The <strong>Windows Registry</strong> stores configuration settings and user activity information.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b5-ab21-d866f7460dd5" class="">Examples of information found:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c9-b519-e0709f30184b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Installed programs</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80c3-b797-ffd6441c8794" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">User login details</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8035-9a80-d81be0e4455a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">System configuration</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8037-8393-ce5a2dfd1735"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8014-abb3-e25cc88027cc" class="">2. Event Logs</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e7-96ce-cabd02a4433c" class="">Windows maintains <strong>event logs</strong> that record system activities.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-b453-d1df2ffedd5a" class="">Types of logs:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8081-9af1-d09130637c32" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>System logs</strong> – hardware and system events</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a1-a5fd-d562d5f02327" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Application logs</strong> – application errors and events</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d0-afe2-cc2f7bccb89b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Security logs</strong> – login attempts and security events</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d4-8281-fdd2e5b06874" class="">These logs help investigators detect <strong>unauthorized access or suspicious activities</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80ce-b6f2-fcae3a8c1c72"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8059-ba3f-fcd6557e74f4" class="">3. File System Analysis</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a9-8e52-e99a2d3f088b" class="">Windows commonly uses the <strong>NTFS file system</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80e8-981b-e95ca5d2432b" class="">Investigators examine:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-ac25-de85dc139540" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">File creation and modification dates</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802f-81a4-e6302d3dbe1d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Deleted files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8078-8608-ce6408faa19b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Hidden files</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8020-b8d3-ee3259361153"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8034-91f3-fa7a7aa771ba" class="">4. Browser History</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8029-a875-fa276cf9f7a0" class="">Internet browsers store:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8054-ad0f-f7236d4a7a2b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Visited websites</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8026-83b8-f45dc5819b98" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Download history</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8099-8799-d1f96485ae5d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Cookies and cache</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-86aa-e9a3cf4dd28a" class="">This helps identify <strong>online activities of a user</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-801b-93c1-c478f089e068"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8067-bb55-fbdfc87f8836" class="">5. Temporary Files</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-804d-a05b-fc77efe5d643" class="">Temporary files may contain traces of user activities such as opened documents or internet usage.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-808b-9fb9-e612f0da1333"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8028-b043-c9a22f16e630" class="">3. Investigation of Unix/Linux Systems</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-804d-9447-f42289fb0454" class="">Unix and Linux systems also store important forensic information that can help in investigations.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-807f-a70c-d89bcaa7233b" class="">Important Areas Examined</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80f1-a469-c94ba7e16620" class="">1. Log Files</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80c6-851f-ff0c2ebe5347" class="">Unix/Linux systems store logs in the <strong>/var/log directory</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8029-9df5-f6251eae8b20" class="">Common log files include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80d2-bce0-ce2a4a7a436a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>auth.log</strong> – authentication attempts</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8021-ba67-e52cffb6d60d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>syslog</strong> – system messages</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801d-b478-e92649706222" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>boot.log</strong> – system boot events</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8091-b2db-ea0bfc2552f0" class="">These logs help identify login attempts and system activities.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8059-a682-cdb725d94991"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80ce-92e0-c21da3d110b6" class="">2. User Account Information</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-80e4-d7f4adc7ee51" class="">Information about system users is stored in files such as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-800b-924c-d54bcbb512c6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>/etc/passwd</strong> – user account details</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-808e-b5f8-dd1e8424a791" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>/etc/shadow</strong> – encrypted passwords</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-803a-9976-e690adf26a38" class="">Investigators check these files to detect unauthorized accounts.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80fc-9649-f8b3741d615e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80ba-85a7-f29c2842053f" class="">3. File System Analysis</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8010-908f-f604280fdcdc" class="">Unix/Linux systems use file systems such as <strong>EXT3 and EXT4</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ec-a067-db15cbf14123" class="">Investigators analyze:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-806c-895b-d8af53e614cc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">File permissions</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803c-9cf8-f3ddf3938c88" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">File ownership</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-809f-826a-fc98ecc3acbe" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">File modification times</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80e0-8e66-e180869f1141"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80c6-8d48-f79da7ac5c88" class="">4. Running Processes</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-807c-b3e8-d0738238efba" class="">Commands such as <code>ps</code> and <code>top</code> can show <strong>active processes</strong>, which may reveal malicious programs.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8006-b481-eb4da2db6cb6"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-802e-8e76-d02de20b152f" class="">5. Network Activity</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-801d-9942-d1c379b209cd" class="">Investigators examine network connections using tools like <code>netstat</code> to detect suspicious communication.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-808d-938f-ef84ddb1087a"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8097-b8df-c2576bdcba1d" class="">4. Comparison of Windows and Unix Investigation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><table id="323f2fef-a309-8081-9b8e-e5b485373376" class="simple-table"><thead class="simple-table-header"><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8042-9d74-e759dd88716d"><th id="}ks&gt;" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Feature</th><th id="BOq~" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Windows Systems</th><th id="wT&lt;;" class="simple-table-header-color simple-table-header">Unix/Linux Systems</th></tr></div></thead><tbody><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-805c-9354-d9345b173ea9"><td id="}ks&gt;" class="">Configuration Storage</td><td id="BOq~" class="">Windows Registry</td><td id="wT&lt;;" class="">Configuration files</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8037-87b9-c76f388dfb40"><td id="}ks&gt;" class="">Log Storage</td><td id="BOq~" class="">Event Logs</td><td id="wT&lt;;" class="">/var/log directory</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-8014-ad36-c947844dcb5c"><td id="}ks&gt;" class="">File System</td><td id="BOq~" class="">NTFS</td><td id="wT&lt;;" class="">EXT3 / EXT4</td></tr></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><tr id="323f2fef-a309-80d0-9132-ecd272192fb6"><td id="}ks&gt;" class="">User Information</td><td id="BOq~" class="">User accounts database</td><td id="wT&lt;;" class="">/etc/passwd and /etc/shadow</td></tr></div></tbody></table></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80c9-89fd-d536fb2956f4"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-802d-8c42-f944dbf624a9" class="">5. Importance of System Investigation</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80bf-926a-e6d21f0c6f0c" class="">Investigating operating systems helps to:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80fd-a2e7-d90ee446a4ef" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Identify cyber attacks</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8089-9883-eacb74654df1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Detect unauthorized access</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a5-b2b2-f50b0b8d0aca" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Recover digital evidence</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8067-89e0-c30499065f28" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Track user activities</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8016-93a0-cc3260011cf1" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improve system security</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8035-9146-ec68725d3bc0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8028-bef2-c80ef6c1139e" class="">6. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80d3-9208-deb2ebb8514e" class="">Investigating <strong>Windows and Unix/Linux systems</strong> is an important part of digital forensics. By analyzing system logs, file systems, user accounts, and network activities, investigators can collect valuable digital evidence and understand how cyber crimes occur.</p></div></div></figure></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8085-aabe-ff8dcf49ac16" class="">
</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="323f2fef-a309-80a2-95e5-dd22284a5fb7" class=""><mark class="highlight-blue">14. Email Tracing</mark></h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="ltr"><figure class="block-color-default_background callout" style="white-space:pre-wrap;display:flex" id="323f2fef-a309-803a-841a-edb047f640cf"><div style="font-size:1.5em"><span class="icon">💡</span></div><div style="width:100%"><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-808c-8feb-faf6daeeceb3" class="">Email Tracing</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8058-b02b-f076c59a8e84" class="">1. Introduction</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8046-aff6-cd861c20a8bb" class=""><strong>Email tracing</strong> is the process of identifying the <strong>origin, route, and sender of an email message</strong>. It is commonly used in <strong>cyber forensic investigations</strong> to detect cyber crimes such as <strong>spam, phishing, fraud, and email harassment</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8060-82e8-c7c4896c428b" class="">By analyzing email headers and server information, investigators can determine <strong>where the email was sent from and how it traveled through different mail servers</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80d1-a1f1-dc73b6158688"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80e5-b0f9-d4be1b5e79d7" class="">2. Definition</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8049-9d68-cc69d4092f82" class=""><strong>Email tracing</strong> can be defined as:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><blockquote id="323f2fef-a309-804c-9e53-e07417aaf488" class="">The process of tracking the source and path of an email by analyzing its header information and server records.</blockquote></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-801b-b38c-fe7b184bde53"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-803f-9f5e-dba981a1f7b6" class="">3. Components of an Email</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80b7-95e0-c84551c740cc" class="">An email message generally consists of two main parts:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-803a-adda-e583bffd3764" class="">1. Email Header</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-802d-bd8d-c3e21083c7c8" class="">The <strong>header</strong> contains technical details about the email transmission.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8006-a5cb-cd095e39a8f3" class="">Important fields include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-807d-9f3e-eedeade25bda" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>From</strong> – Sender’s email address</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a5-82b8-f393ba8a3330" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>To</strong> – Receiver’s email address</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8032-8936-e0f32a20b71d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Date</strong> – Time the email was sent</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-801c-9bd7-e2ed1d9ac013" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Subject</strong> – Topic of the message</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80aa-a133-cf54fe5fee67" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Received</strong> – Mail servers through which the message passed</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a3-94d8-ce0e2743cfdb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>IP Address</strong> – Sender’s originating system</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80d5-b53a-d2f1fbc918a8"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-808d-9871-d253974d4ab2" class="">2. Email Body</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80bb-82cc-cedfef8c188b" class="">The <strong>body</strong> contains the actual message written by the sender.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f4-b4d8-db5f45f9cb9e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80cf-b9e6-caf7f9bf2925" class="">4. Steps in Email Tracing</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8093-ae4e-c1291061f8ed" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Obtain the Full Email Header</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80ec-af28-eaebd04b3054" class="">View the complete header information of the email.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-804a-ba3a-eccff38a8bae" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Analyze the Received Fields</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8044-ae1e-d614853958b0" class="">Check the list of mail servers through which the email passed.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-80f2-8652-f0fd5ec0d1ef" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Identify the Originating IP Address</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80cd-b6bb-dc5692ad8bda" class="">Locate the first server or IP address that handled the email.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-8088-a02c-df6e406c487c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Trace the IP Address</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8070-95fa-eaef53ecaf0a" class="">Use IP lookup tools to determine the <strong>location or internet service provider (ISP)</strong> of the sender.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="323f2fef-a309-807a-9a8f-cb96a7433836" class="numbered-list" start="5"><li><strong>Verify Email Authenticity</strong><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8060-b9ac-df379fcca3be" class="">Confirm whether the email is genuine or spoofed.</p></div></li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-804a-aef9-d3cd98b72941"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8076-befd-e8dbcc72fb2f" class="">5. Techniques Used in Email Tracing</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-8093-af76-f9a2718a75ec" class="">1. Email Header Analysis</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8030-9ebb-f7fe1323105f" class="">Examining header information to track the email route.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-809d-a983-dce212cab589" class="">2. IP Address Tracking</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8094-a453-d2b1a37c17cf" class="">Finding the geographical location of the sender’s IP address.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-80b6-8237-c6577253aca9" class="">3. Log Analysis</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8008-8b8f-e517546391c1" class="">Investigating email server logs for additional evidence.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="323f2fef-a309-800b-b61d-ef97aa191edc" class="">4. Reverse DNS Lookup</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80c7-b327-da95f9b934ec" class="">Identifying the domain name associated with an IP address.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8033-a784-feb3000e8b4e"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80a7-8c5a-f76aa9cf75bd" class="">6. Tools Used for Email Tracing</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80bd-bf5a-ddec596f9439" class="">Common tools used by investigators include:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8098-8671-fce7d5a2f0ce" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Wireshark</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8030-935a-c02cbcb74843" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">MXToolbox</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8079-aa27-e8db0cb98288" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">IP Tracker</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8037-b1da-f88c255358a2" class="">These tools help analyze email headers and identify the origin of suspicious emails.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-8012-8823-c41bc9acacfc"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-80c3-82d9-fb97e302de39" class="">7. Importance of Email Tracing</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-8043-912e-f16dc04db2ce" class="">Email tracing is important because it helps to:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8095-aaaa-e5d207126756" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Identify <strong>spam and phishing emails</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-809b-b74f-d881ff6a6e6c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Detect <strong>email spoofing</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-809c-9110-da3daacf3754" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Track <strong>cyber criminals</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-803a-a17b-f9ba4b8224b0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Provide <strong>digital evidence in cyber crime investigations</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802a-88f6-e7e51da98a89" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Improve <strong>email security</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80f1-98d7-f296c080e439"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8045-a3ac-fa5154aacdfc" class="">8. Limitations of Email Tracing</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-802a-adc3-cedcd5d90f8e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Attackers may <strong>hide their real IP address</strong> using VPNs or proxy servers.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-8088-a867-c4212e71982e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Emails may be <strong>spoofed</strong>, making tracing more difficult.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="323f2fef-a309-80a0-ad61-cf7fe4c3dbd6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Some email services hide the original sender’s IP.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="323f2fef-a309-80b4-8a57-cfaaf0fe0f76"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="323f2fef-a309-8019-bc1f-c49f3cd0f318" class="">9. Conclusion</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="323f2fef-a309-80de-b98b-ed58e530939d" class="">Email tracing is an important technique in cyber forensics used to identify the source and path of an email message. By analyzing email headers, IP addresses, and server logs, investigators can detect malicious emails and track cyber criminals involved in email-based attacks.</p></div></div></figure></div></div></article></body></html>